h pcornet i2b2 k01 hl141690 cwm r03 hl172123 cwm blackman karnes e13871 driest vumc hemingway asselbergs danesh bowton 234cm3 sudlow peakman 224ed4 gottesman kuivaniemi tromp rutter gaziano concato brophy bastarache phenome bastarache labwas e1009077 driest abul husn glessner rumshisky uzuner i2b2 hripcsak desiderata peissig kho e147 e154 pendergrass e80 savova h1885 pendergrass gentry dudek phenome phewas pleiotropy e1003087 hripcsak peissig hripcsak ohdsi pcornet klann abend raghavan mandl i2b2 speltz phekb transportability razzaghi aglio e27876 khare kappelman samson e10243 slaby neurodev siddiqi bakris e53 e90 babcock chucri roumie buse pcornet mosley driest kcnip4 almoguera mcginnis ntrk2 kho torvik foxe1 phenome jilg clostridioides bowton weeke summits dahlin cmtr1 karnes rollin ewald ladanie e23 e32 ricket hammill e006292 mangione dehoff effron bodenreider yearb bodenreider cornet vreeman loinc yearb kilbourne elkin rosenbloom simonaitis dehoff e14777 richesson summits phansalkar kapusnik uner higby bodenreider bahr bodenreider ndf semantics harpaz haerian semantics banda vanguri tatonetti goss plasek shehab weidle manohar arsoniadis saitwal bernstam chute terminological breydo turchin turchin labreche pendergrass einbinder stenner doan waitman medex soysal halgrim chute medxn mcneer medextractr customizable goryachev sordo uzuner solti cadag sezgin krivchenia rust ooab084 savova masanz ogren elhadad cimino hripcsak jao demner fushman e20675 meystre thibault textractor kormilitzin vaci nevado holgado med7 mcneer envirnoment mcneer postmarketing mcneer plasek dimaggio haerian varn vaidya ena carrell cronkite oetjens shenkman oneflorida madigan schuemie s79 s84 phecap e27 horsky drucker ramelson kukhareva staes limdi broun herbig negroes achebe shaper bagheri driest borinstein mosley borinstein agamasu schildcrout','Abstract Electronic health records (EHRs) contain a vast array of phenotypic data on large numbers of individuals, often collected over decades. Due to the wealth of information, EHR data have emerged as a powerful resource to make first discoveries and identify disparities in our healthcare system. While the number of EHR‐based studies has exploded in recent years, most of these studies are directed at associations with disease rather than pharmacotherapeutic outcomes, such as drug response or adverse drug reactions. This is largely due to challenges specific to deriving drug‐related phenotypes from the EHR. There is great potential for EHR‐based discovery in clinical pharmacology research, and there is a critical need to address specific challenges related to accurate and reproducible derivation of drug‐related phenotypes from the EHR. This review provides a detailed evaluation of challenges and considerations for deriving drug‐related data from EHRs. We provide an examination of EHR‐based computable phenotypes and discuss cutting‐edge approaches to map medication information for clinical pharmacology research, including medication‐based computable phenotypes and natural language processing. We also discuss additional considerations such as data structure, heterogeneity and missing data, rare phenotypes, and diversity within the EHR. By further understanding the complexities associated with conducting clinical pharmacology research using EHR‐based data, investigators will be better equipped to design thoughtful studies with more reproducible results. Progress in utilizing EHRs for clinical pharmacology research should lead to significant advances in our ability to understand differential drug response and predict adverse drug reactions. INTRODUCTION Recently, utilization of electronic heath records (EHRs) as a resource for biomedical discovery has greatly accelerated. EHRs can capture a wide variety of phenotypic data on large numbers of individuals collected systematically over decades. EHR data are available at low cost and can be used to study virtually any disease, providing many advantages over traditional studies, which typically focus on a single condition in a narrowly defined population and require large financial, time, and infrastructure investments. 1 , 2 EHR data have been increasingly coupled to biorepositories, creating opportunities to leverage “omic” data in combination with EHR‐derived phenotypes and to translate discoveries back to the clinic. Noteworthy examples include the eMERGE Network, UK Biobank, the Million Veteran Program (MVP), and the All of Us Research Program. 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 Innovative tools have been developed using such resources, such as the phenome‐wide association study (PheWAS) and laboratory‐wide association study (LabWAS). 7 , 8 These approaches exploit the breadth and depth of EHR phenotype data, creating new opportunities to observe pleiotropy, better understand confounding, and systematically investigate associations in a disease‐agnostic manner. EHR data have emerged as a powerful resource to make first discoveries and, more recently, as a resource to identify disparities in our healthcare system. 9 , 10 While the number of EHR‐based studies has exploded in recent years, most of these studies are directed at associations with disease rather than pharmacotherapeutic outcomes, such as drug response or adverse drug reactions. This is largely due to challenges specific to deriving drug‐related outcomes from the EHR. Originally designed for clinical care, billing, and auditing, EHR‐based data come with their own challenges in utilization, including missingness, loss to follow‐up, potential misclassification, inadequate records for lifestyle choices such as diet and exercise, and a lack of standardization across facilities for both documentation and clinical practice patterns. Patients in the United States often receive care from multiple health systems; therefore, EHR data are inherently siloed and data across healthcare systems are not easily shared. Improved EHR standardization across multiple countries and software tools through Systematized Nomenclature of Medicine (SNOMED) terms, Logical Observation Identifier Names and Codes (LOINC), and International Classification of Diseases, Ninth Revision (ICD‐9) or Tenth Revision (ICD‐10) codes has enabled much progress. 1 Often, disease traits can be matched between health systems using ICD codes, but deriving drug‐related outcomes from the EHR is substantially more difficult. Misclassification of allergies, inadequate medication reconciliation records, and a lack of data on adherence to prescribed drugs contribute to this difficulty. Drug‐related phenotypes also rely heavily on temporal relationships, which introduces substantial bioinformatic challenges. 11 Given the prior success of disease‐focused research in the EHR, and the potential for EHR‐based discovery in clinical pharmacology research, there is a critical need to address specific challenges related to accurate and reproducible derivation of drug‐related phenotypes from the EHR. Progress in this area is likely to lead to significant advances in our ability to understand differential drug response and predict adverse drug reactions. When genetic data are available, this progress also promises to bolster efficient and cost effective pharmacogenomic discovery and translation. To this end, this review provides a detailed evaluation of challenges and considerations for deriving drug‐related data from EHRs. We provide a discussion of Computable Phenotypes , Medication Information Mapping , Natural Language Processing (NLP), and Additional Considerations for Electronic Health Record‐Based Clinical Pharmacology Research . We have also provided a table of abbreviations used throughout this review (Table 1 ). TABLE 1. Abbreviations. ADE Adverse drug event ADHD Attention‐deficit/hyperactivity disorder AE Adverse event AERS Adverse Event Reporting System ATC Anatomical Therapeutic Chemical classification system aTRH Apparent treatment‐resistant hypertension CDM Common data model CP Computable phenotype DDI Drug–drug interaction DSI Drug supplement interactions DrOn Drug Ontology EHRs Electronic health records eMERGE Electronic Medical Records and Genomics FDA Food and Drug Administration GWAS Genome‐wide association studies HIV Human immunodeficiency virus i2b2 Informatics for Integrating Biology & the Bedside ICD International Classification of Diseases MED‐RT Medication Reference Terminology MVP Million Veteran Program NDC National Drug Code NDF‐RT National Drug File‐Reference Terminology NLP Natural language processing NPV Negative predictive value OMOP Observational medical outcomes partnership ONC Office of the National Coordinator for health information technology PCORnet Patient‐centered clinical research network PD Pharmacodynamics PheKB Phenotype KnowledgeBase PK Pharmacokinetics PPV Positive predictive value RxCUI RxNorm Concept Unique Identifier SNOMED Systematized Nomenclature of Medicine T2DM Type 2 diabetes mellitus UMLS Unified Medical Language System Open in a new tab COMPUTABLE PHENOTYPES What are computable phenotypes Modern healthcare systems generate vast quantities of patient data in the EHR daily, offering an evergrowing source of longitudinal health information. Thus, EHRs have been increasingly leveraged in clinical pharmacology research to enhance patient care and healthcare outcomes. However, often a patients'' EHR is an indirect reflection of their characteristics based on a complex and heterogenous recording process. This makes identifying patients with particular phenotypic information accurately and reliably from EHR data challenging and time‐consuming. 12 The ability to identify patient groups accurately and precisely can be improved by using an EHR‐based computable phenotype (CP). CPs identify patient populations or cohorts with a particular disease, clinical characteristic, or condition. CPs often utilize information from many EHR sources, including billing codes (i.e., ICD‐9, ICD‐10), Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) codes, vital signs, medication data, clinical documentation, and laboratory data. 13 Many CPs are a set of rules or a rule‐based algorithm that defines the inclusion and exclusion criteria for the cohort and any required temporal relationships. The resulting cohort can be utilized in comparative effectiveness research, genome‐wide association studies (GWAS), observational studies, or clinical trials. Regardless of the application, the CP algorithm must be valid, explicit, and reproducible. 14 The development of a CP algorithm is often and ideally a collaborative, iterative process from a multidisciplinary team of clinicians, data scientists, informaticists, and other study area experts (e.g., epidemiologists and geneticists). Initially, the team sets the criteria for the phenotype based on data available from the EHR. Commonly CPs take the form of a rule‐based algorithm and sequential flowchart. At each step of the flowchart, a patient''s EHR data are assessed for the presence or absence of the criteria before proceeding to the next step. Through this process, patients can be classified as meeting the CP criteria, meeting a comparison group criteria, or being excluded. 15 CP algorithms may use structured, unstructured, or both forms of EHR data. Structured elements include billing codes, laboratory values, vital signs, and medications, while unstructured data include clinical narratives such as progress notes, physician notes, discharge summaries, diagnostic reports, and other types of data that are not readily extractable (e.g., radiological images or attached documents). 13 Some phenotype algorithms incorporate NLP to improve the algorithm performance by transforming unstructured clinical narratives into data that can be easily extracted and analyzed. 16 Computable phenotype validation and performance Following the development process, CP algorithms must be validated to ensure they are accurately identifying patients with the intended phenotype. Manual chart review is the accepted gold standard method for CP algorithm validation. 14 During the validation process, the developed algorithm is applied to EHR data and at least two trained reviewers conduct blinded structured chart review in an iterative process, with fewer charts in the early rounds and more in the subsequent rounds and reviewers overlapping on at least 10% of charts. If the patient status is too difficult to be determined by the two reviewers or there is disagreement, the chart would be reviewed by a third reviewer. 14 After chart reviews are completed, performance characteristics, including positive predictive value (PPV), negative predictive value (NPV), sensitivity, and specificity, are calculated. Often, PPV is the most important characteristic in determining the algorithm''s accuracy as it considers the phenotype prevalence. An algorithm with a PPV of 67.7%–100% is considered acceptable. If the CP algorithm uses NLP, the most common performance metrics are precision, recall, and the F‐measure. 17 Once an algorithm is validated, the pseudocode and sequential flowchart can serve as a detailed map to implement or validate phenotype algorithms at other sites. 14 Computable phenotype algorithm portability and implementation The ultimate goal in developing a CP algorithm is for it to be portable and, thus, capable of being implemented across different sites. However, this is challenging for many reasons, including difficulties in integrating EHRs across healthcare systems due to the absence of EHR standardization. 18 One way to facilitate portable implementation of the phenotype algorithm in different sites is via common data models (CDMs). CDMs are blueprints for transforming various healthcare data sources into standardized table and field layouts for which standard queries may be created. 18 Consequently, researchers are able to write queries that can be shared and implemented at different sites. To enable efficient cross‐site queries, some research networks, including Observational Health Data Sciences and Informatics (OHDSI) and the Observational Medical Outcomes Partnership (OMOP) CDM, 19 the National Patient‐Centered Clinical Research Network (PCORnet), 20 and Informatics for Integrating Biology & the Bedside''s (i2b2''s) Shared Health Research Informatics NEtwork (SHRINE), 21 have adopted CDMs. Given the complex, time‐consuming, and labor‐intensive process of developing and validating a phenotype algorithm, the Electronic Medical Records and Genomics (eMERGE) network developed the Phenotype KnowledgeBase (PheKB) website to facilitate sharing of phenotype algorithms across sites. 22 Utilizing medication information in computable phenotypes Including medication information in the development of CPs has been shown to improve the accuracy and performance of the algorithm. Phillips et al. 23 showed that developing a CP for pediatric lymphoma and leukemia patients based solely on diagnosis codes resulted in an overestimation of cancer patients. However, mapping chemotherapies from RxNorm and including their administration for a minimum of two encounters in the algorithm along with two leukemia diagnoses improved the sensitivity, specificity, and PPV of the algorithm (Table 2 ). 23 PEDSnet, a multi‐institutional pediatric learning health system, sought to develop and evaluate the accuracy of a CP for pediatric Crohn''s disease. Out of the six algorithms tested, adding ≥1 Crohn''s disease medication plus ≥3 Crohn''s disease diagnoses was shown to have an optimal balance between precision and sensitivity of the algorithm (Table 2 ). 24 In another CP that included medication data, researchers at Children''s Hospital of Philadelphia (CHOP) used pediatric data from the Center for Applied Genomics (CAG) to develop an algorithm to distinguish attention‐deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) cases alone from those with comorbid conditions. Their work showed that incorporating ADHD‐specific medications in the algorithm raised the number of detected ADHD cases by 21% and achieved 95% PPV (Table 2 ). 25 Finally, a CP algorithm for Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) that included diagnosis codes, HIV‐specific medications, and laboratory tests had a higher sensitivity (98.9%) and specificity (97.6%) compared with prior HIV CPs (Table 2 ). 26 TABLE 2. Summary of select validated EHR‐based computable phenotypes. Phenotype Type of data and methods included Validation methods and metrics Diagnosis codes Procedure codes Vitals Medication information NLP Validation method Sen. Spe. PPV Angiotensin‐converting enzyme inhibitor‐induced cough 31 X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 100% Apparent treatment‐resistant hypertension (aTRH) 28 X X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 99.1% Attention‐deficit hyperactivity disorder 25 X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 95% Asthma response to inhaled steroids 38 X X Manual chart review N/A N/A N/A Atrioventricular conduction 30 X X X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 97% Clopidogrel response 36 X X X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A N/A Clostridium difficile infection 35 X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 90% Crohn''s disease in pediatric 24 X X Manual chart review 91% 94% 95% Drug‐resistant epilepsy 32 X X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 92% Heparin‐induced thrombocytopenia 39 X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A N/A Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) 26 X X X Manual chart review 99% 98% 81% Hypothyroidism 34 X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 92% Leukemia and lymphoma in pediatric 23 X X X Manual chart review 100% 99% 100% Statin and major cardiovascular vascular events 37 X X X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 90%–97% Type 2 diabetes 29 X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 98% Type 2 diabetes 33 X X X Manual chart review N/A N/A 96% Open in a new tab Note : Phenotypes selected based on the inclusion of medication information, multiple types of data and methods utilized, and one or more of the following criteria: a drug related phenotype, a disease related phenotype with improved performance with the addition of medication information, a CP used in genetic or pharmacogenomic‐based research. Sensitivity, Specificity, and PPV were included if values were reported in the study publication. Abbreviations: EHR, electronic health record; N/A, not available from the study; NLP, natural language processing; PPV, positive predictive value; Sen, sensitivity; Spe., specificity. Medication information may also be used in CP algorithms for phenotypes that are defined by medication exposure(s). An example is apparent treatment‐resistant hypertension (aTRH), which is defined by the number of antihypertensive medication classes a patient is taking and their on‐treatment blood pressure values. 27 By mapping medication information to standard drug terminologies and incorporating temporal relationships between medication exposures and blood pressure values, complex phenotypes such as aTRH can still be identified using EHR data with a high PPV (Table 2 ). 28 Likewise, medication data can help distinguish between two similar diseases such as type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) and type 1 diabetes mellitus (T1DM). Adding oral hypoglycemic agents in addition to diagnoses and laboratory values (e.g., glycosylated hemoglobin [HbA1c]) led to accurate and efficient identification of T2DM patients from four different PCORnet sites. 29 Applications of computable phenotypes By utilizing EHR data coupled with a DNA repository, CP algorithms can be used to identify patients with a phenotype of interest for pharmacogenomic and disease genetics studies. This often allows large sample sizes, a wide range of genetic ancestries, and minimizes the risk of selection bias. 30 In a GWAS of angiotensin‐converting enzyme inhibitor (ACEi)‐induced cough, a CP algorithm was developed and deployed to identify cases and controls using EHR data from six sites in the eMERGE network. The study found that rs145489027 located in the KCNIP4 region on chromosome 4 was associated with ACEi‐induced cough (Table 2 ). 31 A CP algorithm was also used to study the genetic determinants of drug‐resistant epilepsy (DRE), revealing that rs76950094 in the NTRK2 gene was significantly associated with DRE (Table 2 ). 32 CP algorithms paired with GWAS have also been used to study genetic determinants of T2DM, asthma exacerbation, hypothyroidism, Clostridioides difficile infection, variability in atrioventricular conduction, heparin‐induced thrombocytopenia, clopidogrel response, and development of major adverse cardiac events (MACE) among statins users (Table 2 ). 30 , 33 , 34 , 35 , 36 , 37 , 38 , 39 While there have been fewer studies coupling EHR data and other ‘omics (e.g., metabolomics and proteomics) data, EHRs connected to biobanks of discarded samples have the potential to be utilized for other ‘omics research as well. 40 CPs can identify patients meeting a certain phenotype with eligible samples in the biobank, the specimens could be analyzed, and then linked with the EHR data. 40 Conducting clinical trials can be challenging due to the high cost, regulations, and lack of effective strategies to identify and enroll eligible patients. EHR‐based CPs can be a great approach for recruiting a diverse and large cohort of eligible patients for pragmatic clinical trials. 41 For instance, Aspirin Dosing: A Patient‐Centric Trial Assessing Benefits and Long‐Term Effectiveness (ADAPTABLE) is a pragmatic, randomized, open‐label clinical trial that PCORnet launched to compare the effectiveness and safety of 81 mg vs. 325 mg aspirin doses in preventing atherosclerotic cardiovascular events in patients with a history of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD). PCORnet leveraged the ASCVD CP algorithm, which was run across 40 different institutions and identified 657,215 eligible subjects, of whom 15,076 participants were enrolled in the trial. 42 Another example is the Pragmatic Evaluation of Events and Benefits of Lipid‐lowering in Older Adults (PREVENTABLE) clinical trial, which is assessing the benefit of statins in reducing the risk of dementia in elderly patients. PREVENTABLE is using a CP algorithm to enroll about 20,000 participants from 100 different sites across US. 43 These examples indicate that CPs are an effective strategy to identify large and diverse populations for pragmatic clinical trials. Studies such as ADAPTABLE and PREVENTABLE demonstrate that when CPs are well designed, and based on common data fields, such as those in a CDM, CPs can have a high degree of interoperability and can be applied across multiple study sites. 14 , 15 Studies conducted in eMERGE also utilized a multi‐site approach, and through the work on many different phenotypes, eMERGE investigators recommend mutli‐site validation, through an iterative process, to improve phenotype performance. 14 Other studies that have applied CPs to EHR data from multiple sites, have shown the importance of detailed methodology and the inclusion of investigators from each site who understand the health care system and patient population. 22 , 29 , 44 MEDICATION INFORMATION MAPPING Medication information interoperability and terminologies Medication information is a vital component of EHR data that is used in EHR‐based clinical pharmacology research. This information includes medication name, indication, class, dosage form, strength, and route of administration. From a clinical practice perspective, accurate, and standardized documentation of this information enables easier EHR medication retrieval to assess treatment effectiveness and safety, and identify a cohort of patients with a specific disease of interest. 15 However, most healthcare systems record medications differently based on commercial drug databases. The same medication may be associated with various brand and generic names, making normalization and exchange of its information across different clinical systems difficult. 15 This lack of interoperability in addition to affecting patient safety, also hinders drug‐based research. 45 To address this issue, many comprehensive standardized biomedical terminologies have been developed to facilitate interoperability and consistent representation of medication information among different healthcare systems, and promote research endeavors, quality improvement, and clinical decision support. 46 Among these standardized terminologies are RxNorm, Medication Reference Terminology (MED‐RT), Anatomical Therapeutic Chemical (ATC) classification system, National Drug Code (NDC), and Systematized Nomenclature of Medicine‐Clinical Terms (SNOMED‐CT). RxNorm was developed by the National Library of Medicine (NLM) to provide normalized names for clinical drugs. RxNorm consists of both generic and brand names, dose form, and route of administration. RxNorm uses a RxNorm Concept Unique Identifier (RxCUI) to link equivalent drug terms from various vocabulary sources. It also links drug names to many drug vocabulary software used in pharmacy and drug–drug interactions, such as Multum, First Databank, and Micromedex. 47 MED‐RT is a replacement and successor to the National Drug File‐Reference Terminology (NDF‐RT). MED‐RT was established by the Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) Veterans Health Administration and provides details on mechanism of action, physiological effects, and established pharmacological class, as well as relationships between concepts. 48 ATC was developed by the World Health Organization (WHO) to group drugs into different classes based on anatomical, therapeutic, chemical, and pharmacological characteristics. It also includes the defined daily doses and routes of administration for most drugs based on their therapeutic and pharmacological classification. 49 NDC was established by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) as a universal product identifier. Each product is assigned a unique 10‐digit number that identifies the labeler (i.e., producer), package (size and type), and the product (i.e., the concentration, dosage form, and formulation). 50 SNOMED‐CT is a standardized multilingual terminology managed by the International Health Terminology Standards Development Organization (IHTSDO) that allows documenting and exchanging clinical health information in electronic healthcare systems. It contains a wide range of medical categories such as diagnosis, procedures, medications, clinical findings, signs and symptoms, and organisms. 51 Comparisons and challenges of medication information mapping Medication information mapping using standard terminologies has been a great tool for establishing a set of standardized drug codes to classify medications, assess medication concept coverage, and facilitate clinical and epidemiological research, data interoperability, and clinical decision support in EHRs. 46 There have been multiple studies comparing drug information coverage within and between terminologies. One study compared the performance in identification and classification of antihypertensive medications between two terminologies (ATC and MED‐RT) and the Drug Ontology (DrOn) with EHR‐based prescription data from a hypertensive adult population. They found broad overlap between the classification systems (84%–95%), and observed that DrOn, which has a hand curation component, was more accurate at correctly identifying antihypertensive drugs (Table 3 ). 52 Another study explored RxNorm–NDF‐RT mappings of 745 pediatric medications collected from over 8,000 children enrolled in The Environmental Determinants of Diabetes in Young (TEDDY) study. The results showed that 86% and 92% of TEDDY medications were assigned NDF‐RT Chemical Structural and Pharmaceutical Preparation classifications, respectively (Table 3 ). It is noteworthy that although NDF‐RT was designed for the adult Veteran population, it has a remarkable coverage for pediatric medications. 53 There are also tools to assist with automated mapping of medication terms. A study comparing three mapping terminologies, RxMix, Usagi, and MedXN, showed that RxMix achieved the best‐automated mapping performance, with an F1 score of 90%, compared to 82% in Usagi and 74% in MedXN (Table 3 ). 54 TABLE 3. Summary of medication mapping methods and comparisons. Method and data sources Input data Comparisons and findings Drug type DrOn + Manual Curation 52 RxCUIs EHR‐based medication mapping constructed from DrOn, with manual curation, and compared to ATC classifications Antihypertensives RxNorm + NDF‐RT 53 RxCUIs EHR‐based pediatric medications were mapped to RxNorm and then linked to NDF‐RT to measure NDF‐RT drug class coverage Pediatric medications RxNorm + Manual curation 54 RxCUIs Mapping tools such as RxMix, Usagi, and MedXN were compared with respect to their mapping performance Miscellaneous RxNorm 56 RxCUIs 3 commercial knowledge bases: DataBank, Micromedex, and Multum were evaluated for their coverage and overlap of drug–drug interaction Drug–drug interaction DrugBank + NDF‐RT+ RxNorm 57 RxCUIs EHR‐based pediatric medications were mapped to RxNorm and then linked to NDF‐RT to measure NDF‐RT drug class coverage Drug–drug interaction RxNorm + Manual curation 59 , 60 RxCUIs Adverse Event Reporting System with drug names were mapped to RxNorm Adverse drug reactions SNOMED CT + RxNorm + unique ingredient identifier + and NDF‐RT + and MedDRA 61 RxCUIs Encoding allergy content was compared between SNOMED CT, RxNorm, and NDF‐RT, and MedDRA terminologies to facilitate documentation of drug allergy in EHRs Drug allergy UMLS + RxNorm + and NDF‐RT 63 CUI, RxCUIs, and NUI EHRs‐based herbal and dietary supplements data mapped to UMLS, RxNorm, and NDF‐RT Herbal and dietary supplements UMLS + RxNorm + NDF‐RT 64 CUI, RxCUIs, and NUI EHRs and online based dietary supplements data mapped to UMLS, RxNorm, and NDF‐RT Dietary supplements Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CUI, Concept Unique Identifier; DrON, Drug Ontology; EHR, electronic health record; MedDRA, Medication Dictionary for Regulatory Activities; MedXN, Medication Extraction and Normalization; NDF‐RT, National Drug File‐Reference Terminology; NUI, National Unique Identifier; RxCUIs, RxNorm Concept Unique Identifier; SNOMED CT, Systematized Nomenclature of Medicine ‐ Clinical Terms; UMLS, Unified Medical Language System. Medication information mapping can also be used for accurate and prompt identification of adverse events (AE) caused by drug–drug interactions (DDIs) and adverse drug events (ADEs). The Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) established a reference list of highly severe and clinically significant DDI alerts that should be considered in EHR systems. 55 One study found that three commercial knowledge bases (Multum, First Databank, and Micromedex) covered 99.9% of ONC DDI alerts, even though they only had an overlap of 48% among the knowledge bases (Table 3 ). 56 Another study showed that NDF‐RT and DrugBank covered 60% of the ONC DDI list, with an overlap of 24%–30% (Table 3 ). 57 For ADEs, the US FDA Adverse Event Reporting System (AERS) database is a valuable tool for voluntary post‐marketing detection and reporting ADEs. Nevertheless, reporting drug names in this database lacks standardization and hinders its utilization in drug safety research. 58 Through the normalization of drug names in AERS to RxNorm, AEs to SNOMED‐CT, and curating drug class information, the AERS database can be organized in a more meaningful way (Table 3 ). 59 , 60 With the availability of many DDI detection resources and multiple methods to curate and standardize ADEs, additional research is needed to determine which ones are best suited for implementation in EHR systems. Drug allergies and dietary supplements are other areas where medication information mapping may be valuable. Drug allergies are often poorly documented in EHRs due to lack of standardized allergy terminology. A study comparing five terminologies (NDF‐RT, SNOMED‐CT, RxNorm, unique ingredient identifier [UNII], and MedDRA) to assess their representing characteristics and coverage of allergy information found that SNOMED‐CT achieved the highest characteristics, whereas RxNorm showed the highest drug allergen concept coverage (Table 3 ). This suggests that SNOMED‐CT and RxNorm could be appropriate options for encoding drug allergy concepts in EHRs. 61 The recent increase in consumption of dietary supplements to improve health outcomes has increased the possibility of drug supplement interactions (DSI). EHRs could be a rich data source for evaluating the effect of DSIs on patient safety; however, mining EHRs for DSIs is challenging due to the lack of terminologies representing dietary supplements. 62 Studies have shown that ~40% of medications and supplements extracted from the medication list at an academic medical center were herbal and dietary supplements (Table 3 ). 63 Additionally, only 19.65%, 14.67%, and 12.88% of supplement terms extracted from the Dietary Supplement Label Database (DSLD) and an EHR medication list were covered by RxNorm, Unified Medical Language System (UMLS), and NDF‐RT, respectively (Table 3 ). 64 These findings indicate the limited coverage of these terminologies and thus warrant further studies to find a better representative terminology that can be used to improve dietary supplement research. Despite the advantages of standardized medication mapping terminologies in supporting clinical pharmacology research, these terminologies present some challenges. First, with the rapid evolution of these systems, it can be difficult to find the most up to date version of their concept coverage or determine the appropriateness of a certain terminology in a given context. The complexity of these systems increases as they become more complete and computable, suggesting that while they are more valuable, they are more challenging to be utilized appropriately. 45 , 46 Second, medication mapping across terminologies can rarely be achieved automatically; manual curation is often required. 52 Third, medication information mapping is time and effort‐consuming; thus, expert intervention is also often required. Even after mapping is completed, maps need to be updated and maintained regularly. 65 Fourth, terminologies cannot always differentiate between the dosage forms and related indications, resulting in medication misclassification. 52 Finally, despite the high prevalence of dietary and herbal supplement use and the importance of these data in clinical research, the coverage of supplements in standardized terminologies is limited. 63 NATURAL LANGUAGE PROCESSING Natural language processing in clinical research The accessibility of EHRs and the abundance of medication information they contain have contributed to their use for numerous medication‐related studies, including detection of AEs and pharmacokinetic (PK) and pharmacodynamic (PD) studies. EHRs also contain clinical notes that are a very rich source of various clinical outcomes including efficacy and toxicity (e.g., AEs) as reported by patients. Often, however, these notes are kept in an unstructured, free text format, which is difficult to use in research. Natural language processing is a great tool for information extraction from free text. For example, a research group at Partners HealthCare Clinical Informatics Research and Development in Boston, MA developed an NLP algorithm for EHR provider notes that searches for and outputs names of inactive medications, since this information is not always updated properly in patient medication lists (Table 4 ). 66 They also trained another NLP tool to identify medications to which patients had not adhered as noted in provider notes (Table 4 ). 67 TABLE 4. Programs and methods for natural language processing of drug or drug dose information from EHR clinic notes. NLP Brief description Inactive medications identification Algorithm 66 Identifies discontinued medications from clinical notes in the electronic medical record Medication non‐adherence algorithm 67 Identifies documentations of medication non‐adherence in clinical notes MedEx 68 , 88 Extracts medication information such as drug name, strength, route, and duration from clinical notes CLAMP 69 Extracts various type of information from clinical note, ranging from general pipelines (e.g., medication information) to specific pipelines (e.g., smoking status and lab results) MedXN 70 Extracts detailed medication information from clinical notes and normalizes it to the RxNorm Concept Unique Identifier (RxCUI) MedExtractR 71 Extracts medication information of a drug of interest such as drug name, dose amount, strength, route, and duration, and also extracts the last dosing intake time if present in clinical notes HITEx 72 Extracts the principal diagnosis, comorbidities and smoking status for respiratory disorders ScispaCy 74 Extracts information related to medication, dose, therapies, symptoms, bowel movements, and nutritional ontological categories cTAKES 75 Extract medication information from clinical free text MERKI 76 Identifies medication information from discharge summaries MetaMap 77 Extracts medication indication from drug label Textractor 79 Extracts prescribed medications and the indication for their prescription from clinical notes MED7 80 Extracts non‐concept medication related information such as drug name, strength, route, and duration from clinical notes Dose data building algorithm 81 Extracts medication information from clinical notes to build longitudinal medication dose datasets MTERMS 85 Extracts medication information from outpatient clinical notes from an ambulatory EHR Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CLAMP, Clinical Language Annotation, Modeling, and Processing; cTAKES, clinical Text Analysis and Knowledge Extraction System; HITEx, Health Information Text Extraction; MED7, named‐entity recognition model that identifies seven categories of medication information, including: name, route, dose, strength, frequency, duration, and form; MedEx, Medication Extraction System; MedExtractR, Medication Extraction using R; MedXN, Medication Extraction and Normalization; MERKI, Medication Extraction and Reconciliation Knowledge Instrument; MetaMap, a NLP to map biomedical text to concepts in the Unified Medical Language System (UMLS) Metathesaurus; MTERMS, Medical Text Extraction, Reasoning and Mapping System; Textractor, Text Extractor. Other examples include NLP systems that have been developed for the purpose of extracting medication information from clinical notes in EHRs in order to put these data to use in clinical studies. These systems can identify much more than medication names and extract a patient''s dosing information, 68 , 69 , 70 , 71 symptoms, 69 and comorbidities (Table 4 ). 72 Much of the development of NLP systems for application in medication information extraction was facilitated by the Third i2b2 Workshop on Natural Language Processing Challenges for Clinical Records held in 2009, which challenged teams to create an NLP algorithm that could identify medication information from discharge summaries. 73 Twenty teams participated in this challenge and each system was developed with a different methodology including rule‐based, machine learning, or a hybrid of these methods. Natural language processing and medication information extraction One of the first NLP systems to become widely used for clinical data is MedEx, 68 an open‐source stand‐alone application for Linux and Windows (Table 4 ). MedEx was developed in 2010 to extract medication information from clinical notes. After identifying a medication name and signature, the system outputs drug entities such as drug name, strength, route, frequency, and reason for prescription. Before MedEx, the NLP system CLAMP 69 was developed in Java to extract medication information through modules and graphical user interfaces (GUI) (Table 4 ). It allows the user to extract different types of information from clinical notes ranging from medical information to specific demographic information. The GUI system can create a more user‐friendly NLP experience for users who may be just beginning to utilize and learn about NLP. Developed in 2014, the MedXN system is based in Apache UIMA and was built to extract and normalize medication information from clinical notes into the RxNorm concept unique identifier (RxCUI) format (Table 4 ). 70 The RxNorm standard order writes information in the following form: ‘medication+strength+dose.’ A strength of the MedXN system is that the creators manually compiled a list of words that could show up as false positives because they have names similar to medications. To check for these, the system will only treat these specific words as medications if they start with an uppercase letter and have corresponding dosing attributes, which may help this tool to perform better than others. 70 The medExtractR package is the first NLP that was specifically developed to extract medication information using R programming language (Table 4 ). 71 This NLP takes a targeted approach for a specific medication of interest to extract the following information from clinical notes: drug name, strength, dose amount, frequency, route, duration, and the last dosing intake time if present. The system searches the window around a drug name in a clinical note for relevant medical information. As it only searches user‐provided drugs of interest, in addition to generic and brand names, the user can provide abbreviated names or special names that could be specific to each institution (or clinicians), which may not be relevant to other institutions. 71 As a targeted NLP, the length of the search window for drug names can be specified or fine‐tuned by users, which could increase the accuracy of information extraction. The ability to tell the program exactly what drug names are of interest makes it a strong candidate for NLP use when interested in specific medications rather than an overall snapshot of patient medications. 71 This is different from most other NLP tools that take a more general approach as they search for all medication names at the same time. Many of the NLP systems developed for medication extraction rely on some dictionary of medication names such as RxNorm to identify medication data in free‐text clinical notes. Another such system is ScispaCy, 74 which was developed in Python, that maps medication names to SNOMED Clinical Terms (CT) and RxNorm terms, and extracts information related to medication, dose, therapies, symptoms, bowel movements, and nutritional ontological categories (Table 4 ). SNOMED CT and RxNorm are also the basis for the drug name mapping in cTAKES that runs on Java and Apache UMIA (Table 4 ). 75 Another NLP system that relies on RxNorm is the MERKI parser which was developed based on discharge notes (Table 4 ). 76 MetaMap is an NLP system that was built to map concepts from clinical notes to the UMLS Metathesaurus and is accessible via the Java API (Table 4 ). 77 The UMLS Metathesaurus is a library upkept by the National Library of Medicine and includes clinical terms from over 200 medication dictionaries including SNOMED CT and RxNorm. 78 An NLP tool Textractor combines the MetaMap with rule‐based, machine learning, and pattern matching approaches within the Apache UMIA framework (Table 4 ). 79 Another NLP software that uses the UMLS Metathesaurus dictionary is HITEx, 72 a software that runs on the open‐source platform Gate (Table 4 ). Since SNOMED CT and RxNorm are subsets of the UMLS Metathesaurus library, MetaMap, and Textractor should be able to identify more clinical terms from EHRs than the NLPs that only use one or both SNOMED CT and RxNORM. Users working with a large database with many different medications may note the importance of this difference. Finally, Med7 is an open source named‐entity recognition model built in Python using the spaCy environment in 2021 to identify the following medication information: medication name, route of administration, frequency, dosage, strength, form, and duration (Table 4 ). 80 Natural language processing and pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics Longitudinal drug dosing data (i.e., as a function of time) are critical data in any PK and PD studies. EHRs can be useful to conduct population PK and/or PD studies if longitudinal drug dosing information can be reliably obtained. Medication prescription data can be easily extracted from the EHRs as they usually exist as structured form, but these data may be incomplete (e.g., missing dosing or frequency). NLPs can be useful to extract more complete medication dosing information from clinical notes, which can be stand‐alone data or complement the prescription data. NLPs also can be useful to extract PD outcomes that can be studied as a function of PK or medication dosing. Although NLPs developed for the general purpose of extracting information from clinical notes can be utilized to extract medication information and PD outcomes, there has been little work done in developing NLPs specifically applied to PK and PD studies. The medExtractR package is one of few such NLPs developed specifically for this purpose. 71 In addition to detailed medication dosing information, it can also extract the last dosing intake time of a drug of interest if present in clinical notes, which can be crucial information for PK/PD studies. Extracted raw output of detailed medication dosing information is necessary for PK and PD studies but not usable in the format provided by NLP tools. Both structured and NLP extracted raw data must be converted into a longitudinal drug dosing dataset through data processing. To address this issue, an algorithm written in the R programming language was developed to convert medication information extracted via several NLP systems (Table 4 ). 81 , 82 These longitudinal dosing data are especially useful in medication‐related studies including PK/PD components. A system called EHR2PKPD ( https://choileena.github.io/ ) has been developed to facilitate drug‐related studies including PK and PD studies using EHRs. 83 This system contains several modules for data extraction, data processing for both structured and NLP extracted raw output, and data building, which enables performing drug‐related studies more efficiently. Due to the modularized design, new methods can be easily added as needed. EHR2PKPD is implemented as the R package, EHR. 84 Other natural language processing applications NLP has also been used for other applications in clinical pharmacology research, such as medication reconciliation, pharmacovigilance, and in pharmacogenomic studies. NLP can be a great tool for improving medication reconciliation and data interoperability within EHRs. At the institutional level, medication information is often coded using different terminologies, making integration across institutions difficult. NLP can bridge this gap by extracting medication information from clinical notes using various terminologies and establishing a dynamic mapping between them to increase interoperability. One NLP approach, Medical Text Extraction, Reasoning and Mapping System (MTERMS), assesses medication mapping between a local terminology and the RxNorm standard terminology (Table 4 ). 85 NLP can also be an invaluable tool for mining ADEs in EHRs. The Medical Language Extraction and Encoding System (MedLEE) is one example that was used to detect rhabdomyolysis and agranulocytosis in 199,920 hospitalization records from New York Presbyterian Hospital (Table 4 ). 86 MedLEE showed high sensitivity and specificity and identified 687 rhabdomyolysis and 772 agranulocytosis cases. In addition, MedLEE resulted in a significant time saving: for every hour spent on the development process, at least 20 hours were saved on manual review. 86 NLPs have also been used to identify opioid misuse from clinical notes and have been shown to detect approximately one‐third more cases of opioid misuse compared to using ICD‐9 codes alone. 87 Finally, given the growing emphasis on precision medicine in recent years, another application of the NLP systems is the facilitation of pharmacogenetics studies. The MedEx system has been used to extract and calculate the weekly dose of warfarin from clinical notes (Table 4 ). 88 The extracted data on stable weekly warfarin dose were used with data on four genetic variants in the vitamin K epoxide reductase complex subunit 1 ( VKORC1 ) and cytochrome p450 2C9 ( CYP2C9 ) genes. The study revealed that the system was effective in capturing weekly warfarin dosages, and the genetic analysis successfully replicated the established association between weekly warfarin dose and VKORC1 and CYP2C9 genetic variants. 88 ADDITIONAL CONSIDERATIONS FOR ELECTRONIC HEALTH RECORD‐BASED CLINICAL PHARMACOLOGY RESEARCH Incomplete data, heterogeneity, and data structure Complete data capture is a salient consideration when utilizing EHRs for clinical research. Patients can move in and out of healthcare systems and be assigned a different identification number within each system. Sometimes (e.g., due to name changes), patients may even be assigned multiple numbers within the same system. This makes it challenging to connect records over time, generating fragmented EHR data. Transience in EHRs can have a significant impact on phenotype algorithm performance. 14 Methods such as setting a specific study period, or using privacy‐preserving entity resolution have been utilized to overcome this limitation. 14 , 89 However, more novel systems, such as a unique lifetime identification number that links records to death registries and between different providers, could remedy this problem in the future (Table 5 ). TABLE 5. Additional considerations in EHR‐based drug data in clinical pharmacology research. Considerations Challenges Remarks Transience in the EHR and incomplete data capture 14 Patients can move in and out of healthcare systems and are assigned a different identification number within each system. Privacy‐preserving entity resolution has been utilized to overcome this limitation Assigning a unique lifetime identification number that links records to death registries and between different providers could resolve this problem EHR heterogeneity across sites 90 Heterogeneity due to differences in databases and data structure can significantly vary study outcomes under the same study design, and affects replication of clinical research The use of CDMs could enhance reproducibility and validation of EHR‐based studies Data are nonstationary in EHR 91 There is always development of better diagnostics and treatments, changes in the methods of coding and documentation, emergence of more compelling clinical evidence, introduction of new software, and changes in EHR infrastructure Defining study variables, cohorts, and models based on EHR data need to be updated regularly to align with the changes in data structure Presence of unstructured data 15 The extraction and analysis of narrative data are difficult and time‐consuming since they are heterogenous and do not adhere to grammar and punctuation rules NLP could help in extracting and processing these data Rare phenotypes 92 Rare phenotypes might not have consensus on their clinical definition or ICD codes. Despite the widespread use of ICD codes, some rare conditions are not represented It is challenging to evaluate algorithm performance for rare phenotypes with a frequency of less than 1% Inconsistency in diagnosis codes 93 ICD codes were designed for billing purposes and may not accurately reflect the patient''s condition. ICD codes can be used for additional procedures or diagnostic tests, and for conditions that are hard to diagnose. The presence of several ICD codes over multiple visits may imply many misdiagnoses before the definitive diagnosis is made Incorporating multiple types of data such as diagnosis codes, clinical notes, and specific medication from EHRs result in superior phenotype performance, compared to diagnostic codes alone Computable phenotype validation 95 Some computable phenotypes are validated locally by their creators and sometimes by a single reviewer, who may not be a clinical provider Validation by clinical experts and at different sites can produce more rigorous computable phenotypes Phenotypes with temporal relationships 13 Creating temporal timelines translating temporality to future implementation It is important to balance accuracy in models and drug information, with potential for future application Diversity in EHR populations 96 Lack of adequate representation of underrepresented populations Incorporating these populations has the potential to increase diversity and generalizability Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CDM, Common Data Model; EHR, electronic health record; ICD, International Classification of Disease; NLP, natural language processing. Reproducibility is a crucial aspect of high‐quality clinical research; however, replication and validation of EHR‐based study results at different clinical sites can be challenging due to the heterogeneity of different EHRs (Table 5 ). It has been shown that differences in databases and data structure can significantly vary study outcomes under the same study design. 90 Often, heterogeneity can be attributed to the lack of standard terminologies or common fields. The use of CDMs such as OMOP and PCORnet, and sharing codes in a public repository such as PheKB and GitHub could enhance reproducibility and validation in EHR‐based studies. 22 The structure of data within EHRs can also present challenges. Data in EHRs are nonstationary (Table 5 ). They change over time due to the development of better diagnostics and treatments, changes in the methods of coding and documentation, emergence of more compelling clinical evidence, introduction of new software, and changes in EHR infrastructure. These changes impact analysis, replication, and the clinical value of predictive models. Thus, the definitions of study variables, cohorts, and models based on EHR data need to be updated regularly to align with the changes in data structure. 91 Another challenge to using EHRs in clinical research is the presence of unstructured data (Table 5 ). Unstructured data are stored in EHRs in the form of narrative data such as clinical notes, pathology reports, radiological images, surgical records, and discharge summaries. These data contain valuable information necessary to understand certain clinical situations. However, the extraction and analysis of narrative data are difficult and time‐consuming since they are heterogenous and do not adhere to grammar and punctuation rules. 15 NLP and deep learning algorithms can help extract and analyze this type of data, 71 , 92 and integration of both structured and unstructured data has been advantageous in disease prediction in several studies. 14 , 16 , 93 Rare phenotypes and phenotype accuracy There are phenotypes with no consensus on their clinical definition or ICD codes. Despite the widespread use of ICD codes, some rare conditions are not represented (Table 5 ). This creates difficulty in evaluating algorithm performance for phenotypes with a frequency of less than 1%, and to date, most studied phenotypes have a prevalence of ≥1% among the EHR population. 92 The algorithm performance depends on experts'' ability to define the phenotypes based on the available EHR data and independent reviewers'' agreement on the absence or presence of the phenotype. Data in EHRs are inadequate to characterize some conditions, particularly those that are not associated with diagnostic codes, procedures codes, or specified therapies. Therefore, studying a rare phenotype that is poorly reported within the EHR is difficult. To address these issues, best practices recommend multiple encounters, and layers of data. 14 With the widespread use of diagnostic codes, they have become an indispensable component of phenotype development. Yet, the accuracy and completeness of diagnosis codes can vary across care providers and settings (Table 5 ). 94 Additionally, ICD codes were designed for billing purposes and may not accurately reflect the patient''s condition. ICD codes can be used for additional procedures or diagnostic tests, and for conditions that are hard to diagnose, but the presence of several ICD codes over multiple visits may imply many misdiagnoses before the definitive diagnosis is made. Furthermore, the absence of ICD codes from patient''s records may not represent the lack of disease, and diagnostic criteria can change over time for a given condition, ultimately changing which patients do and do not have the condition. 15 Thus, relying on diagnostic codes alone may result in a CP algorithm with low sensitivity and specificity; whereas algorithms that incorporate multiple types of data such as diagnosis codes, clinical notes, and specific medication from EHRs result in superior phenotype performance, compared to diagnostic codes alone. 93 Computable phenotype validation and temporal phenotypes The purpose of validating CP algorithms is to confirm their accuracy (Table 5 ). Validation is ideally done through manual chart review by clinical experts in a blind fashion and across multiple sites, and includes the calculation of PPV, NPV, sensitivity, specificity, and interrater reliability. 14 However, some CPs are validated locally by their creators and sometimes by a single reviewer, who may not be a clinical provider. In addition, validation of some CPs does not include the interrater reliability score, resulting in a less rigorous and lower quality phenotype algorithm. 14 , 95 For many phenotypes, in order to accurately represent disease progression and treatment outcomes there are clinically relevant time events that must be captured (Table 5 ). Some phenotype algorithms that involve temporal relationships include those evaluating medication response or side effects. Identifying patients who meet phenotypes with temporal relationships requires capturing sequential clinical events from time‐stamped records. Depending on the complexity of the phenotype and the temporal relationship, this can be a non‐trivial task involving sophisticated NLP and machine learning approaches. 13 Further, temporal data in EHRs can be complex and misleading as clinical progress notes may reflect note initiation time rather than the actual clinical event. The time between note initiation, note completion, and clinical decision could be spaced by several hours or days, hence not representing the actual clinical scenario. 91 Additionally, information within clinical notes may be copied and pasted from previous notes. Thus, researchers must take care to accurately characterize temporal relationships within CPs. Diversity in EHR A persistent issue in clinical pharmacology research, similar to many areas of research, is the lack of adequate representation of the diverse individuals who make up patient populations (Table 5 ). 96 Underrepresented individuals include but are not limited to those who identify as non‐White race, Latino or other ethnicity, non‐European ancestry, pregnant people, children, the elderly, individuals with disabilities, LGBTQ+, lower socioeconomic status, and/or rural communities. The use of EHR data for research, which is more representative of the patients we serve, has the potential to increase diversity and generalizability of study findings. EHR data can also be used to generate research data for specific underrepresented populations. For example, a series of studies investigated the impact of a single variant, rs2814778‐C (the Duffy Null RBC antigen phenotype), which is established to confer lower white blood cell (WBC) counts without increasing risk for infection or other clinically significant outcomes. 97 , 98 , 99 This variant is common among individuals of African ancestry, and nearly absent from individuals of European ancestry. The lack of diversity in datasets used to define the “normal” reference ranges for WBC counts leads to a Eurocentric reference range. EHR data reveal that individuals with a rs2814778‐CC genotype are observed to have lower WBC counts and provide the necessary data to generate genotype‐specific reference ranges. 100 These genotype‐specific reference ranges are necessary, as despite the well‐established association of this variant to neutropenia with no clinical impact, individuals with this genotype are observed to bear a burden of inappropriate workup for their “low” WBC counts, including increased WBC testing, and more invasive and expensive bone marrow biopsy. 10 As individuals with this genotype are predominantly of African ancestry, this translates into a health equity issue. 101 Finally, EHR data also revealed increased risk for azathioprine discontinuation among those with the rs2814778‐CC genotype, attributed to hematopoietic toxicity, leading to potentially inappropriate exposure to second line agents. 9 Careful use of EHR data may expose sources of additional health inequities, enable research of underrepresented and special populations, and lead to practice changes that reduce health disparity. Careful interpretation of EHR data is also needed to ensure the data are not being used to reinforce health disparities or sustain existing biases. SUMMARY AND CONCLUSION EHRs contain vast amounts of information and hold great potential as a data source for clinical pharmacology research. While many EHR‐based studies have been conducted in recent years, there are additional challenges in creating drug‐related phenotypes from EHR‐based data. 12 , 13 , 14 , 15 We reviewed current considerations and recommendations for creating medication‐based CPs, mapping medication data from EHRs, utilizing NLP‐based methods, and additional issues that impact EHR‐based clinical pharmacology research. CPs have been shown to be more accurate through validation and the inclusion of multiple types of data (e.g., diagnosis, procedure, prescriptions, and vitals). 14 , 15 When medication or prescription information is included in CPs or clinical pharmacology research, mapping medication information becomes necessary. Use of standard terminologies such as RxNorm, MED‐RT, ATC system, NCD, or SNOMED‐CT has become standard. 47 , 48 , 49 , 50 , 51 However, multiple studies have shown that often one database or terminology cannot cover all scenarios, and that the inclusion of multiple terminologies, NLP methods, or manual curation provides the best results. 52 , 53 , 63 , 64 There have been many NLP methods developed to extract medication information from EHRs, particularly from unstructured data. 68 , 69 , 70 , 71 These NLP methods can be used for PK‐ and PD‐based studies, as well as pharmacogenomic based studies. 71 , 81 , 88 Finally, there are many additional considerations for studies utilizing EHR‐based data, including data structure, heterogeneity and missing data, rare phenotypes, and diversity. 14 , 15 , 91 , 92 , 97 , 98 , 99 By further understanding the complexities associated with conducting clinical pharmacology research using EHR‐based data, investigators will be better equipped to design thoughtful studies with more reproducible results. Additionally, there are many training opportunities that exist. There is great potential for continued training in the standardization of data fields and terminologies. 1 Furthermore, standardization of provider training in clinical notes could enable easier and more accurate extraction of clinical information. 73 For clinical pharmacists, there are many training options including formal biomedical informatics or health informatics programs, conferences and workshops, online coursework, and training modules within data platforms like those contained within the All of Us Researcher''s Workbench. 5 The availability of EHR data is only increasing with projects, networks, and data models such as the eMERGE Network, UK biobank, MVP, the All of Us Research Program, PCORnet, i2b2, and OMOP. 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 , 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 We anticipate progress in utilizing EHRs for clinical pharmacology research will only continue to expand and will lead to significant advances in our ability to understand differential drug response and predict adverse drug reactions. FUNDING INFORMATION Support for this project comes from NIH grants K01 HL141690 (CWM) and R03 HL172123 (CWM). CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT The authors declared no competing interests for this work. Jafari E, Blackman MH, Karnes JH, et al. Using electronic health records for clinical pharmacology research: Challenges and considerations. Clin Transl Sci. 2024;17:e13871. doi: 10.1111/cts.13871 Sara L. Van Driest: Manuscript development completed while at VUMC. REFERENCES 1. Hemingway H, Asselbergs FW, Danesh J, et al. Big data from electronic health records for early and late translational cardiovascular research: challenges and potential. Eur Heart J. 2018;39:1481‐1495. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Bowton E, Field JR, Wang S, et al. Biobanks and electronic medical records: enabling cost‐effective research. Sci Transl Med. 2014;6:234cm3. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Allen NE, Sudlow C, Peakman T, Collins R, Biobank UK. UK biobank data: come and get it. Sci Transl Med. 2014;6:224ed4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Gottesman O, Kuivaniemi H, Tromp G, et al. The Electronic Medical Records and Genomics (eMERGE) network: past, present, and future. Genet Med. 2013;15:761‐771. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Denny JC, Rutter JL, Goldstein DB, et al. The “All of Us” Research Program. N Engl J Med. 2019;381:668‐676. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Gaziano JM, Concato J, Brophy M, et al. Million Veteran Program: a mega‐biobank to study genetic influences on health and disease. J Clin Epidemiol. 2016;70:214‐2