INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4865,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610525','Multi-Modality Imaging in Caseous Calcification of the Mitral Annulus: Case Series and Clinical Implications.','Dadański E, Olszanecka A, Sorysz D, Wojciechowska W, Chyrchel B, Sowa-Staszczak A, Surdacki A, Rajzer M.','2024','caseous echogenic caseous ccma ccma ccma caseous ccma ccma camouflaged caseous hasty ccma ccma flights stairs murmur audible echolucency bulging ccma caseous polypoid inetratrial ovalis isodence hypodense obscuration caseous hypodense precession ccma reimplantation protruding caseous consilium cardiosurgeons midesophageal commissural zoomed midesophageal commissural caseous exudates sutured bacteriological tribute stabbing hyperdense caseous pulmonologist deoxyglucose spiculated scrapings deoxyglucose caseous parasternal caseous midesophageal caseous beneath ccma precession caseous ccma regressed ccma ccma pathomorphological ccma ccma ccma ccma ccma ccma myxomas echolucency ccma posteriorly caseous echolucent petrification echogenic ccma ccma consilium customizable ccma ccma ccma ccma ccam ccma electrocardiology jagiellonian cracow sahasakul naessens tajik nagueh massera kizer dweck nakanishi daimon ultrason s10396 elgendy caseous e27 clc mallisho alsafwah jcu akmm myxomas wjc v14 senel schlensak gawaz jaccas srivatsa hor lnmac s10554','Abstract Case series Patients: Female, 78-year-old • Female, 79-year-old • Female, 73-year-old Final Diagnosis: Caseous calcification of the mitral annulus Symptoms: Incidental detection Clinical Procedure: Computed tomography • echocardiography • magnetic resonance image • transesophageal echocardiogram Specialty: Cardiology • Radiology Objective: Rare disease Background: Echogenic masses in the mitral annulus are often incidental findings during routine echocardiography examinations. Most represent the wide spectrum of underlying mitral annular calcifications and usually do not require further diagnostic workup. However, a rare variant called “caseous calcification of the mitral annulus” (CCMA) can be challenging to diagnose and often requires an extensive workup with the use of multiple imaging studies in order to accurately confirm the pathology. Case Reports: The aim of this case series was to illustrate the range of challenges associated with CCMA in various clinical scenarios to highlight the importance of multi-modality imaging in the differential diagnosis. Patient 1 was a 78-year-old asymptomatic woman with a round echo-dense structure incidentally found during a routine echocardiographic examination. Patient 2 was a 79-year-old woman with a suspected mitral abscess. Patient 3 was a 73-year-old woman with an incidentaloma (lung mass), with a concomitant lesion of the mitral annulus. All 3 patients required a unique combination of confirmatory imaging studies to ultimately confirm their diagnosis. Conclusions: The incidental detection of CCMA-like changes requires multi-modality imaging to differentiate from abscesses and proliferative changes in the mitral annulus. Transthoracic echocardiography, transesophageal echocardiography, computed tomography, and cardiac magnetic resonance imaging play a key role in this diagnostic workup. Using a combination of these imaging modalities can enhance diagnostic accuracy and strongly influence the subsequent treatment and management strategies. Keywords: Echo-Planar Imaging; Tomography Scanners, X-Ray Computed; Heart; Heart Diseases; Heart Valve Diseases Introduction Mitral annular calcification is a frequently encountered echocardiographic observation, especially in older populations. A less common variant is caseous calcification of the mitral annulus (CCMA), which has a prevalence of 0.6% in patients exhibiting mitral annular calcification on echocardiograms. The differential diagnosis of CCMA can be challenging. Clinical cardiologists need to recognize the complexities of this diagnosis and be aware of the potential necessity of using multiple imaging studies to confirm it. This condition can be camouflaged initially, as it shares many identifying markers with more severe conditions, such as an underlying infection, abscess of the mitral valve, or a potential tumor. Generally, caseous calcification is non-malignant, with a good prognosis, and requires only annual follow-up visits, unlike the more serious conditions mentioned above. An incorrect and hasty diagnosis can lead to the omission of a potentially life-threatening disease or a qualification for a potentially unnecessary and invasive procedure. The aim of this case series was to provide clinical insight into CCMA and highlight a patient-specific approach to selecting the proper imaging studies for accurate diagnosis. Case Reports Case 1: CCMA as the Incidental Finding A 78-year-old female patient with a history of hypertension and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation was referred to the Cardiology Outpatient Clinic for a wellness assessment. At the time of the visit, the patient was in a generally good condition, without symptoms of angina and heart failure. She was not limited when performing mild exertion and was able to walk up 2 flights of stairs without symptoms. During the physical examination, a mild systolic murmur at Erb’s point was audible (Levine scale 2/6). The patient’s blood pressure was well controlled, with an office blood pressure of 132/71 mmHg. An ECG showed a normal sinus rhythm at 68 beats/min and features of left atrial enlargement. Routine transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) showed preserved ejection fraction, mild diastolic dysfunction, left atrium enlargement, and calcification localized on the posterior mitral annulus. The mass was a round, echo-dense structure with a diameter of 1.8 cm and central areas of echolucency. It was bulging into the left atrium but did not significantly impair mitral inflow ( Figure 1 ). The initial workup ruled out possible underlying neoplasms or abscesses ( Table 1 ). Based on the localization of the mass and its characteristics, a diagnosis of CCMA was established. Considering the entire clinical picture, there was no need to modify antihypertensive, anti-arrhythmic, and anticoagulation therapy. The patient was advised to report for an annual echocardiographic assessment, to monitor the lesion. In the annual follow-up, both the echocardiography and the patient’s condition remained stable, with no significant changes observed. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Transthoracic echocardiography showing ( A ) 4-chamber and ( B ) 3-chamber apical view. The arrows point to degenerative changes on the mitral annulus, with the characteristics of caseous calcification of the mitral annulus. Table 1. Characteristic clinical and imaging features of myxoma and myocardial abscess. Disease Clinical features Echocardiography Computed tomography Magnetic resonance imaging Myxoma Heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, syncope Embolization symptoms Inflammatory symptoms (fever, arthralgia, and weight loss) Variable shape, but most often polypoid or papillary mass Attached by stalk to inetratrial septum (region of fossa ovalis) Oscillation during the heart cycle Common lack of calcification (70–90%) Highly vascular in Doppler image Isodence/slightly hypodense mass Lack of contrast enhancement T1-weighted isointense signal T2-weighted hyperintense signal T1/T2-weighted hypointense foci Myocardial Abscess Septic symptoms (fever, weakness, weight loss, apathy) Heart failure symptoms due to damage of valves History of recurrent infectious disease Complex fluid-like structure Shadowing or obscuration due to possible calcification Can closely resemble caseous calcification of the mitral annulus Hypodense structure Contrast enhancing wall T1/T2-weighted low or intermediate signal Steady-state free precession – isointense to muscle signal Contrast enhancement Open in a new tab Case 2: CCMA Impairing Mitral Valve Inflow and Differentiated with an Abscess A 79-year-old female patient with underlying obesity, hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and hypercholesterolemia presented with a paroxysmal complete heart block, which required an implantation of a permanent pacemaker. After implantation, the pocket site became infected, requiring the subsequent removal of the device, intravenous antibiotics, and a reimplantation on the right side of the chest. Over the next year, the patient was admitted multiple times for recurrent urinary tract infections. During one of these hospitalizations, in the Internal Medicine Department, the patient required a cardiology consultation for an exacerbation of heart failure, with increased episodic dyspnea and fatigue. A TTE was performed revealing a 5×3-cm mass protruding into the left atrium, resulting in dysfunction of the posterior leaflet and a drop in the mitral inflow ( Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Transthoracic echocardiography showing ( A ) 4-chamber and ( B ) 3-chamber apical view. The arrows point to degenerative changes on the mitral annulus, with the characteristics of caseous calcification of the mitral annulus. A chest computed tomography (CT) scan was performed, which suggested a chronic abscess of mitral annulus. Further workup was performed, with inconclusive findings after transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) and coronary angiography ( Figure 3 ). Considering the complexity of the case, a cardiological consilium was performed, consisting of interventional and clinical cardiologists as well as cardiosurgeons. This heart team recognized that, despite inconclusive findings, there was a high clinical suspicion to warrant exploratory surgery. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Transesophageal echocardiography showing ( A ) midesophageal mitral commissural view, ( B ) zoomed midesophageal mitral commissural view with color Doppler with velocity gradient, and ( C ) 3-dimensional reconstruction of the mitral annulus with tumor. The arrows point to ( A, C ) degenerative changes of the mitral annulus with the characteristics of caseous calcification of the mitral annulus and ( B ) color Doppler acceleration. Intraoperatively, after a partial resection of the posterior leaflet of the mitral valve, a 5×3×2-cm abscess within the mitral valve annulus that impeded the flow of blood into the left ventricle was discovered. Serous exudates measuring 15 to 20 g were removed, and the leaflet was sutured to the mitral annulus to conclude the procedure. The content was sent for further bacteriological identification (the result was unavailable at the time of this manuscript’s writing), and the patient quickly returned to a good general condition. She was ultimately discharged from the Cardiothoracic Surgery Department and, at the time of this report, remained under the care of cardiologists in an outpatient setting. Case 3: Clinical Tribute to Multimodality Assessment A 73-year-old female patient with well-controlled hypertension was admitted to the Emergency Department for shortness of breath and stabbing chest pain. Initial diagnostics ruled out any possible acute coronary syndrome, and exploratory high-resolution CT scanning of the chest revealed a subpleural mass measuring 10×16×9 mm, as well as a calcification measuring 36×35×20 mm around the border of the left atrium and ventricle near the mitral valve ( Figure 4 ). Figure 4. Open in a new tab Non-contrast computed tomography scan of the chest showing ( A ) axial and ( B ) coronal views. The arrows point to a hyperdense peripherally calcified mass, typical for caseous calcification of the mitral annulus. After consulting with a pulmonologist, a positron emission tomography (PET) scan using 18-fluoro-deoxyglucose ( 18 F-FDG) to evaluate the lung mass was performed. The study confirmed the presence of a spiculated metabolically active lung tumor (max standardized uptake value [SUV] 4.5) and moderately increased FDG metabolism (max SUV 5.2) around a prominent calcification (30×21 mm) in the posterior part of the left ventricle, involving the mitral valve and a small part of the left atrium ( Figure 5 ). SUV max reference ranges were at 3.8 when the neighboring liver was assessed. Further, a histopathological diagnosis was conducted by sampling nearby lymph nodes and bronchial scrapings, which determined the lesion to be benign in nature. Figure 5. Open in a new tab 18-Fluoro-deoxyglucose positron emission tomography showing ( A ) axial, ( B ) coronal, and ( C ) sagittal views. The arrows point to degenerative changes of the mitral annulus, with the characteristics of caseous calcification of the mitral annulus. TTE further revealed an oval-shaped structure in the posterior mitral annulus measuring 20 mm in diameter ( Figure 6 ). Afterward, TEE was performed, and a cardiac mass measuring 20×21×27 mm, surrounded by a capsule producing an acoustic shadow from the atrial side, was visualized ( Figure 7 ). Figure 6. Open in a new tab Transthoracic echocardiography showing ( A ) parasternal long axis and ( B ) 4-chamber apical view. The arrows point to degenerative changes of the mitral annulus, with the characteristics of caseous calcification of the mitral annulus. Figure 7. Open in a new tab Transesophageal echocardiography showing ( A ) midesophageal long and ( B ) short axis views. Arrows point to degenerative changes of the mitral annulus, which was finally diagnosed as caseous calcification of the mitral annulus. A mitral ring tumor was initially suspected but was ultimately ruled out after cardiac magnetic resonance imaging was performed, revealing a mass measuring 20×28 mm beneath the posterior leaflet of the mitral valve in the basal segment of the posterior wall, with tissue characteristic of CCMA: low signal intensity in cine steady-state free precession and T1 sequences, hyperintense rim in short TI inversion recovery sequence, no contrast enhancement in first pass perfusion, and peripheral late gadolinium enhancement ( Figure 8 ). No other pathologies within the myocardium were detected. Figure 8. Open in a new tab Cardiac cine magnetic resonance imaging showing ( A ) 1–2 min after gadolinium injection, ( B ) gadolinium enhancement, ( C ) T1 map, ( D ) T2 map, and ( E ) sequences. The arrows point to degenerative changes of the mitral annulus, with the characteristics of caseous calcification of the mitral annulus. Based on the available imaging studies and diagnostic work-up, the most likely diagnosis was determined to be CCMA with a benign lung tumor; both of which required only annual follow-up but no aggressive treatment management, considering the benign course of the independent diseases. Over the next 3 years, the nodular lesion in the lung completely regressed, and no CCMA complications were observed. Discussion Mitral annulus calcification is a chronic degenerative process. Thickening of the mitral valve leaflets becomes more prevalent after the age of 50 to 60 years [ 1 ]. There is generally also fibrous accumulation and calcifications involving the posterior wall of the left ventricle, posterior leaflet of the mitral valve, and posterior portion of the mitral annulus [ 2 – 4 ]. In most cases, deposits of calcium slowly begin to accrue over time on the surface of the valve, but they generally maintain their shape, to have very limited effects of the hemodynamic properties of valvular flow. In 0.6% of all mitral annular calcification cases [ 5 ], there is an atypical progression of mitral annular calcification [ 6 ], which is a starting point for the formation of CCMA. The exact pathomorphological mechanism of CCMA development is currently unknown. Considering its close pathological relation with mitral annular calcification, age appears to be one of the most important risk factors for CCMA, as seen in the cases we chose to highlight for this case series. CCMA is also more prevalent in women and patients with concomitant kidney diseases [ 6 ]. CCMA is most often asymptomatic and is usually discovered incidentally after simple imaging studies, as depicted throughout our case series. Most asymptomatic pathologies require only careful observation (case 1, case 3). Rare but potential complications of CCMA include a rupture of the calcified shell, with embolic material formation, heart block due to an impingement on the conduction system, increased frequency of infective endocarditis, or dysfunction of the mitral valve. Despite the benign course of the disease, it is important to remember that CCMA can often mimic more severe pathologies, such as abscesses or tumors; therefore, an urgent but thorough diagnostic workup is paramount to ensure that the patients are adequately treated from this initial consultation [ 4 , 5 ]. Characteristic features of these diseases useful in the differential diagnosis are presented in Table 1 [ 7 , 8 ]. A comparison of echocardiographic images of mitral annular calcification, myxomas, and abscesses is shown in Figure 9 . If the mass is localized to a specific leaflet, and there are particular radiological features, such as echolucency, a TTE is generally sufficient for confirming the diagnosis of CCMA, as described in case 1. However, in cases in which there is a high clinical suspicion, particularly those characterized by restricted acoustic viewing or suboptimal TTE image quality, individuals can require a more advanced TEE for a more precise assessment of the mass and improved visualization of posteriorly situated structures. In the most complicated cases, in which the risk of malignancy or infection is substantial, multiple imaging studies must be considered. Characteristic features in different imaging modalities are shown in Table 2 [ 9 ]. Figure 9. Open in a new tab Transthoracic echocardiography showing 4-chamber apical view. The arrows point to ( A ) mitral annulus calcification 3-chamber apical view, ( B ) myxoma tumor 3-chamber apical view, and ( C ) mitral annulus abscess. Table 2. Characteristic features of caseous calcification of the mitral annulus in particular medical imaging techniques. Echocardiography Computed tomography Magnetic resonance imaging Egg-shaped structure limited to the area of the posterior mitral annulus, reaching the posterior part of left ventricle and posterior peri-annular zone, exhibiting synchronous movement with myocardium Lesion might affect the motion of the mitral valve, leading to functional narrowing or regurgitation The mass displays a central echolucent area surrounded by a dense petrification, leading to a bright echogenic periphery Round or oval-shaped calcified region around the edges of the posterior mitral valve, exhibiting a varied density (calcific: 130 Hounsfield units [HU]; fibrous: 60–110; lipid rich: 60 HU) T1-weighted fast-spin or turbo-spin echo: The mass with bright signals from the center enclosed by a hypointense edge, separated from the nearby myocardium and posterior mitral valve T2 short tau inversion recovery (STIR): The mass with lack of central signal but with contrasting hyperintensity ring-like peripheral area T2 half-fourier single-shot turbo spin-echo: image similar to T2 STIR sequence Well-marked late gadolinium enhancement of peripheral ring of the mass Open in a new tab Case 2 demonstrates how abscess formation in the mitral annulus in patients with a history of recurrent infections and more severe infections of the pacing pocket after implantation are possible. Case 3 illustrates a clinical investigation of a mass in the mitral annulus complicated by the coexistence of a lung tumor, raising concerns of malignancy. These cases were intentionally selected to help illustrate how complex this diagnosis to rule in CCMA can be and how various techniques and diagnostic modalities can be utilized, considering the unique characteristics of each case. This is a cumbersome but essential part of the diagnostic workup to ensure that more severe conditions are not erroneously excluded. Because of these underlying nuances that make each case of CCMA unique, it is impossible to propose standardized guidelines or to suggest a criterion standard approach, due to the need to tailor-make the workup on a case-by-case basis. As highlighted by case 2, there can be cases in which imaging studies provide an ambiguous conclusion, and this is where clinical experience and reasoning help supplement limitations to these modalities. Many medical systems worldwide are starting to incorporate a multi-disciplinary consilium of specialists in order to have various perspectives to help expedite the diagnostic process without making premature conclusions. Despite the customizable diagnostic workup that may ultimately be needed, as authors of this case series, we advocate for the prioritization of excluding life-threatening possibilities, such as abscesses and neoplasms that can often mimic typical CCMA cases, in order to make sure that adequate treatment can begin as quickly as possible, if needed. Once these possibilities are excluded, a more intentional but targeted approach can be used to confirm the presence of CCMA, considering the benign nature of this condition in most cases. We advocate for more in-depth investigations in the future to determine if there are diagnostic modalities that have greater specificity and sensitivity, to help ascertain the diagnosis of CCMA using evidence-based medicine. Perhaps with further analysis, a more generalized approach to the diagnosis of CCMA would be possible in the future. Conclusions Multiple imaging modalities are often but not always necessary to make the diagnosis of CCAM. The use of TTE, TEE, CT, cardiac magnetic resonance imaging, and PET scanning can help differentiate CCMA from more significant pathologies. The incorporation of multi-disciplinary teams can improve the diagnostic process, which ultimately improves the therapeutic decisions dealing with incidental opacities found on the mitral valve. Footnotes Publisher’s note: All claims expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of their affiliated organizations, or those of the publisher, the editors and the reviewers. Any product that may be evaluated in this article, or claim that may be made by its manufacturer, is not guaranteed or endorsed by the publisher Department and Institution Where Work Was Done 1 st Department of Cardiology, Interventional Electrocardiology and Hypertension, Institute of Cardiology, Jagiellonian University Medical College, Cracow, Poland. Declaration of Figures’ Authenticity All figures submitted have been created by the authors who confirm that the images are original with no duplication and have not been previously published in whole or in part. References: 1. Sahasakul Y, Edwards WD, Naessens JM, Tajik AJ. Age-related changes in aortic and mitral valve thickness: Implications for two-dimensional echocardiography based on an autopsy study of 200 normal human hearts. Am J Cardiol. 1988;62(7):424–30. doi: 10.1016/0002-9149(88)90971-x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Agrawal T, Nagueh SF. Changes in cardiac structure and function with aging. J Cardiovasc Aging. 2022;2(1):13. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Massera D, Kizer JR, Dweck MR. Mechanisms of mitral annular calcification. Trends Cardiovasc Med. 2020;30(5):289–95. doi: 10.1016/j.tcm.2019.07.011. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Nakanishi K, Daimon M. Aging and myocardial strain. J Med Ultrason (2001) 2022;49(1):53–60. doi: 10.1007/s10396-021-01115-0. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Elgendy IY, Conti CR. Caseous calcification of the mitral annulus: A review. Clin Cardiol. 2013;36(10):E27–31. doi: 10.1002/clc.22199. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Mallisho M, Hwang I, Alsafwah SF. Liquefaction necrosis of mitral annulus calcification. J Clin Ultrasound. 2014;42(6):382–83. doi: 10.1002/jcu.22115. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Islam AKMM. Cardiac myxomas: A narrative review. World J Cardiol. 2022;14(4):206–19. doi: 10.4330/wjc.v14.i4.206. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Senel M, Schlensak C, Gawaz MP, et al. Advantages of multimodal imaging detecting the rare case of fungal abscess. JACC Case Rep. 2022;6:101694. doi: 10.1016/j.jaccas.2022.101694. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Srivatsa SS, Taylor MD, Hor K, et al. Liquefaction necrosis of mitral annular calcification (LNMAC): Review of pathology, prevalence, imaging and management: proposed diagnostic imaging criteria with detailed multi-modality and MRI image characterization. Int J Cardiovasc Imaging. 2012;28(5):1161–71. doi: 10.1007/s10554-011-9935-x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',40,'2025-10-15 10:17:47','2025-10-15 10:17:47');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4866,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7319003','The potential of the estimands framework for clinical pharmacology trials: Some discussion points.','Ring A, Wolfsegger MJ.','2020','petavy vomited lunchtime dafter shades gluc unblinding criticised bioanalysis mcar reml ols disallowed swapping noncompliance predose mcar incurrent estimators mcar nca nca reml ols antilog nca noncompartmental osl reml favours nonequivalence noncompleters distort modellers sharpen worded vaguely unambiguously medac tribanek wolfsegger principles_en petavy guizzaro antunes teerenstra roes mallinckrodt estimators akacha kothny permutt rufibach pst julious debarnot cpmp ewp qwp ndas inds schall endrenyi cpmp ewp cpmp morland durkalski frueh jts noncompliance','Abstract The recently finalised and published guideline ICH E9 (R1) introduced a new framework for the statistical analysis of clinical trials, namely that of “estimands”. While the framework was originally developed for the analysis of late‐phase trials, it could also provide a rigorous basis for the analysis of clinical pharmacology trials. We illustrate potential applications on two examples: a multiple dose pharmacology trial and the interpretation of confirmatory bioequivalence (BE) trials according to the current FDA and EMA BE guidelines. Keywords: clinical pharmacology, clinical trials, drug development, statistical trial design 1. INTRODUCTION The clinical trial community is currently discussing the impact of the addendum to the ICH E9 (R1) guideline. 1 This update introduces a new framework for the statistical analysis of clinical trials – the estimand. The aim of the estimands framework is to translate the trial objectives into a precise definition of the treatment effect that is to be estimated. A particular focus of the new guideline is on “intercurrent events”, which are defined as “Events that occur after treatment initiation and either preclude observation of the variable or affect its interpretation”. The estimands framework has primarily been developed for confirmatory trials, and most examples that explain the framework are based on efficacy evaluations. 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 The outcomes of these trials are typically interpreted by various stakeholders, such as the sponsor, regulatory authorities and healthcare authorities, and these stakeholders have different roles and responsibilities. This diversity of stakeholders implies that different estimands may have to be considered, but a primary estimand should be always defined. In this paper we attempt to illustrate the application of the estimand framework to pharmacological trials. Using two examples, an early clinical pharmacology trial in patients and a confirmatory BE trial, we follow the steps outlined in guideline ICH E9 (R1): specify the research question (objective), the population targeted by the clinical question, the treatment of interest, the variable (endpoint) of interest, the population‐level summary for the variable and the specification of how to account for intercurrent events to reflect the scientific question (Figure 1 ). Our focus will be to explain how to account for typical intercurrent events in clinical pharmacology trials within the estimands framework. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Components of the estimands as defined in the ICH E9 (R1) guideline 2. THE ESTIMANDS FRAMEWORK Pétavy et al 2 have outlined the general structure and application of the estimands framework. The key idea of the framework is that the analysis and interpretation of the trial data strictly follow a sequence that starts with the full clarification of the trial objective (Figure 1 ). The guideline 1 defines the estimand as “the target of estimation to address the scientific question of interest posed by the trial objective. Attributes of an estimand include the population of interest, the variable (or endpoint) of interest, the specification of how intercurrent events are reflected in the scientific question of interest, and the population‐level summary for the variable.” Although the trial objectives are indeed the central point of any trial protocol, different functions (eg sponsor physicians, investigators or statisticians) might have different attitudes on how these objectives can be met. Therefore, the trial objectives “should be translated into key scientific questions of interest by defining suitable estimands”. 3. EXAMPLE: MULTIPLE‐DOSE PHASE IIA TRIAL To illustrate the considerations when defining appropriate estimands, we use as an example the multiple‐dose titration trial (once daily over 28 days) in patients with metabolic disease described in Box 1. A new drug molecule had been investigated earlier in a single‐dose healthy subjects trial, when the terminal pharmacokinetic (PK) half‐life was estimated to be around 6 hours. The general objective of the new trial is to assess the safety, PK and pharmacodynamics of the drug following multiple‐dose administration by estimating endpoints related to the objective. Box 1: Design of a hypothetical Phase IIa trial in metabolic disease. Four‐week trial of active vs placebo with weekly dose titration. Forced titration: when possible, start a new dose level. When experiencing adverse events, return to a lower dose. PK profile measurements at days 1, 7, 14, 21 and 28. Three meals per day, breakfast 1 hour after dose administration. Glucose measurements in the early morning and before and after each meal. Various intercurrent events could occur in such a clinical pharmacological trial. We provide some examples and discuss their potential impact on the trial endpoints in order to meet the trial objectives. An overview of these events is provided in Figure 2 . A subject experienced a syncope before dinner on day 21 and therefore omitted the meal. The dose was not increased but the subject continued until day 28 and repeated the measurements. A subject vomited shortly after breakfast at day 14. Consequently, the dose was not increased on day 15. As no further adverse events were observed, the dose was increased at day 22 to the third dose level. All PK and glucose measurements were performed except for those close to the event on day 14. A subject experienced mild vomiting at lunchtime (about 5–6 hours drug after intake) on the second dose level between days 8 and 14, and was discontinued dafter day 14. A subject withdrew from the trial after drug intake at day 4 because of repeated dizziness and feeling unwell. However, the subject remained at the study site until day 7 for follow‐up and all PK and glucose measurements were performed on day 7. A subject temporarily discontinued the trial medication on days 11 and 12 due to adverse events, but continued medication on day 13 and completed the trial as planned. A subject received an analgesic drug because of severe headache at day 15, 1 hour after drug intake, and on day 20, 30 minutes after study drug intake. The dose was not increased on day 22, but the current dose was maintained until day 28. A subject did not take the day 4 dose in the morning. When this was detected, the dose was given in the evening. Many of the events above would currently be considered as “lack of adherence” or “protocol deviations” according to ICH E3. A so‐called “per‐protocol” population would be created, which would exclude subjects with such events (or data collected for those subjects after these events). The level of detail available on intercurrent events in clinical pharmacology trials (often performed in a controlled environment) is generally higher than in later phases. Figure 2. Open in a new tab The schedule of a hypothetical 4‐week Phase‐IIa trial (upper part) and potential intercurrent events (lower part). The different shades of blue indicate the dose level in each period. Cases A–G are discussed in the text, D–Level, Dose Level; Gluc, Glucose Measurement; Meas, Measurements; Rand, Randomisation Vomiting during the absorption phase, in particular, would be a typical reason for exclusion of PK data. Although vomiting is not a protocol violation (but an adverse event), it might affect the amount of drug absorbed and could have an impact on the measurements (eg PK or glucose concentrations); since the intention of the protocol was not followed, the intended drug exposure was not achieved. Even before the publication of guideline ICH E9 (R1) the handling of such events was usually discussed in the statistical analysis plan of the trial, although often not in the manner required by the new guideline. From the viewpoint of the estimands framework, it is important to clarify the key trial objectives first, in more detail than just the broad statement given above. In early clinical pharmacological trials, the primary objective is often to investigate the pharmacodynamic (PD) drug effect, eg the analysis variable could be an average of the glucose concentration, for each day with glucose measurements. Another objective of the trial could be the investigation of PK dose proportionality at steady state. Both these objectives could be a basis for the specification of primary estimands. However, other objectives could also be relevant for the interpretation of the data of this trial. For example, the estimation of the effect of treatment on glucose levels and on the PK steady state despite having “minor” events could be of practical interest for subsequent trials in larger patient groups, so that a secondary estimand could be defined. We could also consider a specific PK PD modelling estimand, as well as a within‐subject comparison of PK and glucose data between days 1 and 7. Following the schema in Figure 1 , the population of interest should be specified. For trials that are performed in healthy volunteers, this specification might be somewhat difficult, as the treated subjects are not necessarily a subset of the intended patient population. Instead, the study subjects serve as a proxy for future patients, and it might be an option to handle and interpret their intercurrent events aligned with analysis objectives that are relevant for future patients. In the case of the example Phase IIa trial, patients are members of the intended population; however the inclusion and exclusion criteria for such a trial are often restrictive due to the relatively low level of accumulated knowledge on the drug. In many cases, the so‐called “principal stratum strategy” of the new guideline could be applied. This strategy classifies the subjects according to the potential occurrence of an intercurrent event on all treatments, so that both strata – the one in which the event occurred and the one without that event – lead to valuable information on the drug properties in future patients. In some cases, a principal stratum estimand could be approximated by a well‐formulated per‐protocol analysis. The stratum without that event might be similar to “per‐protocol sets” and their current use in clinical pharmacology trials. However, not only the observed intercurrent events are of importance for defining the principal strata, but also “subjects who would experience the event” (ICH E9 R1) in either of the treatments groups, and the relevance of this consideration for the clinical questions needs to be clarified. In other cases, the “hypothetical strategy” might best relate to the trial objectives: The value of the variable to reflect the clinical question of interest is the value which the variable would have taken in the hypothetical scenario in which the intercurrent event would not have occurred. Hence, it would be required to assess the impact of the intercurrent event on the endpoint, and we would like to indicate some thoughts for the example Phase IIa trial. 4. EXAMPLE: POSSIBLE HANDLING OF THE INTERCURRENT EVENTS The events presented above could lead to the following handling within the primary and secondary estimands of the trial. We assume that the primary PD objective is the determination of the PD drug effect of the drug on the blood glucose levels, while the determination of PK dose proportionality is the primary PK objective. Event A (syncope and dose maintenance): The glucose measurement in the evening is different from those of the other patients at this time point because no meal has been taken. Hence the primary glucose estimand would remove this data point, while the secondary glucose estimand would not (as a representative of patients who might eat more or less). The primary PK estimand should not be affected by the missing meal, while the PD estimand is probably affected. As the dose was maintained for another week, it would be possible to replace the measurements on day 21 with those from day 28 (hypothetical strategy). For event B (vomiting and dose maintenance), considerations might involve the following: There was probably little absorption of the drug at day 14, so PK measurements on this day should be omitted for the primary PK estimand. Whether there would an impact on the glucose measurements would need to be discussed, based on the expected lasting effects of the drug on the metabolic system. As the dose increase was delayed by one week, the data at day 21 could replace the data at day 14 of the other patients for the primary estimand (similar for day 28 data after dose increase). The considerations for events C (several occasions of vomiting) could be that the drug absorption might have been completed, while the glucose measurements after lunch would be affected and would only be included in the secondary, but not in the primary, glucose estimand. In contrast, event D (withdrawal after day 4) might have a major impact on the outcome and hence both primary estimands would not use the data after the withdrawal. Whether the data at day 7 would be considered relevant for the secondary estimands would need to be discussed. Event E (temporary discontinuation) would lead to an expected impact on the attainment of steady state at day 14, hence the primary PK estimand would be affected. Again, the impact on the glucose estimand needs to be discussed based on the drug properties. For event F (symptomatic treatment), the potential for drug–drug interaction could be considered when evaluating the day 21 data. However, it might also be possible to replace these data with those from day 28 as the dose was not increased in the fourth period. Event G (delay of a dose administration) might be considered minor because it was long before day 7, so all data would be used for the primary estimands. All of these considerations must be documented before the data base is locked, before unblinding, if applicable, and before the analysis of the data. The decisions should be made without knowledge of the treatment of the patients. In the past, some sponsors have defined a large number of “per‐protocol sets”, one for each relevant objective (eg PK, PD, biomarkers etc). This was criticised by others, who prefer the stricter late‐phase approach of defining no more than two or three analysis populations for all trial analyses. The estimands framework clarifies that the data have to be considered strictly with the analysis objective in mind. For each objective, the appropriate analysis strategy has to be chosen and the data have to be selected that meet the objective. While imputation of other observed data could often be an option (eg when there was no dose titration, as for events A, B and F), statistical imputation of missing data might be less relevant in clinical pharmacological trials. The above considerations have focussed on the selection of data for each analysis. The guideline proposes several analysis strategies, like the “while on treatment strategy” or the “principal stratum” strategy. 1 These strategies have been primarily developed for late‐phase trials, and none of them might fully cover the intentions and objectives of clinical pharmacology trials. However, based on the considerations above, appropriate analysis strategies can be defined to meet the objectives of investigating the PK and PD drug properties in order to inform and prepare the design of the subsequent clinical development of the drug. For completeness, the population level summary for the glucose endpoints would be the mean (and its confidence interval) over all subjects on each dose level, adjusted for baseline glucose concentration, while for the PK endpoints it would be generally the adjusted (or raw) geometric mean, due to the log‐normal distribution of these endpoints. 7 5. EXAMPLE: BE TRIAL In this section we discuss how the current analyses of BE trials could be interpreted from the viewpoint of the estimands framework. BE trials are often confirmatory trials, and their conduct and analyses are highly regulated. While the regional requirements for BE trials are generally similar, there are some important differences (eg with regard to handling of missing values and protocol deviations) that could be interpreted in terms of the handling of intercurrent events, based on different estimands. The following discussion is based on the most frequent design of a BE trial, a single‐dose AB/BA crossover trial, with subjects being allocated at random to receive in a first period either treatment A followed (after a suitable wash‐out) by treatment B in a second period, or treatment B followed by treatment A. Ideally, the BE evaluation should be performed on all treated subjects; excluding subjects from statistical analysis is permissible provided the potential reasons for doing so are specified in the protocol and the decision to exclude is made before bioanalysis. 8 Intuitively, intercurrent events (to use the language of the estimand framework) that could lead to the exclusion of subjects from analysis are events which might affect the reliability of concentration–time profiles and thus impact the comparison of PK metrics C max and AUC between test and reference formulation. Such events typically include nonadherence to the inclusion or exclusion criteria, the intake of concomitant medication during the PK sampling period, or the premature withdrawal from the trial. In addition, protocols often define reasons for excluding individual data points from the analysis. Intercurrent events would comprise all of the above examples, and the estimands define strategies to account for these events. When using the estimands framework, the first step is a clear and detailed wording of the question of interest, ie the trial objective. The primary objective of BE trials according to the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and European Medical Agency (EMA) BE guidelines 8 , 9 is to “demonstrate that rate and extent of absorption of the drug is equivalent between two formulations/drug products”. However, as we interpret the respective guidelines, the EMA and FDA guidelines differ regarding the handling of intercurrent events affecting a single treatment period in the following sense: Based on the FDA guideline, only data of the treatment period when the event occurs is to be excluded from the analysis, whereas the EMA guideline requires removal of data of both periods (ie remove the complete subject) from the analysis. This difference could be interpreted to reflect different assumptions about the mechanism for how the data had been impacted by the intercurrent event. The various strategies for removal of data from analysis raise questions about missing at random (MAR) or missing completely at random (MCAR) assumptions, respectively. Of note, these different assumptions about potential missing data mechanisms are reflected in the corresponding statistical analysis models when specifying “subject” as a random or fixed effect for FDA and EMA, respectively. The random effect analysis estimates the statistics using restricted maximum likelihood (REML) methods using all available data, while the fixed effect analysis leads to a complete case analysis (removal of subjects with incomplete data) based on the ordinary least squares (OLS) estimator for the statistic of interest. The ICH E9 (R1) guideline proposes several analysis strategies to meet the trial objectives. It appears that two of them might cover the objectives of the two BE guidelines. The first strategy is the “principal stratum strategy”, which assesses the effect of the randomised treatment in the stratum of trial subjects who follow the intention of the protocol as closely as possible. Table 2 indicates that this strategy might be the basis for the current EMA BE guideline, as this guideline requires analysing only those subjects who have followed the protocol closely. In particular, it would require removing subjects from the analysis who only completed one of the crossover periods. Table 2. Handling of intercurrent events as suggested by the FDA and the EMA bioequivalence guidelines Intercurrent events and their handling in BE analyses FDA BE guideline EMA BE guideline Estimand Difference in rate and extent of absorption between test and reference formulation based on the missing at random premise Difference in rate and extent of absorption between test and reference formulation based on the completely missing at random premise Events affecting both treatment periods: • Violation of major inclusion/exclusion criteria (eg disallowed medical conditions) • No informed consent Removal of subjects with events from analysis Removal of subjects with events from analysis Events affecting both treatment periods: • Nonadherence to randomisation (eg unintentional swapping the treatment order) Inclusion of subjects with events based on actual treatment order Removal of subjects with events from analysis Events affecting single treatment periods: • Drop‐out after first period • Noncompliance with protocol (eg smoking, intake of prohibited medication) • Insufficient washout (eg predose concentration > 5% C max ) • Vomiting prior to two times T max • Other events that could impact the reliability of the concentration profile (eg concentration not available at several time points; demonstrated data outlier, confirmed by independent observations 10 ) Removal of data of the treatment period with events from the analysis a Removal of subjects with events from analysis b Open in a new tab a Relates to the MAR premise where available data are analysed via a mixed effects model. b Relates to the MCAR premise where completed cases are analysed via a fixed effects model. Hereby we suppose that there would be no reason to assume that the intercurrent events would be differential between the test and reference treatment, as the aim of the pharmaceutical development would be to mimic the PK profile as much as possible. Alternatively, there is the so‐called “hypothetical strategy”, which might be the intention of the FDA BE guideline, as it would include subjects who only completed one period, so that the treatment effect is evaluated in the hypothetical setting that the subject had completed the trial (ie as if the intercurrent events had not have occurred). Examples for handling of incurrent events based on the different analysis strategies motivated by the two different estimands derived from the EMA and FDA guidelines are given in Table 2 . Table 2 classifies intercurrent events into two main groups: (a) events affecting the outcome of both treatment periods of the crossover trial and (b) events affecting a single period. The majority of these events only affect a single period, and these events lead to different evaluations according to FDA and EMA requirements. While the fixed model approach as requested by the EMA BE guideline removes data for subjects with such events completely from the analysis, the mixed model analysis according to the FDA accounts implicitly for the missingness of the data of one period. For this reason we suggest that the analyses based on the FDA and EMA guideline, respectively, could be associated with two different estimands. Of note, events affecting individual measurements randomly (such as unavailable PK blood concentration, eg due to haemolytic blood samples or missing blood drawing) are not called intercurrent events according to the ICH E9 (R1) guideline because such random events generally do not bias the estimators. Nevertheless, a blinded data review would be preferable to document that the reliability of the corresponding concentration time profile is not impacted by such missing individual measurements. In the past, sensitivity analyses were used in assessing the robustness of the findings or conclusions based on primary analyses of data, such as excluding AUC 0‐∞ values for which the extrapolated area accounted for more than 20% of the total area. According to our interpretation of the ICH E9 (R1) guideline, such an analysis would no longer be considered as a sensitivity analysis but would refer to a different estimand. It should be emphasized that the BE guidelines 8 , 9 were published prior to the development of the estimands framework. Hence the information in Tables 1 and 2 is hypothetical, based on our interpretation of the guidelines. We would welcome a discussion on appropriateness of these interpretations. It would indeed be appreciated by the trial community if the BE guidelines would be updated with respect to the new ICH E9 (R1) guideline, by clearly outlining the regulatory objectives and proposing appropriate estimands. Table 1. General concepts of the FDA and the EMA BE guidelines when interpreted using the estimands framework of the guideline ICH E9 (R1) General concept FDA BE guideline EMA BE guideline Trial objective Demonstrate that rate and extent of absorption of the drug is equivalent between two formulations/drug products Demonstrate that rate and extend of absorption of the drug is equivalent between two formulations/drug products Estimand Difference in rate and extent of absorption between test and reference formulation based on the missing at random (MAR) premise Difference in rate and extent of absorption between test and reference formulation based on the missing completely at random (MCAR) premise Main analysis strategy Hypothetical strategy Principal stratum strategy Population of interest Population for which the reference formulation/drug product is approved Population for which the reference formulation/drug product is approved Analysis variables C max , AUC inf and AUC t derived using NCA, as surrogates for rate and extend of absorption, based on reliable concentration profiles C max and AUC t derived using NCA, as surrogates for rate and extend of absorption, based on reliable concentration profiles Population‐level summary Ratio of variable means between formulations, estimated using a mixed effect analysis with log‐transformed variables Ratio of variable means between formulations, estimated using a fixed effect analysis with log‐transformed variables Estimator The REML a estimator for the difference in means and corresponding two‐sided 90% CI limits in log‐transformed variables assuming normally distributed errors The OLS b estimator for the difference in means and corresponding two‐sided 90% CI limits in log‐transformed variables assuming normally distributed errors Estimate Antilog of the point estimate and two‐sided 90% confidence interval limits calculated from data based on the estimator Open in a new tab Abbreviation: C max , maximum concentration following study drug administration; CI, confidence interval; AUC t , area under the concentration time curve from 0 to the last quantifiable concentration; AUC inf , total area under the concentration versus time curve extrapolated to infinity; NCA, noncompartmental approach; OSL, ordinary least squares; REML, restricted maximum likelihood. a Typically, a linear mixed effects model is used (eg SAS proc MIXED). b Typically, a linear fixed effects model is used (eg SAS proc GLM). 6. DISCUSSION The revision of the ICH E9 guideline has been primarily developed for confirmatory late‐phase trials. However, its framework could be applicable to clinical pharmacology trials as well. 3 While the estimand framework might seem to be difficult at a first glance, it has the potential to overcome gaps between the trial objective and the interpretation of the analyses by clarifying the assumptions behind all components of the data analysis. Therefore, the estimand framework supports the interpretation of results obtained from different studies. The estimand framework also allows one to address the respective roles of the per‐protocol and full analysis sets in (bio)equivalence studies: one can construct corresponding estimands to directly address those intercurrent events which can lead to the attenuation of differences between treatment arms since, in contrast to a superiority trial, the full analysis and the per‐protocol analysis set in a noninferiority or (bio)equivalence are equally important. 11 The full analysis set may be biased towards demonstrating equivalence 12 as a dilution of the treatment effect favours the null‐hypothesis of nonequivalence. However, the per‐protocol set can potentially also be biased if, for example, noncompleters are not included in it, which can distort the reported effectiveness of a treatment. 13 For completeness we would like to mention that some estimands have already been defined for BE trials by Lou. 14 , 15 However, these proposals were for PD evaluations in multiple‐dose biosimilar studies, which require different concepts compared to standard BE trials. The details of any application of the estimands framework to a Phase IIa trial and to BE trials, as presented here, need to be discussed further as usually there will be various options to define primary and secondary estimands based on the trial objectives. To raise these discussion points might be one of the main achievements of guideline ICH E9 (R1), as it might further encourage clinicians, pharmacologists, statisticians and modellers to obtain the most relevant information from the trials for the drug development process. Furthermore, this guideline is expected to sharpen trial objectives in clinical pharmacology trials, which are often worded rather vaguely and in a generalised way. One such objective is the “characterization of PK”, which is typically addressed by presenting only descriptive statistics for PK metrics. However, a comprehensive “characterization of PK” might require more information, including a formal evaluation of dose proportionality after a single dose or at steady state, as well as assessment of PK linearity after repeated administration. 7. CONCLUSION The ICH E9 (R1) guideline has the potential to increase the quality of clinical pharmacological studies as this framework requires a clear definition of the terms and steps of the statistical analysis. The two examples presented here show that the estimands framework can clarify relevant details on how different study objectives can be unambiguously addressed and interpreted by choosing the appropriate components of the analyses for clinical pharmacology trials. COMPETING INTERESTS M.J.W. is an employee of Takeda and owns Takeda stock/shares. AR is an employee of medac. DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT Data sharing is not applicable to this article as no data were created or analysed in this study. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS The authors would like to thank Werner Engl and Michael Tribanek for the very helpful discussions on this topic. We would thank the reviewers for their thoughtful comments. Ring A, Wolfsegger MJ. The potential of the estimands framework for clinical pharmacology trials: Some discussion points. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2020;86:1240–1247. 10.1111/bcp.14233 REFERENCES 1. ICH E9 (R1) . Addendum on estimands and sensitivity analysis in clinical trials – to the guideline on statistical principles for clinical trials. Step 5. Available at: https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/ich-e9-r1-addendum-estimands-sensitivity-analysis-clinical-trials-guideline-statistical-principles_en.pdf 2. Petavy F, Guizzaro L, Antunes dos Reis I, Teerenstra S, Roes K. Beyond “Intent‐to‐treat” and “Per protocol”: Improving assessment of treatment effects in clinical trials through the specification of an estimand. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2020. 10.1111/bcp.14195. In print. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Mallinckrodt CH, Bell J, Liu G, et al. Aligning estimators with Estimands in clinical trials: putting the ICH E9(R1) guidelines into practice. Ther Innov Regul Sci. 2020. 10.1177/2168479019836979. In print. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Akacha M, Kothny W. Estimands: a more strategic approach to study design and analysis. Clin Pharmacol Ther. 2017;102(6):894‐896. 10.1002/cpt.872 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Permutt T. Defining treatment effects: a regulatory perspective. Clin Trials. 2019;16(4):345‐349. 10.1177/1740774519830358 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Rufibach K. Treatment effect quantification for time‐to‐event endpoints – Estimands, analysis strategies, and beyond. Pharm Stat. 2019;18(2):145‐165. 10.1002/pst.1917 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Julious SA, Debarnot CA. Why are pharmacokinetic data summarized by arithmetic means? J Biopharm Stat. 2000;10(1):55‐71. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. European Medicines Agency . Guideline on the investigation of bioequivalence. 2010. Doc. Ref. CPMP/EWP/QWP/1401/98 Rev. 1. 9. Food and Drug Administration . Bioavailability and Bioequivalence Studies Submitted in NDAs or INDs – General Considerations. Draft guidance 2014. 10. Schall R, Endrenyi L, Ring A. Residuals and outliers in replicate design crossover studies. J Biopharm Stat. 2010;20(4):835‐849. 10.1080/10543401003618876 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. European Medicines Agency . Points to consider on switching between superiority and non‐inferiority. 2000. Doc. Ref. CPMP/EWP/482/99. 12. ICH E9 . Statistical Principles for Clinical Trials. 1998. EMA/CPMP/ICH/363/96. 13. Greene CJ, Morland LA, Durkalski VL, Frueh BC. Noninferiority and equivalence designs: issues and implications for mental health research. J Trauma Stress. 2008;21(5):433‐439. 10.1002/jts.20367 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Lou Y, Jones MP, Sun W. Estimation of causal effects in clinical endpoint bioequivalence studies in the presence of intercurrent events: noncompliance and missing data. J Biopharm Stat. 2019;29(1):151‐173. 10.1080/10543406.2018.1489408 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Lou Y, Jones MP, Sun W. Assessing the ratio of means as a causal estimand in clinical endpoint bioequivalence studies in the presence of intercurrent events. Stat Med. 2019;38(27):5214‐5235. 10.1002/sim.8367 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Data sharing is not applicable to this article as no data were created or analysed in this study.',11,'2025-10-15 10:17:49','2025-10-15 10:17:49');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4867,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1285395','''Hunt the thimble'': a study of the radiology of ingested foreign bodies.','Boothroyd AE, Carty HM, Robson WJ.','1987','swallowed halved potsic unsuspected nidus blazer naveh capitanio swallowed batteries gershel shelov ziprkowski nejm198308113090603 pyman fowkes ennis shabino jacep s0361 lacey wignall bradbrooke reidy rationalising s0009 lacey guilding wignall reidy bradbrook s0009','Abstract The role of radiology in the management of patients with a history of ingested foreign bodies is reviewed in a retrospective study over a 2-year period in a Children''s Hospital. If radiography was confined to those children who have a clinical history suggestive of foreign body impaction in the oesophagus, a foreign body aspiration or having swallowed a button battery, the work load and unnecessary radiation arising from this practice could be halved. This study demonstrates another misuse of radiological services. Full text PDF Previous 33 34 35 36 37 38 Next Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Banks W., Potsic W. P. Elusive unsuspected foreign bodies in the tracheobronchial tree. A nidus for infections that may be quiet for long periods of time. Clin Pediatr (Phila) 1977 Jan;16(1):31–35. doi: 10.1177/000992287701600105. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Blazer S., Naveh Y., Friedman A. Foreign body in the airway. A review of 200 cases. Am J Dis Child. 1980 Jan;134(1):68–71. doi: 10.1001/archpedi.1980.02130130050015. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Capitanio M. A., Kirkpatrick J. A. The lateral decubitus film. An aid in determining air-trapping in children. Radiology. 1972 May;103(2):460–462. doi: 10.1148/103.2.460. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] D''Sa Barros E. A., D''Sa Barros A. A. Mercury battery ingestion. Br Med J. 1979 May 5;1(6172):1218–1218. doi: 10.1136/bmj.1.6172.1218-a. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] David T. J., Ferguson A. P. Management of children who have swallowed button batteries. Arch Dis Child. 1986 Apr;61(4):321–322. doi: 10.1136/adc.61.4.321. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Eyes B., Evans A. F. Post-traumatic skull radiographs. Time for a reappraisal. Lancet. 1978 Jul 8;2(8080):85–86. doi: 10.1016/s0140-6736(78)91395-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Gershel J. C., Goldman H. S., Stein R. E., Shelov S. P., Ziprkowski M. The usefulness of chest radiographs in first asthma attacks. N Engl J Med. 1983 Aug 11;309(6):336–339. doi: 10.1056/NEJM198308113090603. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Pyman C. Inhaled foreign bodies in childhood. A review of 230 cases. Med J Aust. 1971 Jan 9;1(2):62–68. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Roberts C. J., Fowkes F. G., Ennis W. P., Mitchell M. Possible impact of audit on chest X-ray requests from surgical wards. Lancet. 1983 Aug 20;2(8347):446–448. doi: 10.1016/s0140-6736(83)90403-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Shabino C. L., Feinberg A. N. Esophageal perforation secondary to alkaline battery ingestion. JACEP. 1979 Sep;8(9):360–363. doi: 10.1016/s0361-1124(79)80259-2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] de Lacey G. J., Wignall B. K., Bradbrooke S., Reidy J., Hussain S., Cramer B. Rationalising abdominal radiography in the accident and emergency department. Clin Radiol. 1980 Jul;31(4):453–455. doi: 10.1016/s0009-9260(80)80191-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] de Lacey G., Guilding A., Wignall B., Reidy J., Bradbrook S. Mild head injuries: a source of excessive radiography? (analysis of a series and review of the literature). Clin Radiol. 1980 Jul;31(4):457–462. doi: 10.1016/s0009-9260(80)80194-2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',4,'2025-10-15 10:17:50','2025-10-15 10:17:50');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4868,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3550166','Toxico-surveillance of infant and toddler poisonings in the United States.','Finkelstein Y, Hutson JR, Wax PM, Brent J; Toxicology Investigators Consortium (ToxIC) Case Registry.','2012','toddler toxico toddler psychotropics narcotic toxico toddler aapcc pccs pursuant narcotic stings aapcc aapcc narcotic stings aapcc toddler helpless narcotic maltreatment pccs toxico aapcc neiss aipo aapcc toxico aapcc sevenfold pccs aapcc appcc toys aapcc publicized intoxications toxico toxico blister bronstein spyker cantilena lrj rumack giffin 27th kleinschmidt polivka casavant nontoxic s10900 setlik chafee bahamon lovejoy fhj waring aspis fountain reith herbison s1386 azkunaga mintegi bizkarra mej 0b013e3283462504 agzikuru comert atilkan erdag telatar','Abstract Infant and toddler poisonings are important to capture and may be challenging to manage. We aim to describe the Toxicology Investigators Consortium (ToxIC) Case Registry as a tool for toxico-surveillance of this problem in the United States. Using the ToxIC Case Registry database of the American College of Medical Toxicology, we identified infant and toddler poisonings over a 15-month period between April 1, 2010 and June 30, 2011 reported to the 31 Registry sites. Of 6,810 poisoning cases reported to the ToxIC registry, 248 (3.6 %) involved children younger than 2 years (51 % males). Fifty-four percent were hospital inpatients, 42 % were in the Emergency Department and 4 % were outpatients. Sixty-three percent were symptomatic. The most common ingested compounds were highly toxic-cardiac drugs (16 %), psychotropics (15 %), recreational drugs, alcohols, and controlled narcotic drugs (13 %), analgesics (9 %), and cleaning compounds (7 %). Fourteen percent of cases involved multiple agents. The ToxIC registry is a potentially useful toxico-surveillance tool to identify and trend clinically significant poisonings in young children, and potentially other populations. These data could be used to target specific preventive interventions. Keywords: Poisoning, Infants, Toxicology, Case registry Introduction Infant and toddler poisonings are a particularly important public health concern, since toxicological adverse effects in this age group have greater potential ramifications in terms of person years-of-life than poisonings in adolescents and adults [ 1 ]. In 2009, the American Association of Poison Control Centers (AAPCC) reported over 1.6 million pediatric poison exposures and the incidence is increasing annually [ 2 ]. Of these, 534,236 (33.1 %) occurred in children younger than 2 years and approximately 90 % of them were managed at home. In the United States, 41,375 infants and toddlers visited emergency departments (ED) in 2004 for poisoning, corresponding to a poisoning rate of 2.2 and 8.0 per 1,000 children aged <1 year and 1 to 2 years, respectively [ 1 ]. Visits to the ED in children ≤5 years of age increased by 30 % between 2001 and 2008 in the USA and poisoning prevention efforts to date are inadequate [ 3 ]. Although estimates of possible pediatric poisonings of varying severity are reported in the above citations, there are sparse data about exposure patterns of confirmed consequential and serious poisonings in this age group. Data from the poison control centers (PCCs) suggest that the most common agents involved in these exposures are household cleaning products and analgesics, and the vast majority of which are managed without referral to a medical facility [ 2 ]. Thus, further observational data are required to identify which agents are responsible for the more serious exposures, resultant ED visits and hospitalizations in order to make progress in reducing childhood injury from poisoning [ 3 ]. We sought to analyze the poisonings in children less than 2 years old reported to the Toxicology Investigators Consortium (ToxIC) Case Registry over a 15-month period, in order to identify substances responsible for consequential exposures in this age group across the United States (US). Methods In 2010, the American College of Medical Toxicology (ACMT) established a prospective, nationwide toxicology database, The ToxIC Case Registry [ 4 ]. This registry is exclusively compiling cases that have been consulted and managed by a medical toxicologist at the bedside in 31 participating medical centers that have established medical toxicology consultation programs across the US. Using prospectively collected data from the ToxIC Case Registry, we identified all cases of poisoning in children up to 2 years, who have been consulted on at the bedside by a medical toxicologist between April 1, 2010 and June 30, 2011. Most of these sites are academic institutions, which collectively constitute the majority of medical toxicology services in the US. Participating sites prospectively enroll and report all patients that are cared for by the medical toxicology service at each center. Detailed demographic and clinical data are collected on each patient including, but not restricted to, age group, location of initial encounter with the patient, circumstances and reasons for exposure, substances involved, presence of clinical signs and symptoms, and treatments provided. Lack of an identified toxin served as an exclusion criteria. Sites contribute cases to the ToxIC Registry pursuant to approval by their Institutional Review Boards. Results Of 6,810 poisoning cases reported to ToxIC registry during the 15-month study period, 248 (3.6 %) involved children less than 2 years. Of them, the toxin was identified in 223 children (90 %), who form the study cohort. Gender was reported in 216 (110 were males; 51 %). Location of initial encounter was reported in 195 cases: 105 (54 %) were consulted while admitted to hospital, 82 (42 %) were consulted while still in the Emergency Department and 8 (4 %) had an outpatient consult. One hundred forty (63 %) children exhibited clinical signs and/or symptoms at the time of consultation. Table 1 summarizes the top seven exposure classes in our patient cohort. The most common groups of exposures were cardiovascular drugs ( n = 36; 16 %), psychotropic drugs ( n = 34; 15 %, mostly antipsychotics followed by antidepressants and anticonvulsants), recreational and controlled narcotic drugs ( n = 20; 9 %, mostly methadone), analgesics ( n = 19; 9 %), cleaning products ( n = 16; 7 %), scorpion stings ( n = 11; 5 %), and toxic alcohols ( n = 9; 4 %). Consultation for iron poisoning was requested for a single case only. Fourteen percent ( n = 32) of the children had histories of ingestion of multiple agents. Table 1. Most frequent infant exposures reported to the ToxIC Registry between April 1, 2010 and June 30, 2011 and the corresponding pediatric exposure rates reported to the American Association of Poison Control Centers (AAPCC) in 2009 [ 2 ] Agent Percentage of cases from ToxIC registry a Percentage of cases reported to AAPCC b Cardiac drugs 16 % 1.9 % Psychotropic drugs 15 % 2.3 % Recreational and controlled narcotic drugs 9 % NR Analgesics 9 % 9.7 % Cleaning products 7 % 9.3 % Scorpion stings 5 % NR Toxic alcohols 4 % 2.0 % Open in a new tab NR , not reported since not in the top 25 agents a For those <2 years of age b For those ≤5 years of age Discussion Poisoning in the first 2 years of life makes up 4 % of all cases consulted at the bedside by medical toxicologists participating in the ToxIC registry. Our data demonstrate that, contrary to expectations based from AAPCC data, cardiovascular and psychotropic medications were the drug classes responsible for a significant proportion of poisonings. The capturing of infant and toddler poisoning cases is especially important for several reasons. These children are vulnerable and helpless in protecting themselves from accidental or intentional environmental hazards, as well as communicating the events leading to injury. About 13 % of our patients were exposed to recreational drugs, alcohols, or controlled narcotic drugs. These data raise the concern of neglect, unsafe environment and child maltreatment. This is most relevant in first-year-of-life exposures, where purposeful motor and coordination skills required to access drugs are largely limited. In malicious pediatric poisonings, an age of less than 2 years is associated with life threatening symptoms, residual disability, and increased risk of death [ 5 ]. Such cases are unlikely to be voluntarily reported to PCCs by caregivers, and clinicians should have high index of suspicion for non-accidental poisonings when confronted with these scenarios. Thus, toxico-surveillance systems, such as the ToxIC registry, can play a potential role in identifying these cases as well as other exposure trends. Similar to our findings, a recent study suggested that the majority (>80 %) of infant poisonings that attended the ED were classified as “toxic”, having a significant potential for an adverse effect. Only a small proportion (19 %) of exposure cases presenting to the ED was reported to the regional PCC [ 6 ]. At the same time, capturing infant poisonings may be challenging. Epidemiological estimates from both the AAPCC data and from nationwide ED records via the National Electronic Injury Surveillance System–All Injury Program (NEISS–AIPO) are both believed to significantly underestimate the true magnitude of the problem [ 7 – 9 ]. In that respect, the ToxIC and AAPCC registries complement each other, providing an effective, real-time toxico-surveillance coverage in the United States. However, they differ in several aspects. First, AAPCC data rely on voluntary self-reporting. In children less than 5 years of age, there is an estimated sevenfold under-reporting of poison-related fatalities to PCCs and an even higher under-estimation of non-fatal cases [ 5 ]. Guardians of poisoned infants that attend the ED are not likely to have previously contacted a PCC [ 6 – 10 ]. The ToxIC case registry addresses that gap by documenting all inadvertent exposure presenting to participating sites and requiring bedside consultation. Second, there are major differences between the type of exposures reported to AAPCC and those captured by the ToxIC Case Registry (Table 1 ). In the APPCC database, the most common possible exposures for children younger than 5 years are to household items, such as cosmetics and personal care (13 %), cleaning products (9.3 %), and foreign bodies/toys (7 %), and the majority were managed at home without referral to a healthcare facility [ 2 ]. The most common category of pharmaceutical exposure was analgesics (9.7 %) and only a small percentage was related to prescription psychotropic medications (2.3 %; Table 1 ). Many of the AAPCC cases are reported as “possible” exposures, where ingestions were not witnessed by the infant’s guardian and have no analytical confirmation, and about 90 % are perceived mild enough to be managed at home [ 2 ]. In contrast, ToxIC registry patients typically represent serious exposures, where front-line clinicians feel the greatest concern and request toxicology expertise. The ToxIC data show a profile of poisonings dominated by classes of medications and compounds associated with potentially high morbidity. Our findings are corroborated by a recent study in children younger than 6 years, which investigated exposures to a single pharmaceutical agent. The authors found that exposure to prescription medications is on the rise, and concluded that prevention efforts to date are inadequate [ 3 ]. In recent years, awareness to the risks of poison or chemical exposure and bioterrorism has increased, and a number of highly publicized terrorist events have heightened community fears. This has led to the establishment of several mechanisms for population surveillance outside the US, such as the Alerting and Surveillance Using Poisons Information Systems [ 11 ] as well as other medical informatics platforms [ 12 ]. Recent attempts to explore patterns and magnitude of consequential pediatric poisonings have also been made outside North America: the Spanish Society of Paediatric Emergencies reported 91 poisonings in children younger than 5 years over 1 year through their Toxicology Surveillance System–Intoxications Working Group [ 13 ]. Of the 91 cases, 70 % were exposed to acetaminophen or cough and cold medications. Another recent retrospective study from Istanbul, Turkey reported on 330 poisoning hospitalizations in children up to 14 years [ 14 ]. The majority of exposures were to pharmaceutical agents (76 %). Of the 330 cases, 29 (9 %) were below 1 year of age, and all the latter were considered accidental. Of the 28 infants that had available data, 16 were exposed to pharmaceutical agents. The main limitation of the study stems from the fact that the ToxIC registry is a sentinel system in mostly academic tertiary care institutions and, therefore, may not be representative of the experience and practice in primary healthcare facilities. However, its strength is that it is intended to describe confirmed, clinically significant and important poisonings. In summary, using a novel toxico-surveillance tool, we identified substances that produce significant poisoning morbidity and mortality in infants and toddlers. The often-cited established toxico-surveillance [ 2 ] system describes potential rather than actual poisonings. These findings raise, once again, the issue of infant access to medications and the need to establish a safer environment, both on the personal level (e.g., open containers left at hand-reach) as well as the role of industry and regulatory agencies in ensuring patient safety by targeted efforts (e.g., child-resistant packaging). There may be a need for specific interventions targeted at the high-risk medications, for example, blister or other types of unit dose packaging. Clinicians should maintain high index of suspicion to the possibility of unusual exposures in infants and toddlers, such as illicit drugs and alcohol. Acknowledgments Financial Disclosure/Conflict of Interest The authors reported no financial disclosures relevant to this article. References 1. Franklin RL, Rodgers GB. Unintentional child poisonings treated in United States hospital emergency departments: national estimates of incident cases, population-based poisoning rates, and product involvement. Pediatrics. 2008;122(6):1244–1251. doi: 10.1542/peds.2007-3551. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Bronstein AC, Spyker DA, Cantilena LRJ, Green JL, Rumack BH, Giffin SL. Annual Report of the American Association of Poison Control Centers’ National Poison Data System (NPDS): 27th Annual Report. Clin Toxicol (Phila) 2009;48(10):979–1178. doi: 10.3109/15563650.2010.543906. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Bond GR, Woodward RW, Ho M. The growing impact of pediatric pharmaceutical poisoning. J Pediatr. 2012;160(2):265–270.el. doi: 10.1016/j.jpeds.2011.07.042. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Wax PM, Kleinschmidt KC, Brent J. The Toxicology Investigators Consortium (ToxIC) Registry. J Med Toxicol. 2011;7(4):259–265. doi: 10.1007/s13181-011-0177-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Yin S. Malicious use of pharmaceuticals in children. J Pediatr. 2010;157(5):832–836. doi: 10.1016/j.jpeds.2010.05.040. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Polivka BJ, Casavant M, Baker SD. Factors associated with healthcare visits by young children for nontoxic poisoning exposures. J Community Health. 2010;35(6):572–578. doi: 10.1007/s10900-010-9243-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Setlik J, Ho M, Bond GR. Emergency department use after pediatric pharmaceutical ingestion: comparison of two national databases. Clin Toxicol (Phila) 2010;48(1):64–67. doi: 10.3109/15563650903397234. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Blanc PD, Jones MR, Olson KR. Surveillance of poisoning and drug overdose through hospital discharge coding, poison control center reporting, and the Drug Abuse Warning Network. Am J Emerg Med. 1993;11(1):14–19. doi: 10.1016/0735-6757(93)90051-C. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Blanc PD, Kearney TE, Olson KR. Underreporting of fatal cases to a regional poison control center. West J Med. 1995;162(6):505–509. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Chafee-Bahamon C, Lovejoy FHJ. Effectiveness of a regional poison center in reducing excess emergency room visits for children’s poisonings. Pediatrics. 1983;72(2):164–169. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Waring WS, Palmer SR, Bateman DN. Alerting and Surveillance Using Poisons Information Systems (ASPIS): outcomes from an international working group. Clin Toxicol. 2007;45:543–548. doi: 10.1080/15563650701365842. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Watts M, Fountain J, Reith DM, Herbison P. Clinical utility of an electronic poisons information and clinical decision support tool. Int J Med Inform. 2003;71:3–8. doi: 10.1016/S1386-5056(03)00009-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Azkunaga B, Mintegi S, Bizkarra I, Fernandez J. Toxicology surveillance system of the Spanish Society of Paediatric Emergencies: first-year analysis. Eur J Emerg Med. 2011;18(5):285–287. doi: 10.1097/MEJ.0b013e3283462504. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Akin Y, Agzikuru T, Comert S, Atilkan P, Erdag GC, Telatar B. Hospitalizations for pediatric intoxication: a study from Istanbul. Turk J Pediatr. 2011;53(4):369–374. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',39,'2025-10-15 10:17:59','2025-10-15 10:17:59');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4869,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722705','Assessment of the effectiveness and perception of different teaching techniques used in pharmacology among students.','Jaju TA, Parmar UI, Gajbhiye SV, Kurle DG, Shah RM.','2024','gradation upgradation diagrammatic crossword puzzles autobiography poetry handwriting messy blackboard educative crossword puzzles memorize piques tikoo imparts jabaay harnesses liking crossword puzzles upgradation mandal dalui haldar vare kurle bagle rani tekulapally padmavathi heaton srj chavda chaudhari kantharia secon 500x whitelock wainwright gasevic bjet gadbury amyot brockman vora 516x tikoo geeta jabaay marotta aita grcevich camba e11875 gilboy heinerichs pazzaglia jneb ottolenghi athauda stumbar kashan lupi kantharia 516x hashilkar s78 hasamnis arya bioallied jpbs jpbs_96_17 sumanasekera jent sumanasekera cptl charfi hamida klouz trabelsi loueslati','ABSTRACT Introduction: Pharmacology is one of the most dynamic and evolving branches, not only in the concepts but also in teaching methodologies. Many attempts have been made by various colleges all over the world to make the teaching of pharmacology more interesting and relevant. Materials and Methods: The literature from two databases (Google Scholar and PubMed) published between 2005 and 2020 were searched using terms related to “Teaching Techniques” AND “Pharmacology.” The articles with unavailable full-text those published in conference proceedings, duplicate studies, and articles published in languages apart from English were excluded. Teaching techniques were compared using success and popularity ratios, which were calculated on the basis of student’s test score and their feedback with an average minimum score of 50%, which was considered the benchmark. Results: Out of the 1,093 articles reviewed, only 95 studies met the inclusion criteria. The majority of the studies were conducted among medical (64.21%) and pharmacy (28.42%) students. Most common teaching techniques belonged to computer and case-based (21.05% each) types followed by multiple techniques (11.58%) types. Flipped, case-based, multiple methods, modules, and other innovative methods have been well received by students with a popularity ratio ranging between 100 and 81, and improvement in students’ test scores further demonstrated their utility with a success ratio between 100 and 88. The effectiveness and popularity of computer-based techniques and student-centered techniques were found to be moderate (73 and 71). Although popular, team-based techniques and simulations seemed to be less effective in improving test scores (50 and 33, respectively). Conclusion: There is a need for periodic up-gradation in techniques utilized for teaching pharmacology. Every teaching technique has its strengths and weaknesses. They need to be utilized as per the student’s requirement. KEY WORDS: Computer simulation, education medical, modules, single-case studies as topic, multiple teaching techniques, undergraduate Introduction Pharmacology is a subject that deals with therapeutics and drug development; it is one of the essential pillars of medicine.[ 1 ] It contributes significantly to the development of students’ knowledge of drug therapies. The fundamental goal of teaching pharmacology is to help students develop an in-depth knowledge of not only the therapeutic effects of medications but also their side effects, drug interactions, etc.[ 2 ] Although knowledge of pharmacology is crucial, it is often perceived as a dry and volatile subject, especially because of its vast content and difficult-to-remember pharmacological terms, and concepts.[ 3 ] A study reported that inadequate pharmacology knowledge is a major cause of incorrect prescriptions.[ 4 ] Therefore, there is a constant need for periodic upgradation in pharmacology teaching techniques to enhance learning with due consideration to students’ preferences. Pharmacology is taught in various settings such as medical schools, nursing schools, pharmacy schools, dentistry, physiotherapy, and veterinary medicine.[ 5 ] It is one of the most dynamic and rapidly developing branches of medicine. It is not only evolving in concepts but also in teaching methodologies.[ 6 ] Many initiatives have been taken by various colleges across the world to make pharmacology learning engaging and pertinent.[ 3 ] It is of interest to understand the common teaching techniques that have been evaluated by researchers and their influence on student learning. Hence, a review was planned to evaluate published literature on various pharmacology teaching techniques and their impact on students learning, knowledge, attitude, and satisfaction. Materials and Methods The study was a web-based literature review conducted between January and June 2021. Exemption from the institutional ethics committee was obtained (EC/OA-163/2019) as the data were freely available in the public domain. The research articles published between 2005 and 2020 were searched in the PubMed database. Only articles in the English language on teaching techniques in pharmacology published in peer-reviewed journals were selected. Teaching technique was defined by the authors as principles and methods used by various teachers to enhance student learning. We used key search terms with the Boolean operator “teaching technique” AND “pharmacology.” They were combined with additional terms such as “undergraduate medical education,” “postgraduate medical education,” “nursing,” “pharmacy,” and “dental.” To identify additional studies, we also hand-searched the reference lists of the studies, which were already included in our full-text review. Two of the authors went through the titles and abstracts of studies found in the search results and selected relevant articles for possible inclusion. The full-text of these articles was retrieved for further review based on our inclusion criteria, which were as follows: English language studies on various teaching techniques, new teaching interventions used on students in pharmacology, and published articles over the period of the past 16 years, that is, from 2005 to 2020. We excluded articles whose full-text reviews were unavailable, those published in conference proceedings, duplicate studies, and articles published in languages apart from English. Data extraction for each study was performed independently by two authors, and differences arising, if any, were resolved by the remaining authors. The variables included study authors, year of publication, description of the intervention (content, timing, and participants), intervention goals, study design, teaching method used, and significant findings. After data extraction, it was re-reviewed, and subsequently, different teaching technique categories were assigned to selected studies. The assessment of the effectiveness and popularity of each teaching technique were calculated using the following ratios: *Improvement was defined as more than 50% of study participants showing better scores. *Positive feedback was defined as more than 50% of study participants giving positive feedback. Results Our initial database search results identified 1,093 articles, from which we selected 407 for abstract review after removing duplicates (50) and irrelevant titles (636). Following the abstract review, we selected 171 articles for full-text review, and finally, 95 studies meeting the criteria were included [ Figure 1 ]. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Diagrammatic representation of the selection of articles We classified teaching techniques into nine different types: Computer-based teaching techniques, case-based learning, multiple teaching techniques, modules, team-based learning, integrated/flipped approach, simulation-based learning, student-led tutorials, and other innovative approaches. The most commonly used techniques were computer-based and case-based learning (21.05% each) followed by multiple teaching techniques (11.58%). Other innovative approaches included role-play, crossword puzzles, pre-lecture assessment, autobiography of drugs, observation of pharmacy, P drug selection, developing personal formulary, concept mapping, cooperative learning, drawing, clinical context teaching, games, poetry, museum, and using original research article; they were observed in 18 (18.94%) articles [ Figure 2 ]. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Types of teaching techniques Out of 95 studies, the majority of studies, that is, 61 (64.21%) studies, were conducted by MBBS students, 27 (28.42%) studies were conducted by pharmacy students, 6 (6.31%) studies were conducted by dental students, and only 1 (1.05%) study was conducted in nursing students. As far as assessment tests were concerned, 44 (46.32%) studies used both test scores and feedback methods, whereas 29 (30.53%) and 22 (23.16%) studies used only feedback and test scores, respectively. All studies involving the flipped technique showed good success and popularity ratios. The studies using other innovative techniques, modules, team-based learning, and simulation-based techniques showed a good popularity ratio but only the former two techniques showed a good success ratio between 88 and 91%, whereas the latter two showed scores of 67 and 33%, respectively. Also, 81 to 88% of studies involving multiple and case-based techniques showed good success and popularity ratios, whereas computer and student-led methods reported both ratios of less than 80% [ Tables 1 and 2 ]. A summary of all teaching techniques with their important advantages and disadvantages is described in Table 3 . Table 1. Success ratio based on test scores of teaching techniques Name of the teaching techniques No of studies reporting improvement in test scores Total no. of studies Studies with a good success ratio (%) Flipped 5 5 100 Case-based 10 11 91 Other innovative 10 11 91 Modules 7 8 88 Multiple methods 7 8 88 Compute-based 11 15 73 Team-based 2 3 67 Student-led 1 2 50 Simulation-based 1 3 33 Open in a new tab Table 2. Popularity ratio based on feedback about teaching techniques Name of the teaching techniques No. of studies reporting positive feedback Total no. of studies Studies reporting a good popularity ratio (%) Other innovative 15 15 100 Modules 4 4 100 Team-based 4 4 100 Flipped 3 3 100 Simulation-based 1 1 100 Multiple methods 7 8 88 Case-based 13 16 81 Student led 3 6 75 Computer-based 12 17 71 Open in a new tab Table 3. Summary of all teaching techniques with their pros and cons Teaching techniques Advantages Disadvantages 1) Computer-based learning - It avoids the issue of poor handwriting and messy blackboard. - PowerPoint presentations may contain too much information. (20 studies conducted in 33 to 1300 medical, pharmacy and nursing students) - It is more interesting and engaging. - Lectures may be delivered too fast. - It integrates the text with the pictures and images, thus enhancing the educative value. - Computer programs are helpful in identifying problem areas and useful for revision purposes. - It may be challenging to develop innovative ways to convey the course content and integrate it with other disciplines. - Students become passive observers rather than active participants. - It reduces the interactive discussion. - Knowledge through e-drug is not all-inclusive in terms of what might appear in teaching and assessment. 2) Case-based learning - It facilitates the learning of rational pharmacology - To have persistent improvements in their critical thinking skills in enhancing professional practice, students need earlier and more frequent exposure. (20 studies conducted in 16 to 179 medical and pharmacy students) - It enables a better understanding of concepts and focuses on clinical application. 3) Multiple teaching techniques - It resulted in contextualized teaching and encouraged the student’s active participation and interaction. They are effective only when they are well structured, of high quality, and supported by tutorials. (11 studies conducted with 68 to 360 medical, dental, and pharmacy students) - It has effectively addressed gaps in the curriculum - It provided students the opportunity to integrate multiple domains of learning 4) Modules - It significantly increased students’ knowledge. - It was not effective in improving the students’ confidence in implementing communication skills in clinical situations. (8 studies conducted in 40 to 165 medical and dental students) - It is an effective tool for teaching prescription writing to the students 5) Team-based learning - It increased student involvement, improved communication skills, and enhanced critical-thinking abilities. - Absenteeism can be a concern. (5 studies conducted in 16 to 179 medical and pharmacy students) - Not all members put in their full effort. 6) Flipped approach - More one-to-one time with the teacher is provided. - Lack of access to the internet or a home computer can hamper learning. (5 studies conducted in 101 to 161 medical and pharmacy students) - Self-paced learning and deeper understanding happen. - Time and effort required from a teacher’s perspective are higher than for a traditional class. - Test score improvements were observed by several teachers. 7) Simulation-based teaching - It can help to connect theory and practice in medical education. Lack of realistic reactions to drug therapy. (4 studies conducted in 90 to 234 medical and pharmacy students) - It improved student counseling aptitude and information retention scores 8) Student-led tutorials - It promotes learners’ motivation and their capacity to learn independently. - It relies on student preparation. (4 studies conducted in 50 to 110 medical students) - It aids in overcoming the logistic challenges due to faculty shortage. - The depth of the subject can be dictated by the students themselves and may not cover the entire subject. 9) Other innovative methods Games designed to develop problem-solving skills, communication skills, and teamwork. - They are rated poorly on the perception of learning. (18 studies conducted in 78 to 583 medical, pharmacy, and dental students) Crossword puzzles made learning fun as they were interactive and enjoyable, and provided a nice change from the typically dry nature of the subject. - They can be used as supplements and not as a substitute for conventional classroom methods. Original research articles improved knowledge of topics and the likelihood of its use in the future. Open in a new tab Discussion This study is the first of its kind to compile various teaching techniques of pharmacology used in different medical and paramedical courses and their impact on students’ learning. The improvement in test scores gives us a better understanding of how effective the teaching approach works to help students understand, memorize, and recall the concept. In contrast, the feedback assessment gives students’ perspectives and piques their interest in innovative teaching strategies. We found that the majority of the studies were computer-based learning, which has now become an integral part of recently introduced competency-based medical education. This might be a result of the easy availability of computer-based resources and the need to integrate technology and medicine. These findings are consistent with a study conducted by John on the nature of the trend in undergraduate pharmacology curriculum and the responsible factors. He found that until the 1990s, laboratory-based practical classes were the center of pharmacology teaching; thereafter, medical schools began incorporating computers and e-learning, starting a new trend.[ 7 ] Although computer-based learning was widely employed, our analysis revealed that a few studies reported negative feedback and failure to improve test scores.[ 8 , 9 ] It might be because such learning demands a cultural shift in how students are taught and requires careful strategic planning, adequate quality control, staff training, and multidisciplinary collaboration. Also, there is minimal direct interaction with teachers, which makes it challenging for the students to stay focused. Our analysis also discovered that many studies employed case-based learning (CBL), which may be due to the shift from a teacher-centered to a learner-centered approach to encourage student’s critical thinking and involvement and foster teamwork. Studies on CBL have shown improvement in test scores compared to didactic lectures, suggesting that this method leads to a deeper understanding of the subject.[ 10 ] A study by Tikoo et al .[ 11 ] found that most pupils favored CBL as a part of regular teaching for greater comprehension and correlation of medications used to treat disorders. Surprisingly, teaching techniques such as simulation and team-based learning received positive feedback; however, they failed to improve the test scores of students significantly. By providing the learner with a somewhat realistic environment, simulation-based techniques raise the bar and boost their confidence. Despite the fact that it primarily imparts practical knowledge without significantly enhancing the basic understanding, it offers a more enjoyable experience than traditional lectures. A study by Jabaay et al .[ 12 ] found that simulation-based learning significantly improved objective performance on a standardized examination, and it is an effective tool for student learning in the pre-clinical stage of undergraduate medical education. Team-based learning (TBL) promotes student engagement, reasoning, and other non-cognitive qualities, namely, individual preparedness and contributions to team discussions. TBL harnesses the resources of students who were well-prepared to engage in the exercises and help their peers in the learning process. It increases the liking for the activity, as suggested by positive feedback. According to the result of our study, techniques such as the flipped teaching method have helped in improving the performance of the students, as evidenced by an increase in test scores along with positive feedback. In the flipped technique, teachers provide students the lecture materials and PowerPoint presentations to view at home the previous day, thus prioritizing active learning. It has been known to promote self-directed learning and help in retaining it longer. Recent evidence shows that the flipped classroom approach improves student perception, learning, critical thinking skills, and motivation in comparison to traditional lecturing methods.[ 13 , 14 ] Modules are organized units of instruction that focus on a specific topic or concept. They can be used to break down complex material into smaller and more manageable parts. By employing modules, teachers can tailor their teaching to the individual needs of their students and provide more opportunities for student engagement and active learning. Studies have shown that the use of modules can produce improvement in learning outcomes and increase student satisfaction with the course.[ 15 ] Student-led teaching approach involves giving a more active role to students in their learning by allowing them to take charge of certain parts of the course. Students could be asked to create and deliver their own presentations or facilitate small group discussions. This method can help students develop a deeper understanding as well as improve their critical thinking and communication skills. Additionally, it promotes a sense of ownership and responsibility for their learning, which can increase motivation and engagement.[ 16 , 17 ] Finally, multiple teaching techniques can also be used in pharmacology to cater to different learning styles and preferences. Teachers can incorporate web-based interactive games, crossword puzzles, an instructional video, a music video, and fill-in-the-blank tables to help students better understand complex concepts. By using various teaching techniques, instructors can engage students in different ways and help them develop a more comprehensive understanding of the material.[ 18 , 19 ] Limitations of our study were that we studied only free full-text articles published in the English language, which led to the inclusion of relatively few studies. We missed out on studies that were part of paid journals, which can contribute to the selection bias. Conclusion Teaching techniques such as innovative, flipped, case-based, multiple methods, and modules have been well received by students, and an increase in test scores further demonstrated their utility. The effectiveness and popularity of computer-based techniques and student-centered techniques were found to be moderate. Although popular, team-based techniques and simulations seemed to be less effective. There is a need for periodic upgradation in techniques utilized for teaching in pharmacology. Every teaching technique has its own strengths and weaknesses. They need to be utilized as per the student’s requirement. Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts of interest. Funding Statement Nil References 1. Ghosh A, Mandal K, Biswas S, Dalui S, Haldar M, Biswas S. Evaluation of pharmacology teaching-learning methods in a government medical college. Int J Basic Clin Pharmacol. 2016;5:126–30. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Vare VA, Kurle DG, Bagle TR, Hire RC, Shukla AO. Evaluation of teaching methods in pharmacology among MBBS students. Int J Basic Clin Pharmacol. 2017;6:1352–7. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Rani V, Tekulapally K, Padmavathi V, Simpson G. Second year medical students perception about pharmacology and teaching methodologies used: A questionnaire based cross sectional study. Indian J Basic Appl Med Res. 2016;5:238–45. [ Google Scholar ] 4. Heaton A, Webb DJ, Maxwell SRJ. Undergraduate preparation for prescribing: The views of 2413 UK medical students and recent graduates. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2008;66:128–34. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2125.2008.03197.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Engels F. Pharmacology education: Reflections and challenges. Eur J Pharmacol. 2018;833:392–5. doi: 10.1016/j.ejphar.2018.06.032. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Chavda N, Yadav P, Chaudhari M, Kantharia ND. Secon student''s feedback on teaching methodology and evaluation methods in pharmacology. Natl J Physiol Pharm Pharmacol. 2011;1:23–31. [ Google Scholar ] 7. John LJ. A review of computer assisted learning in medical undergraduates. J Pharmacol Pharmacother. 2013;4:86–90. doi: 10.4103/0976-500X.110870. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Yan L, Whitelock-Wainwright A, Guan Q, Wen G, Gašević D, Chen G. Students''experience of online learning during the COVID-19 pandemic: A province-wide survey study. Br J Educ Technol. 2021;52:2038–57. doi: 10.1111/bjet.13102. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Gadbury-Amyot CC, Brockman WG. Transition of a traditional pharmacology course for dental students to an online delivery format: A pilot project. J Dent Educ. 2011;75:633–45. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Vora MB, Shah CJ. Case-based learning in pharmacology: Moving from teaching to learning. Int J Appl Basic Med Res. 2015;5:21–3. doi: 10.4103/2229-516X.162259. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Tikoo D, Gupta M, Geeta S. Student feedback on teaching-learning methodology and evaluation methods in pharmacology. Int J Basic Clin Pharmacol. 2015;4:1260–6. [ Google Scholar ] 12. Jabaay MJ, Marotta DA, Aita SL, Walker DB, Grcevich LO, Camba V, et al. Medical simulation-based learning outcomes in pre-clinical medical education. Cureus. 2020;12:e11875. doi: 10.7759/cureus.11875. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Gilboy MB, Heinerichs S, Pazzaglia G. Enhancing student engagement using the flipped classroom. J Nutr Educ Behav. 2015;47:109–14. doi: 10.1016/j.jneb.2014.08.008. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Ottolenghi J, Athauda G, Stumbar SE, Kashan SB, Lupi C. Contraceptive pharmacology and risk communication: A case-based flipped classroom exercise. MedEdPORTAL. 2019;15:10790. doi: 10.15766/mep_2374-8265.10790. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Yadav PP, Chaudhary M, Patel J, Shah A, Kantharia ND. Effectiveness of integrated teaching module in pharmacology among medical undergraduates. Int J App Basic Med Res. 2016;6:215–9. doi: 10.4103/2229-516X.186962. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Arora K, Hashilkar NK. Effectiveness of student-led objective tutorials in pharmacology teaching to medical students. Indian J Pharmacol. 2016;48:S78–82. doi: 10.4103/0253-7613.193321. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Hasamnis AA, Arya A. Evaluation of teaching clinical pharmacology of antiarrhythmic drugs to first-year MBBS students through worksheet-based small-group discussion. J Pharm Bioallied Sci. 2017;9:282–3. doi: 10.4103/jpbs.JPBS_96_17. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Sumanasekera W, Turner C, Ly K, Hoang P, Jent T, Sumanasekera T. Evaluation of multiple active learning strategies in a pharmacology course. Curr Pharm Teach Learn. 2020;12:88–94. doi: 10.1016/j.cptl.2019.10.016. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Charfi R, Ben Hamida E, Klouz A, Trabelsi S, Loueslati MH. Progress and assessment of a contextualized teaching session of pharmacology. Tunis Med. 2018;96:6–11. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',115,'2025-10-15 10:18:02','2025-10-15 10:18:02');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4870,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10803227','[Characteristics performance of laryngopharyngeal reflux in narrow band imaging].','Zheng N, Liu J, Jiang L, Gao Q.','2023','研究分析窄带成像 技术在咽喉反流 诊断中的应用价值 选取2022年9月 2023年4月因咽喉不适就诊于哈尔滨医科大学附属第一医院耳鼻咽喉头颈外科住院部或喉镜室的患者共计275例作为研究对象 所有患者均完成rsi rfs评分量表和电子喉镜检查 包括普通白光和nbi 根据2022年lprd专家共识 以rsi和rfs评分量表为诊断标准分为lpr组和非lpr组 采用χ 检验分析不同组间nbi下特征性表现阳性率的差异 nbi与量表诊断方法的一致性采用kappa分析 以rsi和rfs评分为诊断标准 分析nbi方法的诊断效能 lpr组共有190例 其中157例在nbi模式下可见特征性表现 阳性率为82 非lpr组共有85例 阳性16例 阳性率为18 2组间阳性率差异有统计学意义 rfs量表诊断方法与nbi诊断方法的一致率为82 采用kappa一致性分析 提示两种诊断方法的一致性良好 以rsi rfs量表为lpr诊断标准 nbi诊断方法的敏感度为82 特异度为81 阳性预测值为90 阴性预测值为67 nbi作为一项新型内镜成像技术 能够显示黏膜表面微血管的微小变化 可在lpr诊断中发挥重要作用 咽喉反流 窄带成像 特征性表现 harbin lprd 咽喉反流 是指胃十二指肠内容物反流至食管上括约肌以上的上呼吸消化道 包括鼻咽 喉咽和喉等部位 可引起上呼吸消化道的形态学改变及一系列症状和体征 研究表明 耳鼻咽喉科很多疾病与lpr有关 随着人们生活水平的提升及饮食习惯的改变 lpr的发病率也在逐年提高 最近一项多中心流行病学调查显示 在我国耳鼻咽喉头颈外科门诊就诊的患者中 lpr发病率约为10 但其中仅约14 的患者有明确lpr的诊断史 目前国内外尚无诊断lpr的金标准 近年来 窄带成像 作为一项新型内镜成像技术 可以显示黏膜表面微血管的微小变化 多用于消化 耳鼻咽喉头颈外科等科室的癌前病变 早期癌变的诊断 手术安全缘 切缘的判断及术后随访复查等 较少用于炎症性疾病的诊断 lpr的病理特征是胃十二指肠反流物导致咽喉部黏膜的广泛炎症性改变 早期癌性病变和炎症性病变都会引起浅表微血管的改变 如血管增生和新血管生成 这就为nbi技术应用于lpr的诊断提供了依据 kasp等 研究也认为nbi较普通白光在反流性疾病的诊断中更具敏感性 目前国内外关于nbi应用于lpr诊断的相关研究较少 li等 和wu等 研究认为lpr在nbi下的表现具有良好的诊断价值 本研究主要目的是探讨nbi在lpr诊断中的应用价值 资料与方法 临床资料 选取2022年9月 2023年4月因咽喉不适 包括声嘶 咽部异物感 咳嗽等 就诊于哈尔滨医科大学附属第一医院耳鼻咽喉头颈外科住院部或喉镜室的患者作为研究对象 病程1个月 30年不等 有下列情况的患者将会被排除在外 ①1个月内上呼吸道感染病史 ②3个月内口服过ppi和促胃动力等药物 ③患有精神 神经等方面疾病的患者 ④其他严重影响反流症状指数 反流体征评分 的情况 如咽喉部巨大恶性肿瘤 喉部术后粘连等 所有患者对本研究知情并签署知情同意书 研究通过了哈尔滨医科大学附属第一医院医学伦理委员会的批准 belafsky等根据lpr患者典型的症状和体征设计了反流症状指数 反流体征评分 所有入组患者均完成了rsi rfs量表和电子喉镜检查 包括普通白光和nbi 请一位不了解患者病情的医生与患者进行沟通并填写rsi量表相关内容 再请两位不了解患者病情的医生对电子喉镜白光下体征进行评分 取两位医生rfs评分的平均值作为最终得分 电子喉镜检查 包括普通白光和nbi 均由另一位不了解患者病情的医生完成 根据2022年lprd的专家共识 的诊断标准 以rsi 13分和 7分的患者为lpr组 以rsi 13分且rfs 7分的患者为非lpr组 根据临床观察及查阅相关文献 我们认定电子喉镜nbi模式下如杓区 杓间区或环后区见稀疏或簇状的棕色斑点即为阳性 反之为阴性 nbi结果的确定先由两位不了解患者病情的医生进行判定 如果给出了不一样的结果 再请第三位医生给出最终的结果 这些医生都熟练掌握nbi的基本原理和使用方法 杓间区稀疏的棕色斑点 环后区簇状的棕色斑点 杓间区阴性对照图像 环后区阴性对照图像 杓间区簇状的棕色斑点 统计学方法 采用ibm 0软件对数据进行统计分析 计量资料采用 计数资料采用 lpr组和非lpr组的nbi下阳性率的差异采用 检验分析 量表诊断方法和nbi诊断方法的一致性分析采用kappa检验 以rsi和rfs量表为诊断标准 分析nbi的诊断价值 05为差异有统计学意义 共招募275例符合条件的患者 根据专家共识分为lpr组和非lpr组 lpr组190例中男91例 女99例 年龄25 79岁 中位数52 00岁 非lpr组85例中男42例 女43例 年龄18 75岁 中位数52 00岁 2组患者在性别 年龄方面差异均无统计学意义 lpr组中有157例在nbi模式下杓区 杓间区或环后区可见稀疏或簇状的棕色斑点 阳性率为82 而非lpr组中有16例可见类似棕色斑点 阳性率为18 2组阳性率的差异采用 检验分析 差异有统计学意义 认为nbi模式下见稀疏或簇状的棕色斑点可用于lpr的诊断 rfs量表诊断方法与nbi诊断方法的一致率为82 提示两种诊断方法的一致性良好 以rsi rfs诊断量表为lpr诊断标准 nbi诊断方法的敏感度为82 特异度为81 阳性预测值为90 阴性预测值为67 rfs量表诊断方法与nbi诊断方法结果分析 nbi诊断 rfs诊断 lpr是耳鼻咽喉头颈外科门诊的常见病和多发病 耳鼻喉科许多疾病也与lpr有关 如声带白斑 下咽癌 近些年儿童lprd也逐渐受到重视 如分泌性中耳炎 腺样体肥大 目前常用lpr的诊断方法有rsi量表 rfs量表 质子泵抑制剂 诊断性治疗 双探头24 ph监测 h多通道腔内阻抗 ph监测 唾液胃蛋白酶检测等 根据lpr患者典型的症状和体征设计的rsi量表和rfs量表 尽管评分受患者和检查医生的主观影响较大 仍广泛为国内外多数学者所接受 高竞逾等 研究也表明rsi rfs评分量表与客观方法唾液胃蛋白酶检测对诊断lpr的一致性较高 ppi诊断性治疗只能改善以酸性反流为主的症状和体征 并且目前诊断性ppi治疗的周期 次数不统一 以及治疗效果受患者用药依从性的影响 诊断缺乏客观性 li等 称ppi不能缓解高达40 的lpr患者的症状 越来越多的研究表明非酸反流在lpr中扮演着重要角色 国外有文献称 海藻酸盐可以缓解单纯ppi治疗效果不明显的lpr患者的症状 但国内目前尚无相关文献报道 双探头24 ph监测由于不能监测非酸反流 导致其准确性大大降低 h多通道腔内阻抗 ph监测 multichannel mii 是精确诊断酸性 非酸性或混合反流最可靠的手段 但是mii ph监测的各种参数众多 各参数的标准值尚未达成共识 针对ph监测 无论哪种方法都会存在一些不足 如果监测期间没有反流发作并不一定意味着患者没有lpr 这会造成假阴性 同样如果监测期间出现生理性反流也会造成假阳性 而且实际应用中 ph监测都有操作过程复杂繁琐 检查费用高 监测时间长 且为侵入性检查等问题 导致患者接受度较低 唾液胃蛋白酶检测是近年来逐渐兴起的一种无创 客观诊断lpr的方法 具有方便 无创伤 客观性强等优点 张青青等 研究证实胃蛋白酶在lpr的发病过程中发挥着重要作用 但由于生理性反流的存在 健康人喉部也可能检测出胃蛋白酶 导致出现假阳性的结果 唾液胃蛋白酶的取样时间点 取样次数 诊断阈值 等尚无统一标准 研究发现 血红蛋白对光波有2个吸收峰 蓝光的415 nm和绿光540 nbi就是利用人体血红蛋白的不同光波频谱吸收峰以及处于这些吸收峰范围内的光波在人体黏膜组织内的穿透深度不同 从而达到内镜下特殊的染色效果 以便于观察 国内外相关文献关于nbi用于lpr诊断的研究中 大多用于描述lpr的体征 且认为nbi下lpr的体征变化比普通白光更明显 评分更高 galli等 利用普通白光和nbi评估儿童lpr患者的体征 发现nbi下rfs评分更高 且结果显著 朱增辉等 通过比较儿童lpr患者普通白光和nbi两种模式下的rfs评分及诊断的符合率 发现nbi模式下声带水肿 喉黏膜弥漫性水肿 充血和红斑等方面更明显 也得出了nbi下rfs评分更高 且有显著差异 he等 研究发现lpr患者喉黏膜在nbi下特殊的形态学表现为血管增生和绿点 出现率分别为92 和88 特异性和敏感性高 可在lpr的辅助诊断中发挥作用 何宁等 研究发现nbi在观察黏膜表面血管的变化中有独特优势 恶性肿瘤的表浅血管往往有连续性中断 呈斑片状 袢状等特征性表现 而lpr引起的咽喉部炎症黏膜则表现为表浅血管扩张 galli等 发现lpr患者舌扁桃体隐窝炎症和咽后壁鹅卵石样改变在nbi下更明显 且舌扁桃体隐窝炎症的分级与rfs评分之间存在显著相关性 认为nbi可用于lpr的诊断 本研究也存在一些不足 ①我们没有使用咽喉ph监测和唾液胃蛋白酶检测等客观检测手段 仅使用了rsi和rfs量表评分对患者进行分组 由于受主观影响较大 分析lpr组中可能并非所有患者都是真正的lpr患者 同样非lpr组中可能也会存在真正的lpr患者 并且这种主观影响只能通过两位医生对rfs量表进行分别评估后取平均值 以求把主观影响的误差降到最低 ②我们所观察的nbi下的特征性表现 对lpr的诊断也不能达到百分之百的正确率 我们寻求的是找到一种方便 患者接受度高 相对准确的辅助诊断lpr的方法 必要时可联合其他诊断方法 ③我们没有具体阐明lpr导致的咽喉部黏膜广泛炎症性改变的机制 即体现在nbi模式下为杓区 杓间区或环后区的稀疏或簇状的细小棕色斑点 是如何通过nbi观察不同形态上皮乳头内毛细血管袢 ipcl 得到的 我们准备在以后的研究中 从不同类型反流物 黏膜组织病理学检查等方面进一步探讨反流性的慢性炎症是如何导致不同形态ipcl的 更进一步解释nbi模式下不同类型棕色斑点的成因 lpr的客观诊断方法中 大多从检测反流物中的某种物质或离子等方面入手 较少从反流物导致咽喉部损伤后所需观察的最终结果方面入手 本研究中做到了这一点 在研究中 我们还发现了一个问题 有的患者仅杓间区或环后区可见稀疏或簇状的细小棕色斑点 而有的患者在环后区 杓间区及杓区均可见细小的棕色斑点 我们猜测可能与患者的反流次数 反流类型 病程长短等有关系 有实验证明 每周3次lpr事件的发生就会导致严重的喉部损伤 但具体原因还需要国内外学者进一步去探讨 研究和分析 目前nbi应用于lpr的诊断尚处于研究探索阶段 缺乏大量的临床数据 相信随着研究的深入 nbi会成为一种很有前景的辅助诊断lpr的方法 哈医大一院科研创新基金 2021m12 利益冲突 所有作者均声明不存在利益冲突 中华耳鼻咽喉头颈外科杂志编辑委员会咽喉组 中华医学会耳鼻咽喉头颈外科学分会咽喉学组 中华医学会耳鼻咽喉头颈外科学分会嗓音学组 咽喉反流性疾病诊断与治疗专家共识 2022年 修订版 中华耳鼻咽喉头颈外科杂志 cn115330 咽喉反流性疾病的发病机制及其与耳鼻咽喉疾病相关性研究进展 听力学及言语疾病杂志 irjala matar remacle pharyngo boscolo nata tirelli capriotti suronc kasap zeybel asik nonerosive gastrointestin pepsin belafsky postma koufman s0892 belafsky postma koufman pepsin s12935 vageli doukas doukas hypopharyngeal restuti tamin nugroho e065291 tou 胃蛋白酶免疫组织化学与胃蛋白酶检测试剂盒在咽喉反流诊断中的一致性分析 临床耳鼻咽喉头颈外科杂志 pepsin physiopathology nonacid multichannel weissbrod nyas akst hamdan 质子泵抑制剂对咽喉反流患者唾液胃蛋白酶浓度的影响 临床耳鼻咽喉头颈外科杂志 multitime pepsin lio2 pepsin pepsin meucci salonna pharyngo 窄带成像技术在诊断儿童咽喉反流中的应用 中国耳鼻咽喉颅底外科杂志 窄带成像高清电子鼻咽喉镜对咽喉反流病的诊疗价值 临床耳鼻咽喉头颈外科杂志 settimi salonna lingual laryngo koufman casiano mhn','Abstract 目的 研究分析窄带成像(narrow band imaging，NBI)技术在咽喉反流(laryngopharyngeal reflux，LPR)诊断中的应用价值。 方法 选取2022年9月-2023年4月因咽喉不适就诊于哈尔滨医科大学附属第一医院耳鼻咽喉头颈外科住院部或喉镜室的患者共计275例作为研究对象，所有患者均完成RSI、RFS评分量表和电子喉镜检查(包括普通白光和NBI)，根据2022年LPRD专家共识，以RSI和RFS评分量表为诊断标准分为LPR组和非LPR组，采用χ 2 检验分析不同组间NBI下特征性表现阳性率的差异，NBI与量表诊断方法的一致性采用Kappa分析，以RSI和RFS评分为诊断标准，分析NBI方法的诊断效能。 结果 LPR组共有190例，其中157例在NBI模式下可见特征性表现，阳性率为82.6%(157/190)，非LPR组共有85例，阳性16例，阳性率为18.8%(16/85)，2组间阳性率差异有统计学意义(χ 2 =102.47， P < 0.05)。RSI、RFS量表诊断方法与NBI诊断方法的一致率为82.2%(226/275)，采用Kappa一致性分析，Kappa=0.605( P < 0.05)，提示两种诊断方法的一致性良好。以RSI、RFS量表为LPR诊断标准，NBI诊断方法的敏感度为82.6%(157/190)，特异度为81.2%(69/85)，阳性预测值为90.8%(157/173)，阴性预测值为67.6%(69/102)。 结论 NBI作为一项新型内镜成像技术，能够显示黏膜表面微血管的微小变化，可在LPR诊断中发挥重要作用。 Keywords: 咽喉反流, 窄带成像, 诊断, 特征性表现 Abstract Objective To study the application value of narrow-band imaging in the diagnosis of laryngopharyngeal reflux. Methods A total of 275 patients admitted to the inpatient department or laryngoscopy room of the Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery Department of the First Affiliated Hospital of Harbin Medical University from September 2022 to April 2023 due to throat discomfort were selected as the research subjects. All of them completed RSI, RFS scoring scales and electronic laryngoscopy(including ordinary white light and NBI). According to the expert consensus of LPRD in 2022, RSI and RFS scoring scale were used as diagnostic criteria to divide them into LPR group and non-LPR group. Chi-square test was used to analyze the differences of positive rates of characteristic manifestations under NBI among different groups. The consistency of NBI and scale diagnostic methods was analyzed by Kappa, and RSI and RFS scoring were used as diagnostic criteria, The diagnostic efficiency of NBI method was analyzed. Results There were 190 people in the LPR group, 157 of whom showed characteristic performance under the NBI mode, with a positive rate of 82.6%(157/190); there were 85 people in the non-LPR group, with a positive rate of 18.8%(16/85). There was a statistically significant difference in the positive rate between the two groups(χ 2 =102.47, P < 0.05). The consistency rate between RSI, RFS and NBI was 82.2%(226/275). Kappa consistency analysis was used, and Kappa=0.605( P < 0.05), indicating good consistency between the two diagnostic methods. Using RSI and RFS as diagnostic criteria for LPR, the sensitivity of NBI diagnostic method was 82.6%(157/190), specificity 81.2%(69/85), positive predictive value 90.8%(157/173) and negative predictive value 67.6%(69/102). Conclusion Narrow-band imaging, as a new endoscopic imaging technique, can show small changes in mucosal surface micro vessels and play an important role in the diagnosis of laryngopharyngeal reflux. Keywords: laryngopharyngeal reflux, narrow-band imaging, diagnosis, characteristic performance 咽喉反流(laryngopharyngeal reflux，LPR)是指胃十二指肠内容物反流至食管上括约肌以上的上呼吸消化道，包括鼻咽、口咽、喉咽和喉等部位，可引起上呼吸消化道的形态学改变及一系列症状和体征 [ 1 ] 。研究表明，耳鼻咽喉科很多疾病与LPR有关，随着人们生活水平的提升及饮食习惯的改变，LPR的发病率也在逐年提高。最近一项多中心流行病学调查显示，在我国耳鼻咽喉头颈外科门诊就诊的患者中，LPR发病率约为10.15%，但其中仅约14.09%的患者有明确LPR的诊断史 [ 2 ] 。 目前国内外尚无诊断LPR的金标准。近年来，窄带成像(narrow band imaging，NBI)作为一项新型内镜成像技术，可以显示黏膜表面微血管的微小变化 [ 3 ] ，多用于消化、耳鼻咽喉头颈外科等科室的癌前病变、早期癌变的诊断，手术安全缘、切缘的判断及术后随访复查等 [ 4 ] ，较少用于炎症性疾病的诊断。LPR的病理特征是胃十二指肠反流物导致咽喉部黏膜的广泛炎症性改变。然而，早期癌性病变和炎症性病变都会引起浅表微血管的改变，如血管增生和新血管生成，这就为NBI技术应用于LPR的诊断提供了依据。Kasp等 [ 5 ] 研究也认为NBI较普通白光在反流性疾病的诊断中更具敏感性。但是，目前国内外关于NBI应用于LPR诊断的相关研究较少，Li等 [ 6 ] 和Wu等 [ 7 ] 研究认为LPR在NBI下的表现具有良好的诊断价值。本研究主要目的是探讨NBI在LPR诊断中的应用价值。 1. 资料与方法 1.1. 临床资料 选取2022年9月-2023年4月因咽喉不适(包括声嘶、咽部异物感、清嗓、咳嗽等)就诊于哈尔滨医科大学附属第一医院耳鼻咽喉头颈外科住院部或喉镜室的患者作为研究对象，病程1个月~30年不等。有下列情况的患者将会被排除在外：①1个月内上呼吸道感染病史；②3个月内口服过PPI和促胃动力等药物；③患有精神、神经等方面疾病的患者；④其他严重影响反流症状指数(reflux symptom index，RSI)、反流体征评分(reflux finding score，RFS)的情况(如咽喉部巨大恶性肿瘤、喉部术后粘连等)。所有患者对本研究知情并签署知情同意书，研究通过了哈尔滨医科大学附属第一医院医学伦理委员会的批准。 1.2. 方法 Belafsky等根据LPR患者典型的症状和体征设计了反流症状指数(reflux symptom index，RSI) [ 8 ] 、反流体征评分(reflux finding score，RFS) [ 9 ] ，所有入组患者均完成了RSI、RFS量表和电子喉镜检查(包括普通白光和NBI)。请一位不了解患者病情的医生与患者进行沟通并填写RSI量表相关内容，再请两位不了解患者病情的医生对电子喉镜白光下体征进行评分，取两位医生RFS评分的平均值作为最终得分，电子喉镜检查(包括普通白光和NBI)均由另一位不了解患者病情的医生完成。根据2022年LPRD的专家共识 [ 1 ] 的诊断标准，以RSI>13分和(或)RFS>7分的患者为LPR组，以RSI≤13分且RFS≤7分的患者为非LPR组。 根据临床观察及查阅相关文献，我们认定电子喉镜NBI模式下如杓区、杓间区或环后区见稀疏或簇状的棕色斑点即为阳性( 图 1 ~ 3 )，反之为阴性( 图 4 、 5 )。NBI结果的确定先由两位不了解患者病情的医生进行判定，如果给出了不一样的结果，再请第三位医生给出最终的结果，这些医生都熟练掌握NBI的基本原理和使用方法。 图 1. Open in a new tab 杓间区稀疏的棕色斑点 图 3. Open in a new tab 环后区簇状的棕色斑点 图 4. Open in a new tab 杓间区阴性对照图像 图 5. Open in a new tab 环后区阴性对照图像 图 2. Open in a new tab 杓间区簇状的棕色斑点 1.3. 统计学方法 采用IBM SPSS 26.0软件对数据进行统计分析。计量资料采用 t 检验，计数资料采用 χ 2 检验。LPR组和非LPR组的NBI下阳性率的差异采用 χ 2 检验分析，量表诊断方法和NBI诊断方法的一致性分析采用Kappa检验。以RSI和RFS量表为诊断标准，分析NBI的诊断价值。以 P < 0.05为差异有统计学意义。 2. 结果 共招募275例符合条件的患者。根据专家共识分为LPR组和非LPR组，LPR组190例中男91例，女99例，年龄25~79岁，平均(51.41±11.09)岁，中位数52.00岁；非LPR组85例中男42例，女43例，年龄18~75岁，平均(49.55±13.45)岁，中位数52.00岁。2组患者在性别、年龄方面差异均无统计学意义( P ＞0.05)。 LPR组中有157例在NBI模式下杓区、杓间区或环后区可见稀疏或簇状的棕色斑点，阳性率为82.6%(157/190)，而非LPR组中有16例可见类似棕色斑点，阳性率为18.8%(16/85)，2组阳性率的差异采用 χ 2 检验分析，差异有统计学意义(χ 2 =102.47， P < 0.05)，认为NBI模式下见稀疏或簇状的棕色斑点可用于LPR的诊断。 RSI、RFS量表诊断方法与NBI诊断方法的一致率为82.2%(226/275)，Kappa=0.605( P < 0.05)，提示两种诊断方法的一致性良好，见 表 1 。以RSI、RFS诊断量表为LPR诊断标准，NBI诊断方法的敏感度为82.6%(157/190)，特异度为81.2%(69/85)，阳性预测值为90.8%(157/173)，阴性预测值为67.6%(69/102)。 表 1. RSI、RFS量表诊断方法与NBI诊断方法结果分析 例 NBI诊断 RSI、RFS诊断 合计 阳性 阴性 阳性 157 16 173 阴性 33 69 102 合计 190 85 275 Open in a new tab 3. 讨论 LPR是耳鼻咽喉头颈外科门诊的常见病和多发病。耳鼻喉科许多疾病也与LPR有关，如声带白斑、喉癌 [ 10 ] 、下咽癌 [ 11 ] 等。此外，近些年儿童LPRD也逐渐受到重视，如分泌性中耳炎 [ 12 ] 、腺样体肥大 [ 13 ] 等。 目前常用LPR的诊断方法有RSI量表、RFS量表、质子泵抑制剂(proton pump inhibitor，PPI)诊断性治疗、双探头24 h pH监测、24 h多通道腔内阻抗-pH监测、唾液胃蛋白酶检测等。 根据LPR患者典型的症状和体征设计的RSI量表和RFS量表，尽管评分受患者和检查医生的主观影响较大，仍广泛为国内外多数学者所接受，高竞逾等 [ 14 ] 研究也表明RSI、RFS评分量表与客观方法唾液胃蛋白酶检测对诊断LPR的一致性较高。PPI诊断性治疗只能改善以酸性反流为主的症状和体征，并且目前诊断性PPI治疗的周期、剂量、次数不统一，以及治疗效果受患者用药依从性的影响，诊断缺乏客观性。Li等 [ 15 ] 称PPI不能缓解高达40%的LPR患者的症状，越来越多的研究表明非酸反流在LPR中扮演着重要角色 [ 16 - 17 ] 。国外有文献称 [ 18 ] 海藻酸盐可以缓解单纯PPI治疗效果不明显的LPR患者的症状，但国内目前尚无相关文献报道。 双探头24 h pH监测由于不能监测非酸反流，导致其准确性大大降低。24 h多通道腔内阻抗-pH监测(multichannel intraluminal impedance，MII-pH)是精确诊断酸性、非酸性或混合反流最可靠的手段 [ 19 ] ，但是MII-pH监测的各种参数众多，各参数的标准值尚未达成共识 [ 19 - 20 ] 。针对pH监测，无论哪种方法都会存在一些不足，如果监测期间没有反流发作并不一定意味着患者没有LPR，这会造成假阴性，同样如果监测期间出现生理性反流也会造成假阳性。而且实际应用中，pH监测都有操作过程复杂繁琐，检查费用高，监测时间长，且为侵入性检查等问题，导致患者接受度较低。 唾液胃蛋白酶检测是近年来逐渐兴起的一种无创、客观诊断LPR的方法，具有方便、无创伤、客观性强等优点。张青青等 [ 21 ] 研究证实胃蛋白酶在LPR的发病过程中发挥着重要作用。但由于生理性反流的存在，健康人喉部也可能检测出胃蛋白酶，导致出现假阳性的结果 [ 17 ] 。同时，唾液胃蛋白酶的取样时间点 [ 20 , 22 ] 、取样次数 [ 23 - 24 ] 、诊断阈值 [ 6 , 25 ] 等尚无统一标准。 研究发现，血红蛋白对光波有2个吸收峰：蓝光的415 nm和绿光540 nm。NBI就是利用人体血红蛋白的不同光波频谱吸收峰以及处于这些吸收峰范围内的光波在人体黏膜组织内的穿透深度不同，从而达到内镜下特殊的染色效果，以便于观察。目前，国内外相关文献关于NBI用于LPR诊断的研究中，大多用于描述LPR的体征，且认为NBI下LPR的体征变化比普通白光更明显，评分更高。Galli等 [ 26 ] 利用普通白光和NBI评估儿童LPR患者的体征，发现NBI下RFS评分更高，且结果显著。朱增辉等 [ 27 ] 通过比较儿童LPR患者普通白光和NBI两种模式下的RFS评分及诊断的符合率，发现NBI模式下声带水肿、喉黏膜弥漫性水肿、充血和红斑等方面更明显，也得出了NBI下RFS评分更高，且有显著差异。He等 [ 28 ] 研究发现LPR患者喉黏膜在NBI下特殊的形态学表现为血管增生和绿点，出现率分别为92.9%和88.6%，特异性和敏感性高，可在LPR的辅助诊断中发挥作用。何宁等 [ 29 ] 研究发现NBI在观察黏膜表面血管的变化中有独特优势，恶性肿瘤的表浅血管往往有连续性中断、呈斑片状、袢状等特征性表现，而LPR引起的咽喉部炎症黏膜则表现为表浅血管扩张、紊乱。Galli等 [ 30 ] 发现LPR患者舌扁桃体隐窝炎症和咽后壁鹅卵石样改变在NBI下更明显，且舌扁桃体隐窝炎症的分级与RFS评分之间存在显著相关性，认为NBI可用于LPR的诊断。 另外，本研究也存在一些不足：①我们没有使用咽喉pH监测和唾液胃蛋白酶检测等客观检测手段，仅使用了RSI和RFS量表评分对患者进行分组。由于受主观影响较大，分析LPR组中可能并非所有患者都是真正的LPR患者，同样非LPR组中可能也会存在真正的LPR患者，并且这种主观影响只能通过两位医生对RFS量表进行分别评估后取平均值，以求把主观影响的误差降到最低。②我们所观察的NBI下的特征性表现，对LPR的诊断也不能达到百分之百的正确率。我们寻求的是找到一种方便、快捷、患者接受度高、相对准确的辅助诊断LPR的方法，必要时可联合其他诊断方法。③我们没有具体阐明LPR导致的咽喉部黏膜广泛炎症性改变的机制，即体现在NBI模式下为杓区、杓间区或环后区的稀疏或簇状的细小棕色斑点，是如何通过NBI观察不同形态上皮乳头内毛细血管袢(intraepithelial papillary capillary loop，IPCL)得到的。我们准备在以后的研究中，从不同类型反流物、黏膜组织病理学检查等方面进一步探讨反流性的慢性炎症是如何导致不同形态IPCL的，更进一步解释NBI模式下不同类型棕色斑点的成因。 目前，LPR的客观诊断方法中，大多从检测反流物中的某种物质或离子等方面入手，较少从反流物导致咽喉部损伤后所需观察的最终结果方面入手，本研究中做到了这一点。在研究中，我们还发现了一个问题，有的患者仅杓间区或环后区可见稀疏或簇状的细小棕色斑点，而有的患者在环后区、杓间区及杓区均可见细小的棕色斑点。我们猜测可能与患者的反流次数、反流类型、病程长短等有关系，有实验证明，每周3次LPR事件的发生就会导致严重的喉部损伤 [ 31 ] ，但具体原因还需要国内外学者进一步去探讨、研究和分析。 但是，目前NBI应用于LPR的诊断尚处于研究探索阶段，缺乏大量的临床数据，相信随着研究的深入，NBI会成为一种很有前景的辅助诊断LPR的方法。 Funding Statement 哈医大一院科研创新基金(No: 2021M12) Footnotes 利益冲突 所有作者均声明不存在利益冲突 References 1. 中华耳鼻咽喉头颈外科杂志编辑委员会咽喉组, 中华医学会耳鼻咽喉头颈外科学分会咽喉学组, 中华医学会耳鼻咽喉头颈外科学分会嗓音学组 咽喉反流性疾病诊断与治疗专家共识(2022年, 修订版) 中华耳鼻咽喉头颈外科杂志. 2022;57(10):1149–1172. doi: 10.3760/cma.j.cn115330-20220711-00428. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. 徐 志宇, 刘 旭, 陈 世彩, et al. 咽喉反流性疾病的发病机制及其与耳鼻咽喉疾病相关性研究进展. 听力学及言语疾病杂志. 2022;30(6):587–590. doi: 10.3969/j.issn.1006-7299.2022.06.004. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Irjala H, Matar N, Remacle M, et al. Pharyngo-laryngeal examination with the narrow band imaging technology: early experience. Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol. 2011;268(6):801–806. doi: 10.1007/s00405-011-1516-z. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. B