INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4872,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12230504','Reflections on the Core Concepts of Physiology and Pharmacology.','Michael J, White PJ.','2025','heraclitus ephesus bce silverthorn rang doyen neatly paraphrased 1900s aka nuffield sweeping aka acquaintance bransford useable conditionalized rovick rovick culminated physiologists tangalakis inanimate animate unpacked unpacked conceives cc13 cc2 cc14 cc3 cc15 cc4 cc16 cc5 cc17 cc6 cc18 cc7 cc19 cc8 cc20 cc9 cc21 cc10 cc22 cc11 cc23 cc12 cc24 memorizing factoids regurgitating factoids solvers bransford berne boron boulpaep marieb hoehn silverthorn hierarchically overloaded reinvent memorize decode etherington cliff organismal crosswhite prato boil e70146 prp2 prato prato physoc silverthorn rang rang silverthorn wehrwein famine feast nuzzo chadwick nuffieldfoundation tangalakis lexis hryciw e00836 teqsa teqsa cliff wiggins mctighe duschl schweingruber shouse bransford cocking klahr erlbaum rovick rovick guilding guilding unpacking banning garvin doxas klymkowsky elrod cliff wenderoth ar35 wenderoth cliff martinkova munday feduc babey laubscher facilitative slominski grindberg momsen advan stanescu wehrwein bornmann mutz koeppen berne boron boulpaep marieb hoehn barzansky devi zulkifli binti razali fasinu wilborn e1178 guilding laubscher netere leversha mededpublish dornan ashcroft heathfield danos staab whitenack organismal iob obac019 asbmb educat sonner etherington callan hourani nolton recreating advan cliff organismal advan crosswhite cpe sso cpe','ABSTRACT Core concepts are “big ideas” that can play a number of roles in helping students master a particular discipline: (1) they may define the important content of a discipline, (2) serve as tools to help students gain a better understanding of the discipline, and (3) facilitate transfer of learning between topics in the discipline. The core concepts of Physiology and of Pharmacology have been defined, and their deployment as part of Physiology and Pharmacology pedagogy is well underway. Here we compare and contrast the core concepts of these two disciplines and suggest lessons that have been learned that might assist other disciplines in deploying core concepts in their discipline. Keywords: core concepts, pharmacology education, physiology education The graphical abstract shows an example of a concept map representing the relationships between the core concepts of a discipline. 1. Introduction It is said that Heraclitus of Ephesus, a 5th century bce Greek philosopher, observed that “the only constant in life is change.” In the 21st century, change in the field of science education is present wherever one looks. In this paper we discuss one of the key changes that is occurring in the teaching and learning of two disciplines, Physiology and Pharmacology. In both disciplines attention is turning to a focus on core concepts, and we will discuss the history and the uses of the core concepts of both disciplines. We will then compare the approaches taken to developing and using core concepts by the two disciplines, and offer some thoughts about the lessons that have been learned as both disciplines have sought to reform how students learn. 2. The Disciplines of Physiology and Pharmacology The discipline of Physiology as a research endeavor is grounded in physics, chemistry, and molecular biology. According to The Physiological Society website [ 1 ], physiology “aims to understand the mechanisms of living things, from the basis of cell function at the ionic and molecular level to the integrated behavior of the whole body and the influence of the external environment.” Silverthorn [ 2 ] in her widely used textbook “Human Physiology: An Integrated Approach” describes physiology this way: “Physiology is the study of the normal functioning of a living organism and its component parts, including all of its chemical and physical processes.” Humphrey Rang, a doyen of Pharmacology research and author of a seminal textbook [ 3 ] defines Pharmacology as “the study of the action of drugs on living systems—neatly paraphrased as the chemical control of physiology and pathology”. As is the case for Physiology, the integration of chemistry and biology lies at the heart of the discipline of Pharmacology. However, for Pharmacology, concepts such as structure–activity relationships and drug distribution and metabolism require relevant synthetic chemistry and physical chemistry. Physiology and Pharmacology are therefore related disciplines, with Pharmacology focused on the use of medicines to restore normal physiological function. In the United States, Physiology is taught in all institutions of higher education: community colleges, 4‐year colleges and universities, and graduate and professional schools [ 4 , 5 , 6 ]. The number of physiology (or anatomy and physiology) courses taught and the number of institutions with physiology programs (majors) are difficult to determine, in part because the titles assigned to these programs varied considerably, and in part because there is no national institution to collect such data [ 6 ]. However, there is a report [ 7 ] that the number of exercise physiology graduates in 2021–2022 was over 30 000. Physiology is clearly a popular course of study. A majority of students taking a Physiology course are enrolled in programs to prepare for careers in a variety of health care professions (nursing, physical therapy, and medicine to name just a few). The number of students taking Physiology courses as part of a healthcare‐oriented program is unknown. A small number of undergraduates go on to earn advanced degrees in Physiology or related disciplines. It is thought that the numbers of programs and the number of students enrolled in Physiology courses are increasing [ 8 ]. Pharmacology is similarly taught in all institutions of higher education. There are three broad groups of students who take Pharmacology courses: those undertaking science programs who major or minor in Pharmacology (some go into industry, some to postgrad research, some to health professions postgrad); those pursuing postgraduate (Masters or PhD) qualifications in pharmacology; health professions students completing medical, nursing, pharmacy, dentistry, and veterinary programs. 3. A Brief History of Science Education Reform in the 20th and 21st Centuries Calls for reform of science education in America go back to as early as the 1900s when Dewey [ 9 ] the philosopher and educator observed that “… science has been taught too much as an accumulation of ready‐made material with which students are to be made familiar, not enough as a method of thinking, an attitude of mind, after the pattern of which mental habits are to be transformed.” More than 110 years later this observation sounds very current! A more recent call for reforms in science education in America came in the very influential report “Vision and Change in Undergraduate Biology Education: A Call to Action” [ 10 ]. The working group that authored the final report began its work in 2007. At much the same time, AAAS and the Howard Hughes Medical Institute issued a report [ 11 ] “Scientific Foundations for Future Physicians,” that advanced a similar set of ideas. One of the things that both reports had in common was a call for science education to focus on a set of core concepts (aka “big ideas”) and core competencies rather than the mere accumulation of ever more knowledge. It is worth noting that this concern about the state of science education was not limited to the United States. In 2008 the Nuffield Foundation in England issued a report [ 12 ] on the state of science education in Europe and called for sweeping changes to meet contemporary needs. In Australia, there have been significant changes to science education. International educational research has informed changes at the discipline level, for example, efforts by national teams to develop core concepts in Physiology [ 13 ] and Pharmacology [ 14 ]. More recently, the Higher Education Regulator Tertiary Education Quality and Standards Agency [ 15 ] has required universities to address issues related to generative Artificial Intelligence, both in terms of the integrity of assessments and the development of skills needed to thrive in an AI professional environment. The calls for STEM education to focus on core concepts or big ideas require some definition of a “concept” or a “big idea.” 4. Core Concepts and Understanding Science 4.1. What Are Core Concepts (Aka “Big Ideas”)? It is difficult to define what is meant by a “concept.” Michael et al. [ 16 ] have pointed out that the definitions from philosophy and psychology center on the idea of “categories.” However, this is not what science educators mean by a “concept.” In these fields, a “core concept” is more or less synonymous with a “big idea.” Below are two definitions of a “big idea.” By definition, big ideas [emphasis added] are important and enduring. Big ideas are transferable beyond the scope of a particular unit … Big ideas are the building material of understanding. They can be thought of as the meaningful patterns that enable one to connect the dots of otherwise fragmented knowledge. [ 17 ] Each big idea [emphasis added] is well tested, validated, and absolutely central to the discipline. Each integrates many different findings and has exceptionally broad explanatory scope. Each is the source of coherence for many key concepts, principles and even other theories in the discipline. [ 18 ] The “core concepts” outlined in Vision and Change [ 10 ] are clearly “big ideas” as defined above, and a focus on core concepts is intended to promote student understanding of science. Below we will discuss what it means to understand Physiology and Pharmacology and then summarize the big ideas of these two disciplines. 4.2. What Does It Mean to Understand Something? The STEM educational reforms being advocated were clearly aimed at helping students “understand” science more effectively than the usual pedagogy has been successful at doing. But, what does it mean to “understand” some topic? Dictionary definitions of the term “understand” are not very helpful. The Merriam‐Webster dictionary [ 19 ] offers this definition: “to have a thorough or technical acquaintance with or expertise in the practice of.” The Cambridge dictionary [ 20 ] says: “to know why or how something happens or works.” Philosophy and psychology both have dealt with the problems of defining “understanding” but the breadth of the subject has made it difficult to extract a definition that would help define what STEM practitioners mean by “understanding.” The reform movement described above is predicated on the idea that the simple accumulation of more and more facts about a discipline does not lead to “understanding.” Something more is required. Bransford et al. [ 21 ], educational researchers, have offered a definition that is more helpful: … research also shows clearly that “useable knowledge” is not the same as a mere list of disconnected facts. Experts'' knowledge is connected and organized around important concepts (e.g., Newton''s second law of motion); it is “conditionalized” to specify the contexts in which it is applicable; it supports understanding [emphasis added] and transfer (to other contexts) rather than only the ability to remember. And Simon [ 22 ], a cognitive scientist, has offered this definition: “In broad terms, a person understands [emphasis added] some information to the extent that he or she can use it in performing the tasks for which it is relevant. There is no great difficulty in measuring understanding, thus defined.” 4.3. What Does It Mean to Understand Physiology and Pharmacology? Simon''s definition, of course, leads to another question. What sorts of tasks are relevant to an understanding of Physiology or Pharmacology? What would a student have to be able to do to demonstrate that she “understands” these disciplines? Michael and Rovick [ 23 ] have observed that understanding Physiology may mean any or all of the following depending on the particular course being taught and the goals set by the particular teacher: (1) knowing facts, (2) being able to solve problems [using causal reasoning], (3) having the skill to carry out certain procedures in the laboratory, (4) being able to design experiments to study physiological phenomena, and analyze the results of experiments to define the components of physiological systems and their behavior, or (5) having the ability to analyze certain problems from a physiological perspective. The ability to solve novel problems, ones that have not been presented in class or in the textbook, has certainly been a dominant goal in determining student understanding (see Rovick and Michael [ 24 ] for one approach to carrying out such assessment). There is a lack of discourse in Pharmacology education, and certainly no consensus, regarding what it means to understand Pharmacology. Educators typically focus their efforts on a 2‐part conceptual framework, with the first being the fate of a drug once administered (pharmacokinetics, with its fundamental principles of absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion), and the second being the effects of the drug once it reaches a cell (pharmacodynamics, often focused on interactions between the drug and its targets) [ 25 , 26 ]. Students often need to remember a large number of facts, including long lists of drug names and properties, and often find it challenging to focus on conceptual learning in this context. When students understand Pharmacology, they can “use basic theoretical knowledge to solve problems.” [ 27 ] Many core concepts of Pharmacology are themselves underpinned by concepts in chemistry, mathematics and biochemistry. Health professions graduates need a range of cognitive skills to effectively manage patient medication use and safety [ 28 , 29 ]. 5. A Brief History of the Development of the Core Concepts of Physiology and Pharmacology 5.1. The Core Concepts of Physiology The development of the core concepts of Physiology was stimulated by the same science education reform efforts that sparked the publication of “Vision and Change.” In 2007 the National Science Foundation convened a meeting to discuss “Conceptual Assessment in the Biological Sciences.” [ 30 , 31 ]. One of the first tasks undertaken by the 21 participants (representing many different biological sciences) was to identify the “concepts” that needed to be assessed. In spite of the diversity of the group, a list of eight “big ideas” in biology was quickly agreed on. Following this meeting, Michael et al. [ 32 ] generated a list of nine core concepts of Physiology that were thought to be important for students to understand. Michael and McFarland [ 33 ] then surveyed physiology faculty in the United States and 10 foreign countries, asking respondents, in their own words, to tell the investigators what they thought the “big ideas” of Physiology were. The result was a list of 15 core concepts (“big ideas”) of Physiology. Subsequently, McFarland et al. developed the conceptual framework for homeostasis [ 34 ], followed by the development of a concept inventory for homeostasis [ 35 ]. The group went on to develop conceptual frameworks for cell–cell communication [ 36 ], cell membrane [ 37 ], mass balance [ 38 ], and structure/function [ 39 ]. This work culminated in a book “The Core Concepts of Physiology: A New Paradigm for Teaching Physiology” [ 16 ] which summarized the work done by this group since 2009 and offered preliminary suggestions for implementing the use of core concepts in Physiology courses and curricula. In 2023 a group of Australian physiologists published a total of 12 papers on the core concepts of Physiology (these papers can be found in the core concepts collection of articles on the Advances in Physiology Education website). This group started with the Michael and McFarland [ 33 ] list of core concepts. They identified six of the Michael and McFarland core concepts as being relevant to Australian students studying physiology. The core concepts not put on the Australian list were eliminated, not because it was felt that these concepts were unimportant, but because it was thought that they were learned in other courses taken by their students [ 13 ]. The results of this project would seem to validate the Michael/McFarland list of core concepts of Physiology. The modified Michael/McFarland core concepts [ 40 ] and the Australian list of core concepts [ 13 ] can be seen in Table 1 . TABLE 1. The core concepts of Physiology. Michael and McFarland [ 40 ] Tangalakis et al. [ 13 ] Cell–cell communication Cell–cell communication Cell membrane Cell membrane Cell theory Energy Evolution Flow down gradients Movement of substances Genes to proteins Homeostasis Homeostasis Levels of organization Mass balance Physical properties of matter Scientific reasoning Structure ↔ function Structure and function Systems integration Integration Physiological adaptation Open in a new tab Michael and McFarland [ 40 ] noted that their list of core concepts could be sorted into three categories: core concepts that describe all aspects of the physical world (inanimate and animate), those that describe aspects of the biological world, and ways of looking at the world. In addition, it is important to note that the Michael/McFarland core concepts are overlapping and interrelated. Consider the core concept of homeostasis . Component ideas of this concept include cell–cell communication (e.g., ANS control of many physiological systems) which in turn involves flow down gradients (e.g., the flow of ions down electrochemical gradients across the membranes of nerve and muscle). It is important that students understand these interrelationships as they learn the core concepts themselves. 5.2. The Core Concepts of Pharmacology The development of core concepts of Pharmacology is a more recent initiative. Interestingly, this work started in the area of pharmacy education, with a group of educators who were looking to define the core science concepts of pharmacy [ 41 ]. Early surveys of pharmacy education suggested that educators found it overwhelming to identify core concepts for an entire profession, and the group shifted focus to a single discipline, this time with greater success. A group of educators from Australia and New Zealand pilot tested the methodology to identify 19 core concepts, using a Delphi method [ 14 ]. The same group then defined and unpacked those core concepts using a method adapted from Michael et al. [ 16 ]. Subsequently, an international Core Concepts of Pharmacology (CCP) group was established under the banner of the International Union of Basic and Clinical Pharmacology Education Section (IUPHAR‐Ed). The IUPHAR‐Ed CCP built on the Australasian work to identify 25 core concepts of pharmacology [ 25 ], 15 of which were present in the Australasian study. A novel aspect of the pharmacology core concepts work was the validation of the five criteria used to distinguish core concepts, namely the characteristics useful, enduring, foundational, complex, and challenging [ 42 ]. The IUPHAR‐ED CCP then defined and unpacked the core concepts [ 26 ]. Recently, over 700 students from 11 countries have completed free‐text quizzes requiring not only the definition but also the application of the core concepts in novel scenarios; analyses of the responses identified a large number of misconceptions held by students about these concepts [ 43 , 44 ]. One important aspect of the intended use of the core concepts of Pharmacology is that they are themselves content as well as tools or building blocks, and students should “learn, remember, understand, and apply—in other words, to learn [the core concepts] deeply.” [ 25 ] Remembering the definition of a core concept is not necessarily important for students. However, we argue that graduates in a health professions or scientific workplace won''t be able to use core concepts to solve problems, predict outcomes and be effective practitioners unless they understand the concept deeply. Understanding of dose–response relationships is necessary for prescribers to avoid errors in dose adjustment, for example awareness of the plateau phase helps prescribers understand that simply increasing drug dose may not result in any further beneficial effects for patients and may cause adverse effects. Once learnt, the core concepts can be used in real‐life situations related to medicines and their actions over many years. In that sense, these core concepts are both content in themselves, as well as being tools. Time in the curriculum needs to be devoted to learning the meaning and how to apply these concepts, perhaps best done integrated within a topic for which the core concept is an important tool. Note that this does not mean that IUPHAR‐Ed conceives the core concepts to be organizing structures for the curriculum. The core concepts of Pharmacology, developed by IUPHAR‐Ed CCP, are shown in Table 2 and a concept map showing the relationships between the core concepts is shown in Figure 1 . The concepts can be grouped into three categories: pharmacokinetic concepts, pharmacodynamic concepts, and patient outcome concepts as shown in Figure 1 . Concept mapping [ 45 ] is one of the most effective strategies for facilitating student conceptual understanding, and this map represents one of many possible concept maps that describe the relationships between concepts. Educators looking to establish a conceptual framework for their students could provide them with the core concepts and ask them to produce their own concept maps. TABLE 2. Core concepts of Pharmacology [ 26 ]. CC1. Drug targets CC13. Volume of distribution CC2. Drug–target interaction CC14. Drug metabolism CC3. Structure–activity relationship CC15. First‐ and zero‐order kinetics CC4. Mechanism of drug action CC16. Drug elimination CC5. Dose/concentration–response relationship CC17. Drug elimination half‐life ( t ½ ) CC6. Drug affinity CC18. Drug clearance CC7. Drug efficacy CC19. Steady state concentration CC8. Drug potency CC20. Drug tolerance CC9. Drug selectivity CC21. Adverse drug reaction/drug event CC10. Drug absorption CC22. Therapeutic index CC11. Drug bioavailability CC23. Drug interaction CC12. Drug distribution CC24. Individual variation Open in a new tab FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Concept map showing the core concepts of Pharmacology [ 26 ]. 6. What Problems Are the Core Concepts of Physiology and Pharmacology Meant to Solve? 6.1. Physiology 6.1.1. Students'' Lack of Conceptual Understanding As pointed out above, the stimulus for the development of the core concepts of physiology was the widespread acknowledgment that biology education (and by extension, physiology education) was not helping students develop an understanding of the core concepts of biology or physiology [ 10 ]. In other words, students were very good at memorizing “factoids” and regurgitating them on an exam, but were not building those factoids into robust mental models (concepts) of physiology. When students understand core concepts and recognize where they are applicable, they are better able to build robust mental models of the phenomena being learned. These models will in turn help them to be better problem solvers in the domain. 6.1.2. Physiology Is Hard for Students to Learn When Michael asked Physiology faculty a series of questions about what makes Physiology hard for their students [ 46 ], they responded that the most important source of difficulty was the very nature of the discipline—the need to be able to reason causally about mechanisms. When students in Physiology courses were asked the same questions [ 47 ], they too thought that the nature of the discipline was the most important barrier to their success. A more recent study of student perceptions of core concepts revealed that students viewed the concepts as “tools to reason with” and that these tools provide deeper learning and transfer of knowledge to novel contexts [ 48 ]. It is difficult to know which of the core concepts are most commonly used. Some preliminary survey results [ 49 ] suggest that homeostasis , structure/function , cell–cell communications , and flow down gradients are viewed by faculty and students as being particularly important. If students understand and can apply these core concepts, their ability to understand physiological phenomena and solve problems about these phenomena will be improved. 6.1.3. Student''s Inability to Transfer What They Already Know Transfer of learning is a simple idea with complex consequences. Bransford et al. [ 21 ] offer the following definition; “transfer is the ability to extend what has been learned in one context to new contexts.” For example, if students understand hemodynamics, one would expect these same students to be able to apply their understanding that flow occurs down a pressure gradient to understanding that air flows through the airways down a pressure gradient. However, all too often this transfer does not occur; students seem to learn about airflow as though it were a thoroughly new subject. Michael [ 50 ] has argued that if students understand the core concept of flow down gradients and if they learn to recognize where that concept is applicable, they are more likely to be able to transfer their understanding of flow in the circulation to building an understanding of air flow in the airways. This ability to transfer will contribute to student success in understanding Physiology. 6.1.4. There Is More to Know Than We Can Teach or Students Can Learn The knowledge explosion is real and getting worse [ 51 ]. It is no longer possible for even the expert to know all that there is to know even in their field of expertise. Clearly, students cannot be expected to learn everything known in the discipline they are studying. Five of the most widely used medical physiology textbooks range in length from Berne and Levy [ 52 ] at 864 pages to Boron and Boulpaep [ 53 ] at 1312 pages. A commonly used undergraduate textbook of anatomy and physiology by Marieb and Hoehn [ 54 ] is 1200 pages long, and an undergraduate physiology textbook by Silverthorn [ 2 ] is 992 pages long. There is more in these textbooks than students can learn. Core concepts offer at least a partial solution to this problem. For example, if students studying endocrine physiology understand the core concept of cell–cell communication , it may not be necessary that they learn every hormonal system; the core concept should make it possible for them to understand any endocrine system they might encounter in the future. This is, of course, another example of the power of the core concepts to facilitate transfer of learning. 6.1.5. The Wide Spectrum of Students Taking Physiology Courses As pointed out above, physiology courses are taken by students at every level of American higher education. The learning objectives (expectations) are obviously different at each of these levels. The challenge, then, is to tailor one''s learning objectives to the needs of the students taking your course. The conceptual frameworks that have been developed for six of the core concepts of physiology (see above) are hierarchically organized statements of the component ideas making up the core concept [ 34 ]. This provides faculty with a relatively easy way to tailor the details of a particular core concept to meet the needs of her particular students. 6.1.6. Reduction and Fragmentation of the Basic Science Curriculum at the Professional Level In recent years, the basic science curricula at American medical schools have undergone two profound changes. There is widespread agreement that the time devoted to the preclinical curriculum (where the basic sciences are taught) has decreased [ 55 ]. At the same time, the basic science portion of the medical curriculum has become increasingly fragmented. The number of medical schools whose first‐year curriculum contains disciplinary courses such as Physiology or Pharmacology decreased markedly from 2010 to 2017, replaced by integrated organ system courses [ 56 ]. However, the sequence of organ system courses, and the sequence of topics within each block, is not necessarily presented in a sequence that is optimum for student learning of any particular basic science discipline. There is no reason to believe that this trend has reversed since 2017. When confronting a fragmented exposure to physiology, a focus on the core concepts can help students build robust mental models of physiological phenomena by making more visible the commonalities between seemingly different phenomena. That is to say, by facilitating the transfer of learning, core concepts can help students build the mental models that will let them see the functional relationships between phenomena at the organ system or cell levels. 6.2. Pharmacology 6.2.1. Pharmacology Is Hard to Learn Pharmacology students, like physiology students, have trouble understanding pharmacology and show many misconceptions regarding the core concepts. Students find pharmacology conceptually difficult [ 57 ]. A recent study of student mastery of core concepts found significant gaps in student understanding even after three or four courses of pharmacology, and a large number of misconceptions were identified [ 43 ]. 6.2.2. Pharmacology Courses Have Too Much Content, and the Discipline Knowledge Base Is Growing Pharmacology has traditionally been taught in a content‐rich manner, often involving memorization of a large number of drugs [ 58 ], and details of how they work. A greater understanding of effective teaching methods, supported by evidence regarding the benefits of active learning and other student‐centered approaches, has led many pharmacology educators to prioritize conceptual understanding over factual recall [ 59 ]. Pharmacology curricula have at times been seen by students and educators as overloaded, making it difficult to transfer from didactic to clinical practice [ 60 ]. Recent advances in the nature and mechanism of action of next generation medicines, including biologics and mRNA vaccines, have only enlarged the “potential curriculum.” 6.2.3. New Educators Struggle to Know What Concepts to Teach and Assess The challenges above have often led to frustration and confusion among those new to teaching pharmacology. Core concepts have the potential to act as anchors in the curriculum, as seen in nursing. We note that pharmacology core concepts have been developed for foundational (Pharmacology 101) courses rather than systems pharmacology. In this context, core concepts allow educators to ensure that, if nothing else, they have focused their time on understanding and application of the ideas that matter. 6.2.4. Current Graduates Show Gaps in Pharmacology Understanding and Application Studies of health professional graduates report perceived gaps in pharmacology knowledge [ 60 , 61 ], including the need for “more to be done during undergraduate education to link theory with practice” in order to prevent prescribing errors [ 62 ]. The IUPHAR‐ED project team concluded that “From the discovery of new medicines to safe and effective prescribing, our scientists and health professionals require the ability to apply these enduring ideas years after graduation.” [ 25 ]. 7. What has Been Learned About Adopting A Core Concepts Pedagogy? As more disciplines attempt to define core concepts and introduce them into their courses and curricula, it is important that we learn from another''s experiences doing this. There is no need for each discipline to “reinvent the wheel.” That said, it is important to keep certain things in mind. 7.1. Different Disciplines Define Core Concepts Differently The core concepts (“big ideas”) of Physiology are models or thinking tools that have wide applicability across all areas of physiology [ 40 ]. As such, they aid students in understanding similar phenomena wherever they occur. They also facilitate the transfer of learning from one topic to a similar topic. The core concepts are NOT themselves topics to be covered in a Physiology course or curriculum [ 40 ]. Flow down gradients, one of the most commonly used core concepts, is not a topic that is covered in a Physiology course or textbook per se. There is no expectation that all of the core concepts will be used in any single course or even in the entire curriculum. The core concepts of Pharmacology represent content that should be included in all pharmacology courses and understood by all students [ 25 ]. Unlike physiology, the core concepts of pharmacology need to be learned in their own right. A foundations of pharmacology course would address drug efficacy, potency, and affinity as part of the foundational content, usually involving examples and case studies. We should also point out that several other biomedical disciplines have generated lists of core concepts: anatomy [ 63 ], biochemistry and molecular biology [ 64 ], immunology [ 65 ], and neuroscience [ 66 ]. The definitions of what a core concept is in each of these disciplines vary to a significant extent. It is clear, then, that what each discipline considers a core concept to be depends on many factors, most importantly the nature of the problems that the core concepts are meant to solve (see below). As other STEM disciplines begin to generate core concepts, it is important that each discipline recognize the need for explicit definitions of what they mean by a core concept. It is also important to recognize that while the core concepts of other disciplines may be applicable in your discipline, you must keep in mind the nature (and intended use) of that core concept. For example, there is a fundamental difference between core concepts that are defined as topics to be taught and learned, and those that are defined as tools to facilitate understanding the content, or something in between. 7.2. Different Disciplines Use Core Concepts to Solve Different Kinds of Problems In Physiology the major problems to be solved were a need for students to understand concepts and be able to apply them wherever they are applicable, and not just memorize more facts. This facilitates transfer of learning, reducing the cognitive load of learning our increasing knowledge about physiological function. It also promotes integration of knowledge about organ system function in curricula that are increasingly fragmented. The problems to be solved by core concepts in pharmacology are similar, in that pharmacology graduates need to solve complex, novel patient‐ or drug‐related problems. In order to be successful, students need skills in analyzing novel scenarios, decoding the most important elements, and then utilizing the core concepts to solve the problem. In that sense, core concepts of pharmacology may be seen as cognitive resources or tools that can be employed to decode a complex problem. It is of interest to note that the core concepts of neuroscience [ 66 ] were in part meant to solve a very different kind of problem. Neuroscience is a relatively young discipline and a very broad one encompassing biology, psychology, and one that is taught at the undergraduate level in a variety of departments and programs. The core concepts are, in part, an attempt to define what students should learn, regardless of the program they are in. What follows from the differences facing each discipline is that the core concepts they develop will be used differently. 7.3. How Core Concepts Are Used Can Be Different The core concepts of Physiology are beginning to be used as organizing tools for a number of different reasons and in different ways. Etherington et al. [ 67 ] have designed a core concept based introductory course aimed at “helping students think like a physiologist.” Ball [ 68 ] has designed an introductory course build around the core concepts with the aim of enabling “transfer of knowledge across the curriculum.” Cliff [ 69 ] is using core concepts in an attempt to engender “integrated learning” in an introductory organismal biology course. Crosswhite and Anderson [ 70 ] have reported on the implementation of four quite different core concept organized courses. Although the four courses were in different kinds of Institutions and served kinds of students the reports from the faculty showed a number of similarities. Similar, but not identical, sets of core concepts were emphasized; there was considerable overlap in the perceived strengths of the core concept approach, and similar challenges were identified. Physiology educators will know more about how to employ core concepts most effectively in their courses as more faculty make the required changes and report their experiences. In pharmacology, educators have been working to integrate the core concepts into foundational courses in pharmacology for a range of health and science disciplines [ 57 ]. 7.4. Who Is Going to Implement the Reforms? Education reform can be driven from two directions: from the top (Deans, department chairs etc.) down (course directors, individual faculty), and from the bottom up (individual faculty interacting with one another). In most institutions, some combination of both approaches is most productive. Ultimately the goal is to educate individual faculty about the core concepts approach to teaching their discipline and the various ways in which it can be implemented. There are many different players in this process. 7.5. What Role Can or Should Professional Societies Play? Professional societies can play a significant role in advancing the educational reform agenda. In Physiology, the American Physiological Society and the activities of its Center for Physiology Education have played a major role in introducing physiology faculty to the core concepts of physiology via sponsoring the publication of the core concepts book [ 16 ], creation of online modules about the core concepts [ 71 , 72 ], and programming at its annual meetings. In Pharmacology, the International Union of Basic and Clinical Pharmacology Education Section adopted the Core Concepts of Pharmacology project in 2021, and the project is supported and managed under the banner of that organization. This leadership from the international society has been critical in the recruitment of new educators to the program, and in funding conferences in Prato, Italy in 2022 and 2024. 8. Conclusions There are many different reasons why practitioners in biomedical disciplines make the decision to identify and utilize the core concepts of their discipline. Physiology was concerned about a lack of student understanding of the core concepts and the students'' inability to transfer what they already know to new situations. Pharmacology discovered that its graduates were functioning poorly in their careers because of a lack of understanding of the core concepts of their discipline. Neuroscience was concerned that the scientific breadth of their discipline and the various contexts in which it was being taught led to students not understanding important ideas in the discipline. All three of these reasons ultimately boil down to a concern about what students were learning. As teachers, our responsibility is to help them learn to learn [ 72 ] and to do so in a way that supports whatever future goals the student might have. With the explosion of scientific knowledge, it is essential that students understand and can apply the core concepts wherever they are applicable, whether in the classroom or in their future careers. However, educational reform is difficult and slow to occur. Departments and their faculty have taught a certain way for a long time, and change requires extra time and effort. There are a variety of ways in which the implementation of a core concept—focused course or curriculum can be accomplished. Professional societies can play a major role in educating their members about this new paradigm for teaching their discipline. They can also make a significant contribution to the creation of a community of practitioners who are using or want to use the core concepts in their teaching. But regardless of the difficulty, adopting a core concept‐focused approach to teaching the biomedical sciences offers the possibility of significant rewards in the form of improved student learning. Author Contributions Both the authors made substantial contributions to the conception and writing of the commentary; have been involved in drafting the manuscript or revising it critically for important intellectual content; and given final approval of the version to be published. Each author have participated sufficiently in the work to take public responsibility for appropriate portions of the content; and agreed to be accountable for all aspects of the work in ensuring that questions related to the accuracy or integrity of any part of the work are appropriately investigated and resolved. Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Acknowledgments Open access publishing facilitated by Monash University, as part of the Wiley ‐ Monash University agreement via the Council of Australian University Librarians. Michael J. and White P. J., “Reflections on the Core Concepts of Physiology and Pharmacology,” Pharmacology Research & Perspectives 13, no. 4 (2025): e70146, 10.1002/prp2.70146. Portions of this paper were first presented as a lecture to the “Concepts‐based pharmacology education: Teaching learning and assessment tools” workshop, Monash University Prato Center, Prato, Italy July 11, 2024. Data Availability Statement The authors have nothing to report. References 1. “The Physiological Society,” 2025, http://www.physoc.org . 2. Silverthorn D. U., Human Physiology: An Integrated Approach, 8th ed. (Pearson, 2019). [ Google Scholar ] 3. Rang H. P., Dale M. M.