INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4903,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1568880','Cell-mediated immunity and its application in toxicology.','Luster MI, Dean JH, Boorman GA.','1982','lymphoproliferation radiometric immunotoxic boorman luster myelotoxicity luster boorman leubke gell hypersensitivities bfb0030490 koller methylmercury lefford tuberculin luster boorman luebke padarathsingh luster halogenated aromatics tb56628 vadas whitelaw radioisotopic','Abstract A variety of in vivo and more recently in vitro assays have been described to assess cell mediated immunity (CMI). Two methods routinely employed in our laboratory to assess CMI following exposure to chemicals in rodents include delayed hypersensitivity and in vitro lymphoproliferation. Preliminary studies indicate that depressed delayed hypersensitivity responses, as performed by a radiometric assay, correlates with altered susceptibility to infectious agents and tumor cell challenge following exposure to immunotoxic chemicals. Furthermore, suppression of T-cell lymphoproliferative responses to at least 50% below control values correlated with depressed delayed hypersensitivity responses and altered host susceptibility. On the other hand, when suppression of T-cell lymphoproliferative responses are within 50% of control values, delayed hypersensitivity and host susceptibility parameters are not affected. Assuming adequate technical expertise and accurate data interpretation, CMI assays of these types can provide a valuable data base for toxicology studies and immunotoxicity assessment. Full text PDF Previous 31 32 33 34 35 36 Next Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Boorman G. A., Luster M. I., Dean J. H., Campbell M. L. Assessment of myelotoxicity caused by environmental chemicals. Environ Health Perspect. 1982 Feb;43:129–135. doi: 10.1289/ehp.8243129. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Dean J. H., Luster M. I., Boorman G. A., Leubke R. W., Lauer L. D. Application of tumor, bacterial and parasite susceptibility assays to study immune alterations induced by environmental chemicals. Environ Health Perspect. 1982 Feb;43:81–88. doi: 10.1289/ehp.824381. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Godfrey H. P., Gell P. G. Cellular and molecular events in the delayed-onset hypersensitivities. Rev Physiol Biochem Pharmacol. 1978;84:1–92. doi: 10.1007/BFb0030490. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Koller L. D. Some immunological effects of lead, cadmium and methylmercury. Drug Chem Toxicol. 1979;2(1-2):99–110. doi: 10.3109/01480547908993184. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Lefford M. J. The measurement of tuberculin hypersensitivity in rats. Int Arch Allergy Appl Immunol. 1974;47(4):570–585. doi: 10.1159/000231251. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Luster M. I., Boorman G. A., Dean J. H., Harris M. W., Luebke R. W., Padarathsingh M. L., Moore J. A. Examination of bone marrow, immunologic parameters and host susceptibility following pre- and postnatal exposure to 2,3,7,8-tetrachlorodibenzo-p-dioxin (TCDD). Int J Immunopharmacol. 1980;2(4):301–310. doi: 10.1016/0192-0561(80)90030-2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Luster M. I., Faith R. E., Clark G. Laboratory studies on the immune effects of halogenated aromatics. Ann N Y Acad Sci. 1979 May 31;320:473–486. doi: 10.1111/j.1749-6632.1979.tb56628.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] MacLean L. D. Host resistance in surgical patients. J Trauma. 1979 May;19(5):297–304. doi: 10.1097/00005373-197905000-00001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Vadas M. A., Miller J. F., Gamble J., Whitelaw A. A radioisotopic method to measure delayed type hypersensitivity in the mouse. I. Studies in sensitized and normal mice. Int Arch Allergy Appl Immunol. 1975;49(5):670–692. doi: 10.1159/000231449. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Vos J. G. Immune suppression as related to toxicology. CRC Crit Rev Toxicol. 1977 May;5(1):67–101. doi: 10.3109/10408447709101342. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',7,'2025-10-15 10:19:49','2025-10-15 10:19:49');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4904,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9560879','The effect of COVID-19 on radiology postgraduate training in Ireland.','McDonnell C, Lambe G, Hutchinson B.','2022','s11845 deskilling workspaces statdx radiopaedia intradepartmental bsti formalised bsti cvcx0 pcvcx0 nchd emotive unexampled subscriptions statdx radiopaedia pressurised radiopaedia statdx nimis rotas enthusiastically veerasuri e877 e814 alvin aboutus bsti bsti bsti bsti bsti bsti feeney preach covid_19_24 loughlin creedon kagetsu kanfi flink covid19ireland geohive','Abstract Background The COVID-19 pandemic had a significant impact on the healthcare provision, and while it has also had unprecedented effects on medical education and training, the extent has not been fully evaluated. Aims We wished to assess the effects of COVID-19 on postgraduate radiology training, and due to the structure of postgraduate radiology training in Ireland, this setting allows for the surveying of an entire national cohort of trainees due to the relatively small national population and centralised national training body. Methods A 70-question survey, covering 11 areas of the training experience, was devised. The survey was reviewed by the national trainee committee and approved by the national training and education committee for radiology. This was distributed to all radiology trainees ( n = 124), who were given 2 weeks to submit responses anonymously. The survey was not mandatory. Results Out of 124 trainees, 64 (51.6%) submitted responses. A total of 37.5% of respondents felt that their workload had decreased, 23.5% reported that they had been required to take greater than 7 days of leave due to COVID-19 (either primary infection or required isolation due to ‘close contact’), 77% felt that their subspecialty rotations had been significantly impacted by COVID-19, and 56.3% of respondents reported a worsening in their sense of wellbeing due to the COVID-19 pandemic. Conclusion Our survey, which, to our knowledge, is unique in its representation of the entirety of a national postgraduate training programme, has demonstrated trainees’ attitudes that there has been a significant, multifaceted impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on the quality of their training. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s11845-022-03190-9. Keywords: COVID-19, Medical education, Postgraduate training Introduction The landscape of healthcare service provision internationally has been significantly impacted by the COVID-19 pandemic. However, the effects of the COVID-19 pandemic on medical training have been less widely discussed. The speciality of radiology has had a unique role during the COVID-19 crisis, given the function that diagnostic imaging and minimally invasive intervention have to play in the management of patients with COVID-19 and its secondary effects. However, concern has been voiced regarding the possible deskilling of trainees due to a large volume of day-to-day acute reporting being primarily COVID-19-related. This complex area is difficult to accurately evaluate; however, internationally, groups of trainees have been directly consulted to quantify any effects that they have subjectively perceived [ 1 , 2 ]. The Irish setting of radiology specialist training affords a unique opportunity to assess a national radiology training programme in its entirety due to the relatively small national population. With 124 trainees distributed throughout 9 major training centres, performing a survey with balanced representation of all trainees was achievable to evaluate the effect of the COVID-19 pandemic on training opportunities, workload, and burnout, on the entire national Irish radiology trainee cohort [ 3 ]. Materials and methods A 70-question survey was devised by the national radiology trainee committee and approved by the national radiology training and education committee. The survey covered the topics of routine workload, on-call workload, subspeciality training, redeployment, teaching, examinations, conferences, multidisciplinary team meetings, COVID-19-specific reporting, trainee wellbeing, and trainee perspectives ( Supplementary Material ). The survey was created using Google forms and distributed online to all radiology trainees in the Republic of Ireland on the 7th of March 2021, with the survey remaining open to responses for 2 weeks until the 22nd of March 2021. The survey was not mandatory, and respondents could choose to omit answers to any questions they wished. Anonymity was protected, and all respondents consented to the processing of the data provided within the limits of the survey. Results Responses were obtained from 64 of 124 (51.6%) of all radiology trainees in the Republic of Ireland, with representation from every major training centre in the country. There was a relatively even distribution of responses from each site (Fig. 1 ), varying from the smallest proportion of respondents from a single centre making up 6.3% of the entire cohort, in comparison to 17.2% being the largest proportion from a single centre. With regards to seniority of respondents, the largest proportion of respondents were in their first year of training (Fig. 2 ); however, there was representation from all 5 years of training. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Number and distribution of respondents by respective training sites Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Responses from trainees asked to comment on whether departmental workload had increased or decreased during the COVID-19 pandemic. Respondents who had not been working in their department prior to the department were not included Workload and absences When asked about workload, 37.5% of respondents felt that the workload in their department had decreased overall, in comparison to 21.9% who felt that it had increased. With regards to workload during on-call shifts, the majority (61%) of respondents felt that workload had not increased. The minority who reported an increase in workload was, however, not negligible, at 38.6%. A total of 15.6% of respondents described an increase in frequency of on-call shifts due to absences secondary to COVID-19. While the majority (56.3%) of respondents did not report any absence secondary to COVID-19 (including due to suspected or confirmed infection, or exposure to a known case necessitating self-isolation), almost a quarter (23.5%) reported a significant number of days (i.e. greater than 7 days) lost due to COVID leave, with 9.4% absent from work for over 14 days. The majority of respondents (89.1%) reported that there is no contingency in place to cover COVID-related absence at their institution. Only 2 respondents out of 64 (3.1%) were offered locum rates when having to cover a colleague’s on-call shift due to COVID-related absence. Subspeciality rotations There is particular emphasis on special subspeciality in both paediatric radiological and neuroradiology as part of the higher specialist training scheme in radiology in the Republic of Ireland. This is provided either locally in certain large centres, or in the form of off-site rotations in paediatric radiology in one of the two main paediatric hospitals in Dublin, and a rotation in Beaumont Hospital in Dublin, one of the country’s two main centres and the main national referral centre for neurology and neurosurgery [ 4 ]. While the majority of respondents had not undergone their paediatric rotation during the pandemic (51.6%), 77% of those who had did not feel that the rotation had been significantly affected by COVID-19. Those who did feel the rotation had been negatively affected mainly referenced the reduced exposure to patient-facing modalities such as ultrasound and fluoroscopy and, to a lesser extent, reduced consultant-led teaching. These modifications reflect recommended restrictions secondary to social distancing in workspaces. A single respondent stated that their rotation had been delayed, and another stated that the length of time spent on their rotation had been reduced. The neuroradiology subspeciality rotation appeared to be more significantly affected by COVID-19-related restrictions. Half of the respondents who had been due to undergo their rotation during the pandemic stated that the rotation had been negatively affected. A total of 82% of respondents who had been due to undergo their rotation stated that it had either been delayed or cancelled. One respondent stated that, due to delays, they will not carry out the rotation before pursuing a fellowship internationally and will, therefore, not receive the expected degree of exposure to neuroradiology. There were some positive effects on subspeciality training secondary to COVID-19 described by respondents. Primarily, these involved the growth of online educational resources, as well as access over Zoom, Microsoft Teams, or other video conferencing software to lectures and tutorials provided by the Irish Faculty of Radiology and international educational resources. Redeployment Four out of 64 (6.3%) respondents underwent redeployment. Only 1 of these 4 respondents (25%) stated that they did not want to be redeployed but felt that they did not have a choice in the matter, whereas the remaining 75% were ‘happy to help’ where appropriate. Of note, no training (online or otherwise) was offered prior to redeployment, and respondents were provided with a maximum of 2 days’ notice prior to redeployment. Education and examinations A total of 57.8% of respondents felt that consultant-led teaching had decreased during the pandemic. Fortunately, all respondents reported that formal teaching had continued to some extent throughout the pandemic, and video conferencing software appears to have played a significant role in allowing this. The Faculty of Radiologists in Ireland has provided all trainees with access to StatDx during the course of their training. A total of 96.8% of respondents stated that they have found this either helpful or extremely helpful, during the COVID-19 pandemic. A selected number of trainees have also been granted full access to the online resources of Radiopaedia. One hundred percent of these respondents (36 trainees) have found this either helpful or extremely helpful. Of the 64 respondents, 24 (37.5%) had completed Fellowship examinations (either FFR RCSI primary (part 1) or final part 2A/2B) during the COVID-19 pandemic. Of these 24, 13 (54.2%) stated that the experience of an entirely online-format examination went better than they had expected. There were 2 (8.3%) respondents who stated that they had a worse experience than they had expected. In comparison to a traditional exam format, 21 (87.5%) felt that the examination experience was similar or better, whereas 3 (12.5%) felt that the online experience was worse than a traditional experience. Conference/multidisciplinary team meetings We asked participants about their experiences related to scientific conferences (either national or international) and presenting local multidisciplinary team meetings (MDMs) during the pandemic. Fifty-seven of 64 (89.1%) respondents had attended a scientific conference online during the pandemic, with 27 of these (47.4%) describing the experience as worse than a scientific conference in person. In contrast, 11 respondents (17.1%) described an improved experience than previous conferences in person. The experience in relation to MDMs appears largely similar to that of scientific conferences. There were 35 of 64 (54.7%) respondents had presented at least one MDM during the pandemic, with 52.8% describing a worse experience than traditional MDMs presented in person. A large minority (41.4%) described the experience as similar, with 5.6% of respondents reporting an overall improved experience. Reporting of COVID-19 imaging Out of the 64 respondents, 43 (67.2%) felt confident in reporting chest radiographs with findings potentially secondary to COVID-19, with this number increasing to 57 (89.1%) in relation to CTs with COVID-19 features. This reflects 30 of 64 (46.9%) respondents stating that they received no formal intradepartmental training in COVID-19 reporting, with 13 (20.3%) describing teaching in their department to be primarily registrar-led, and a further 13 (20.3%) describing self-directed learning, such as the resources provided by the British Society of Thoracic Imaging (BSTI) [ 5 ]. The majority (56.3%) of respondents stated that there was no formalised or template format of COVID-19 reporting in their department. This increases to 81.7% of respondents who stated that BSTI COVID-19 report codes (such as CVCX0/PCVCX0) [ 6 ] were not utilised in their department, and only 2 respondents of 64 (3.1%) stating that all registrars and consultants consistently utilise the standardised report codes. Wellbeing Non-consultant hospital doctor (NCHD) wellbeing has been an emotive and much-discussed issue in Ireland (and internationally), in recent decades [ 7 ]. As such, we felt that it was integral to include an assessment on the effect that COVID-19 has had on radiology trainee wellbeing during these previously unexampled times. The majority of respondents (56.3%) experienced a worsening in their wellbeing during the pandemic. There were 10 respondents out of 64 (15.6%) felt the need for additional support in their current role as a radiology trainee during the pandemic. While 77.5% of respondents who required time off during the pandemic felt supported in this situation, the remainder (22.5%) did not. Of note, the cause of time off (i.e. COVID-19 infection, self-isolation due to exposure or symptoms, or other need for time off) was not delineated in our survey. Trainee perspective Out of the 64 respondents, 35 (54.7%) felt that their training in radiology had been negatively impacted by the effects of the COVID-19 pandemic (Fig. 3 ), with a further 21 (32.8%) trainees feeling that it remains too early to comment on potential negative impacts. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Responses from trainees when asked if their overall wellbeing had improved or worsened during the course of the COVID-19 pandemic Only 8 out of 64 trainees (12.5%) did not feel that their training has been negatively impacted. Fourteen trainees (21.9%) expressed an interest in the option of extension of the training scheme to mitigate the negative effects (Fig. 4 ). Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Responses from trainees when asked if their training had been impacted negatively by the COVID-19 pandemic Respondents were offered the opportunity to add any additional comments or suggestions in the final section of the survey. A number of themes were identified in the responses received. For instance, trainees requested that lectures provided online by consultants on behalf of the Faculty of Radiology be recorded. Trainees also requested access to more online resources (including paid subscriptions to services such as StatDx and Radiopaedia) and a preference for more structured teaching/curriculum. Concerns were also raised over the reduced case mix due to prevalence of COVID-relating imaging and a relative decrease in trainees having face-to-face time with consultants due to social distancing. Discussion Our study aimed to assess the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on radiology training and on trainee wellbeing. The survey was distributed to every radiology trainee in the country. Responses were obtained from sixty-four trainees (52%). To the authors’ knowledge, this makes our study the largest of its kind in radiology. A statement from the Faculty of Radiologists during the first wave of infection in March 2020 made a series of recommendations on patient screening for COVID-19 infection, reporting of imaging findings, prioritisation of imaging examinations, and infection control within radiology departments [ 8 ]. Among these recommendations was the advice that social distancing should be practised, elective and non-urgent examinations should be deferred, and conferences should be held using video conferencing software where possible. The wearing of masks at all times in the workplace soon became another feature of the pandemic response. While earlier studies have assessed the impact of the first wave on radiology trainees [ 1 ] and radiographers [ 9 ], our study was conducted a year to the month from Ireland’s first confirmed case of COVID-19 infection and also in the midst of Ireland’s third and most severe wave to date. Conducting the survey at this time allowed us to capture the experience of first-year trainees who, having started their training in July 2020, had never known radiology training outside of the pandemic. We were also able to capture the responses of second- to fourth-year trainees and to compare their experiences both before and during the pandemic. The 1-year time point allowed us to capture a full annual exam cycle, to explore trainees’ experiences of the Primary and Final Fellowship of the Faculty of Radiologists (FFR) exams using video conferencing software, and to compare these with their prior exam experiences. It allowed us to capture a full annual conference cycle, to explore trainees’ experiences of online conferences, and to compare these with their experiences of traditional conferences. Finally, it enabled us to incorporate the intended period of subspecialty (i.e. neuroradiology and paediatrics) rotations for a large number of trainees and, therefore, to assess the impact of the pandemic on subspecialty training. The experience of Irish trainees was similar in many ways to that of United Kingdom (UK) trainees [ 1 ]. The majority of respondents felt that the overall department workload had either decreased or stayed the same. This was most likely due to a combination of reduced emergency department attendances, postponed elective investigations, and increasingly labour-intensive image acquisition. For example, two radiographers were required to perform portable radiographs outside of the radiology department. A similar majority of Irish and UK trainees reported that their overall wellbeing was worse than before the pandemic began. The pandemic has had an adverse effect on subspecialty training. Half of all respondents who were due to undergo their neuroradiology training during the pandemic said that their rotation had been affected. The most common reasons cited were a delayed rotation and a shortened rotation. Almost a quarter of respondents who were due to undergo their paediatric training during the pandemic said that their rotation had been affected. However, the most common reasons cited were reduced exposure to modalities such as ultrasound and reduced consultant-led teaching. The disproportionately large effect of the pandemic on subspecialty rotations may reflect the fear of cross-infection between hospitals. Similarly to UK trainees, almost half of Irish trainees reported that they had received no formal departmental training on reporting COVID-19 imaging. A similar proportion reported that communication in relation to changing imaging pathways had been suboptimal. Trainees reported that some areas of their training had improved since the start of the pandemic. These were broadly similar to those of UK trainees and included less pressurised reporting, access to online resources such as Radiopaedia and StatDx, as well as tutorials and exams using video conferencing software. In other areas, the experience of Irish trainees differed from that of their UK counterparts. A far smaller proportion of Irish trainees were redeployed to other areas of the hospital. This most likely reflected the greater severity of the UK’s first wave. Irish trainees reported a larger reduction in consultant-led teaching than UK trainees but a similar reduction in peer-to-peer teaching. This may reflect adherence to the faculty’s advice that departments should facilitate remote reading where possible. The overall reduction in consultant-led teaching in Ireland, however, was likely balanced by the greater availability of trainees to attend whatever tutorials were available because of lower levels of redeployment. Our study of Irish trainees offered some new insights compared with previous studies. There was a high level of satisfaction with the FFR exams which were conducted for the first time using video conferencing software. More than half of trainees felt that the experience was better than expected. Almost 90% said that the experience was similar or better than traditional exams. Online tutorials were also well received by trainees. Many reports have highlighted the different ways in which radiology training can adapt to the challenges of the pandemic [ 10 , 11 ]. Video conferencing software may become the new normal for teaching and examining in postgraduate training, especially considering the flexibility it offers to trainees and trainers alike. The Faculty of Radiologists has adapted by providing scheduled lectures and course days online for the foreseeable future. Unlike the positive experiences trainees report with online exams, respondents were less enthusiastic about online multidisciplinary team (MDT) meetings. The majority of respondents found this experience worse than traditional meetings. Better integration of video conferencing software with the National Integrated Medical Imaging System (NIMIS) is one measure that may help to improve trainees’ experiences. Most trainees also found online conferences to be a worse experience to traditional in-person conferences. The size of the trainee cohort is one potential limitation of this study. Sixty trainees did not respond and were therefore not represented. However, 51.6% was considered an adequate response rate, particularly given the survey was voluntary and anonymous and considering that there was representation from every Irish training centre. The highest proportion of responses came from first years who had only experienced radiology training during the pandemic. Their experiences and responses may not be representative of many more senior trainees who did not respond. Six of the nine training centres surveyed were in Dublin. There have been regional variations in COVID-19 infections across the country, but the highest number of cases and deaths overall has occurred in Dublin [ 12 ]. For this reason, our results may not be fully generalizable. In addition, there were significant variations in the way each individual hospital responded to the challenges of COVID-19 in terms of training, rotas, redeployment, and day-to-day work practices. Any individual’s experience of the pandemic will have been affected to some extent by factors unique to their own training centre. Our nationwide survey of postgraduate trainees shows that the COVID-19 pandemic has had a significant effect on radiology training and trainee wellbeing. However, online training resources, tutorials, and exams conducted remotely using video conferencing software have been enthusiastically embraced by trainees. The lessons learnt from the last 12 months should serve to prepare radiology trainers and trainees for the future challenges of this and other health crises. Supplementary Information Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary file1 (PDF 371 KB) (370.7KB, pdf) Declarations Informed consent and ethics Participants provided informed consent prior to undertaking the survey, and permission was granted for publication of results. Ethical approval was not required due to the observational nature of the study. Conflict of interest The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Veerasuri S, et al. Impact of COVID-19 on UK radiology training: a questionnaire study. Clin Radiol. 2020;75(11):877.e877–877.e814. doi: 10.1016/j.crad.2020.07.022. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Alvin MD, et al. The impact of COVID-19 on radiology trainees. Radiology. 2020;296(2):246–248. doi: 10.1148/radiol.2020201222. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. HSE National Doctors Training & Planning (2021) Medical Workforce Report 2020–2021 4. Beaumont Hospital - Welcome (2021) Beaumont.ie. Available from: http://www.beaumont.ie/aboutus . Accessed 3 May 2021 5. COVID-19 Resources (2021) The British Society of Thoracic Imaging. Bsti.org.uk. Available from: https://www.bsti.org.uk/covid-19-resources/ . Accessed 3 May 2021 6. COVID-19 BSTI Reporting templates (2021) The British Society of Thoracic Imaging [Internet]. Bsti.org.uk. Available from: https://www.bsti.org.uk/covid-19-resources/covid-19-bsti-reporting-templates/ . Accessed 3 May 2021 7. Feeney S, et al. Practise what you preach: health behaviours and stress among non-consultant hospital doctors. Clin Med (Lond) 2016;16(1):12–18. doi: 10.7861/clinmedicine.16-1-12. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Faculty of Radiologists (2020) The Faculty of Radiologists statement on COVID-19 management in radiology departments. https://radiology.ie/images/Covid_19_24.02.2020.docx . Accessed 5 May 2021 9. Foley SJ, O’Loughlin A, Creedon J. Early experiences of radiographers in Ireland during the COVID-19 crisis. Insights Imaging. 2020;11:104. doi: 10.1186/s13244-020-00910-6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Chong A, Kagetsu NJ, Yen A, Cooke EA. Radiology residency preparedness and response to the COVID-19 pandemic. Acad Radiol. 2020;27:856–861. doi: 10.1016/j.acra.2020.04.001. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. England E, Kanfi A, Flink C, et al. Radiology residency program management in the COVID era - strategy and reality. Acad Radiol. 2020;27:1140–1146. doi: 10.1016/j.acra.2020.05.001. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Ireland''s COVID-19 Data Hub. COVID-19 Ireland, Government of Ireland. Available at: https://covid19ireland-geohive.hub.arcgis.com/ . Accessed 13 Oct 2022 Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary file1 (PDF 371 KB) (370.7KB, pdf)',16,'2025-10-15 10:19:49','2025-10-15 10:19:49');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4905,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11333716','The evaluation of digital educational game use in pharmacology teaching process.','Granat MM, Paź A, Mirowska-Guzel D.','2024','hypolipidemic kahoot gbl kahoot drbg gbl preset gbl endanger unintelligible uninteresting therapeutical gbl enthusiastically apprised beginnings drbg drbg cryptography randomize randomlist_1 group_no 6for group_no list_1 append shuffle list_1 group_1 list_1 len list_1 group_2 list_1 len list_1 group_1 group_2 kahoot kahoot projector whiteboard kahoot kahoot gameplay puzzles kahoot cellphone pseudonyms gameplay kahoot aliases puzzles hypolipidemic hypolipidemic randomlist_1 questions_no 30for questions_no list_1 append shuffle list_1 test_1 list_1 len list_1 test_2 list_1 len list_1 len list_1 test_3 list_1 len list_1 test_1 test_2 test_3 statistica lilliefors lilliefors equaled hypolipidemic lilliefors equaled hypolipidemic equaled hypolipidemic lilliefors equaled equaled gbl repeatable dependently gbl gudadappanavar gbl pharmacyphlash hasbro calco pharmacyphlash braincept motivator kahoot imitating poker imitate millionaire kahoot gbl gameplay kahoot leaderboard motivator leaderboard gbl kahoot kahoot prp2 e1237 prp2 e1237 andrzej cacko granat mirowska guzel e1237 prp2 bangalee oosthuizen perumal suleman pharmacyphlash gudadappanavar benni javali karbownik wiktorowska owczarek kowalczyk kwarta mokros pietras fnw045 cued zafeiropoulos kalles sgourou kameas adventure telel lelli gamut haranath brunton knollmann aynsley braincept poh gnatyuk okhrimenko tynymbayev iavich prng cryptographic secur hamed abdolhamid gpu prng cuda fortran aip soundeffects tincher odeng otu herdman persky stegall zanation correll kahoot nadeem oroszlanyova farang secur escudeiro sgames aslan balci gamed prp2 e1237 prp2 e1237','Abstract The aim of the study was to determine the usefulness of educational video games as a variety applied to the traditional teaching of pharmacology in topics of antifungal and hypolipidemic drugs. Sixty‐six volunteers were divided into control group ( n = 33) and experimental group ( n = 33). The first group received traditional seminar only, and the second played video game designed with the use of Kahoot! platform after the seminar. The assessment of knowledge was conducted with the use of three tests: pre‐test was presented before seminar, post‐test after seminar (for control group) or after playing the video game (for experimental group), and test no. 3 took place 4 weeks later. Analysis of tests'' scores with respect to both topics resulted in the observation that the control and experimental groups show statistically significant improvement in knowledge, whether it was measured after the seminar or 4 weeks later. However, the results of experimental group were statistically better in comparison to the control group. It proves that electronic GBL (game‐based learning) applied to standard educational processes increases short‐ and long‐term knowledge retention compared to traditional seminars. Keywords: game‐based learning, Kahoot!, pharmacology education A scheme presenting information about the tests determining students'' knowledge and questionnaire survey. Abbreviations DRBG deterministic random bit generator GBL game‐based learning 1. INTRODUCTION An educational game can be defined as a tool that requires learner participation in competitive activity with preset rules. 1 In medical sciences, electronic game‐based learning (electronic GBL) has been present since the 1960s, but nowadays, it is gaining more attention as an educational method. 2 Electronic educational games are becoming known as useful tools in the teaching process, and they have proved efficacy. They increase learners'' interest in the topic and motivate them to learn more than traditional methods. 2 Educational games also create risk‐free environment for decision‐making, 3 which is crucial in the field of medical sciences where mistakes may endanger human''s health or life. Electronic educational games provide competition among participants and reduce feelings of stress and anxiety, thereby increasing knowledge retention. 3 Their use in teaching process seems to be well‐based, as we know that traditional education alone is often less engaging and presents discussed topics in unintelligible or uninteresting way. 4 Electronic educational games apply to different type of classes: laboratory, theoretical, and theoretical‐practical. 5 , 6 , 7 However, despite many advantages, there is a small number of research concerning how to develop and effectively use educational games, which limits their role in teaching process. 4 Among many branches of medicine that need to be learned by medical students is pharmacology. 8 It is a part of science that concerns drugs: their origin, chemical structure, pharmacokinetics, therapeutical properties, mechanisms of action, and toxicity. 9 Large volume of knowledge makes pharmacology difficult to remember and attractive field for electronic GBL. Although some were developed with the aim to learn pharmacology, 1 , 2 , 3 , 10 their number is still small, and they are not widely used. It is worth to develop new educational games as they are enthusiastically perceived by students: according to research over 90 percent of them are interested in playing as a part of the teaching process. 11 2. OBJECTIVE AND METHODS The aim of the study was to determine the effect of self‐made educational game in teaching pharmacology in reference to short‐ and long‐term knowledge. The study was conducted in relation to two basic pharmacological topics to determine if the study results depend on the addressed topic. Additionally, we wanted to gain students'' point of view and their perception of such kind of education in standard classes. The study was conducted with the participation of volunteers among third‐year medical students of the Medical University of Warsaw. The research was positively reviewed and accepted by the Bioethics Committee of Medical University of Warsaw in document no. KB/156/2022. Before the study began, informed consent was acquired from all participants. Every student was apprised that the results of the study would not affect their marks during the course of pharmacology and the final exam and that quitting the experiment is possible at any time without consequences. 2.1. Randomization procedure Randomization procedure was based on the algorithm written in Python programming language version 3.11.2. Python can be described as high‐level programming language of general purpose that emphasizes code readability. The beginnings of Python reach the late 1980s, but version 3.0 of this programming language is dated December 3, 2008, and is under constant development, for this study, Python''s property to use a deterministic random bit generator (DRBG) to provide proper randomization for this study was used. This method is sufficient for the purposes of this study as DRBG is successfully used in, for example, cryptography, Monte Carlo experiments, and electronic games. 12 , 13 , 14 The algorithm used to randomize participants to control and experimental group is presented below: import randomList_1 = []Group_No = 6for i in range(1, Group_No + 1): List_1.append(i)random.shuffle(List_1)Group_1 = sorted(List_1[:len(List_1)//2])Group_2 = sorted(List_1[len(List_1)//2:])print(f"Experimental group is: {Group_1}")print(f"Control group is: {Group_2}") 2.2. The Kahoot! platform While both control and experimental groups participated in the standard pharmacology seminar, the last‐mentioned group additionally played educational games using Kahoot!, an online game‐based platform. It was developed as a project that demands from users to gather around projector, computer monitor, or interactive whiteboard. The choice to apply Kahoot! to this study was made based on the platform''s features: its ease of access, user‐friendly interface, understandable rules, high popularity, being free of charge for study participants, and great software stability. It is worth mentioning, that from a game designer perspective, Kahoot! is available in two versions: as a free platform and as a paid one with subscription. The latter version includes more gameplay options (e.g., puzzles), and this variant was used in our study. The costs of the subscription were covered by the Medical University of Warsaw. All Kahoot!‐based game participants need to have any sort of mobile device (e.g., cellphone) to connect to each other using a generated game PIN and later to answer questions during the game. It is worth mentioning that when players are connected, they can choose their own pseudonyms, so they are anonymous during the gameplay. The Kahoot! games allow competition: users gain points for the correct answers and for the time how fast they act. At the end of the game, a ranking with chosen aliases and achieved points is displayed. During the study, the game took place after the seminar and consisted of 10 tasks, among which multiple and single choice tests, true or false sentences to choose, and puzzles were present. Each task had a time limit to answer, and when it passed, or all participants gave answers, the correct one was displayed. To eliminate the effect of many academic teachers that could interfere with the analysis, there was only one person who gave seminars and assisted with the educational game during the study. 2.3. Tests determining students'' knowledge To determine short‐ and long‐term knowledge retention, three tests composed of 10 single‐choice questions were applied. For one correct answer, the participant acquired one point, so 10 points in each test could be gathered at the maximum. Pre‐test was presented to all students before the seminar. In the control group, a post‐test was carried out after the seminar and in the experimental group, after the educational game. What is more, test no. 3 was implemented as well, and it took place 4 weeks after the day when pre‐ and post‐tests were presented (Figure 1 ). Short‐term knowledge retention was assessed by comparison of pre‐test and post‐test scores. Long‐term knowledge retention, on the other hand, was determined by analysis of test no. 3 scores. FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab A scheme presenting information about the tests determining students'' knowledge and a questionnaire survey. During the study, participants were engaged in two seminars relative to two different pharmacological subjects: antifungal and hypolipidemic drugs. The idea was to compare tests concerning this both subjects to determine if the educational game''s effectiveness will change depending on the seminar subject. Course of pharmacology for third‐year medical students consists of topics of various duration: they are taught over the time of one, two, or three seminars. Antifungal and hypolipidemic drug subjects were chosen randomly among the group of one seminar topics, and they represent similar difficulty levels and comparable amounts of knowledge to learn. Because each subject needed to take three tests, 30 questions were prepared for one topic. Questions were designed to represent the same level of difficulty and their assignment to pre‐test, post‐test, or test no. 3 was randomized with the use of Python algorithm: import randomList_1 = []Questions_No = 30for i in range (1, Questions_No + 1): List_1.append(i)random.shuffle(List_1)Test_1 = sorted(List_1[:len(List_1)//3])Test_2 = sorted(List_1[len(List_1)//3:len(List_1) //2 + 5])Test_3 = sorted(List_1[len(List_1)//2 + 5:])print(f"Pre-test is composed of questions: {Test_1}")print(f"Post-test is composed of questions: {Test_2}")print(f"Test no. 3 is composed of questions: {Test_3}") All the tests were printed and solved by participants individually and anonymously. To avoid false results, all participants were advised that in case of not knowing the answer, the sentence “I don''t know” should be written or no answer should be given rather than choosing the random one. 2.4. Questionnaire survey To gain information about the game from the participants'' point of view, the anonymous questionnaire survey was printed and presented to each representative in the experimental group after the post‐test (see Supporting Information S1 ). 2.5. Statistical analysis Statistical analysis was conducted using Statistica software version 13.3. For all calculations, a 95% confidence interval of the difference was applied so p < .05 was considered statistically significant. For calculations, five statistical tests were applied. To determine if data are distributed normally, Lilliefors test and Shapiro–Wilk test were used. The Friedman test was applied to non‐independent data represented by pre‐test, post‐test, and test no. 3 scores within groups. This test allowed us to calculate mean scores as well as mean ranks for the aforementioned tests. The Wilcoxon signed‐rank test was subsequently employed to determine if tests within their groups were statistically different. Terminally, the Mann–Whitney U test was used for independent data analysis such as comparison of pre‐tests, post‐tests, and tests no. 3 scores between the control and experimental groups. 3. RESULTS The study was conducted on six seminar groups of students, 5 of 11 students and 1 of 12 students, and 1 student refused participation in the study. Three students'' groups were randomized to the control group ( n = 33), and another three to the experimental group ( n = 33). All volunteers ( n = 66) who signed an informed consent took part in the study as no one quit the experiment. This allowed us to conduct the research as planned, and participants were successfully divided into control group ( n = 33) and experimental group ( n = 33). It is important to note that our sample size selection was based on maximal number of students who could be taught by single academic teacher. In the academic year 2022/2023, from which the data are presented, one academic teacher could only assist 66 students during the pharmacology course. 3.1. Evaluation of antifungal drugs in study and control group Data sets present in pre‐test, post‐test, and test no. 3 were analyzed to determine if normal distribution is present. According to the Lilliefors test and Shapiro–Wilk test, data sets in all tests in the conducted study were non‐normally distributed. 3.1.1. Comparison within groups Friedman test analysis of pre‐test, post‐test, and test no. 3 results in the control group resulted in mean scores respectively 2.42, 5.30, and 5.58. While the mean rank of pre‐test scores was 1.18, post‐test, and test no. 3 scores were, respectively, 2.35 and 2.47. Median values were 3, 5, and 6 (Figure 2 ). To determine if test results differ among themselves, a Wilcoxon signed‐rank test was applied. Statistical analysis concluded that there was a statistically significant difference between pre‐test scores compared to post‐test scores ( p < .001) and test no. 3 scores ( p < .001). No statistically significant difference was observed between post‐test and test no. 3 scores ( p = .558). The analysis of the tests in the control group revealed that both questions nos. 5 and 7 of the pre‐test (presented respectively as questions nos. 1 and 2 in Supplementary Materials ) appeared to be the most difficult and were answered correctly by only one person. On the other hand, question no. 1 of the post‐test (presented as question no. 3 in Supplementary Materials ) was the one answered correctly by 29 participants, which is the biggest number. FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Median values of all three tests on the topic of antifungal drugs in experimental and control groups ( p —Mann–Whitney U test; minimal and maximal values presented in brackets). Similar outcome was recognized in relation to the experimental group where mean scores of pre‐test, post‐test, and test no. 3 were respectively 2.39, 8.73, and 8.09. The mean rank of pre‐test scores was 1.03, but in the case of post‐test and test no. 3 the results were 2.55 and 2.42, respectively, while median values equaled 3, 9, and 8 (Figure 2 ). Wilcoxon signed‐rank test analysis brought the conclusion that a statistically significant difference was present between the results of pre‐test scores compared to post‐test scores ( p < .001) as well as to test no. 3 scores ( p < .001). However, no statistically significant difference was observed between post‐test and test no. 3 scores ( p = .114). In the experimental group, the analysis of tests resulted in the conclusion that the question no. 7 of the pre‐test (presented as question no. 2 in Supplementary Materials ) were answered correctly only by one participant. Questions no. 1, 8, and 9 of the post‐test and question no. 10 of the test no. 3 (presented subsequently as questions no. 3, 4, 5, and 6 in Supplementary Materials ) were in turn answered correctly by 32 participants, which is the biggest number. 3.1.2. Comparison between study and control group The Mann–Whitney U test analysis resulted in the observation that no statistically significant difference between pre‐test scores in the control and experimental groups is present ( U = 538.5, p = .939). On the other hand, post‐test scores in both groups were significantly different ( U = 71, p < .001), and the same result was observed for test no. 3 scores ( U = 180, p < .001; Figure 2 ). 3.2. Evaluation of hypolipidemic drugs in study and control group Lilliefors test and Shapiro–Wilk test were applied to analyze data sets distribution in pre‐test, post‐test, and test no. 3, which resulted in the observation that the aforementioned data are non‐normally distributed. 3.2.1. Comparison within groups For pre‐test, post‐test, and test no. 3 in the control group, the Friedman test was applied. Mean scores were calculated and equaled respectively 2.52, 5.45, and 5.52, while median values were 2, 6, and 6 (Figure 3 ). Other values were obtained in the case of mean ranks that were respectively 1.15, 2.36, and 2.48. Subsequently, the Wilcoxon signed‐rank test was applied, which resulted in the observation of a statistically significant difference between pre‐test and post‐test scores ( p < .001) as well as between pre‐test and test no. 3 scores ( p < .001). No statistically significant difference was present between post‐test and test no. 3 scores ( p = .538). The analysis of tests in the control group resulted in the conclusion that question no. 9 of the pre‐test (presented as question no. 1 in Supplementary Materials ) was answered correctly by no one. On the other hand, question no. 1 of the post‐test (presented as question no. 2 in Supplementary Materials ) was the one answered correctly by the biggest number of participants, 32. FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Median values of all three tests on the topic of hypolipidemic drugs in experimental and control group ( p —Mann–Whitney U test; minimal and maximal values presented in brackets). Comparable observations were noticed in relation to the experimental group. Friedman test analysis showed that mean scores for pre‐test, post‐test, and test no. 3 equaled, respectively, 2.76, 8.52, and 8.85 (median values were 3, 8, and 9) (Figure 3 ). Mean ranks were also calculated, and their values were 1.00, 2.41, and 2.59. Afterward, Wilcoxon signed‐rank test analysis was applied, which proved a statistically significant difference between pre‐test and post‐test scores ( p < .001) and between pre‐test and test no. 3 scores ( p < .001). No statistically significant difference was observed between post‐test and test no. 3 scores ( p = .271). In the experimental group, the analysis of tests resulted in the conclusion that question no. 9 of the pre‐test (presented as question no. 1 in Supplementary Materials ) was answered correctly only by one participant. Questions no. 2 and 6 of the post‐test and questions no. 1 and 6 of the test no. 3 (presented subsequently as questions no. 3, 4, 5, and 6 in Supplementary Materials ) were in turn answered correctly by all 33 participants. 3.2.2. Comparisons between control and study groups The Mann–Whitney U test was applied and resulted in the observation that no statistically significant difference between pre‐test scores in control and experimental groups is present ( U = 498.5, p = .555). However, post‐test scores in both groups were significantly different ( U = 25.5, p < .001), and the same result was observed for test no. 3 scores ( U = 19.5, p < .001; Figure 3 ). 3.3. Comparison of total results on hypolipidemic and antifungal drugs Because the study concerned two pharmacological topics, but the methodology and tools to obtain results were the same, data from two experimental groups were finally analyzed together as one pre‐test, post‐test, and test no 3. The same action was applied to two control groups. To determine data sets distribution Lilliefors test and Shapiro–Wilk test were used, which resulted in conclusion that non‐normal distribution is present in both, experimental and control, groups. Pre‐test, post‐test, and test no. 3 scores in control group were analyzed with the use of Friedman test, which resulted in mean scores, respectively, 2.47, 5.38, and 5.55. Mean ranks calculated for the aforementioned tests equaled: 1.17, 2.36, and 2.48, while median values were 2.5, 6, and 6. The Wilcoxon test was applied to pre‐test and post‐test scores as well as pre‐test and test no. 3 scores resulted in observed statistically significant differences ( p < .001). No significant difference was present when between post‐test and test no. 3 scores ( p = .411). Similar conclusions were drawn for the experimental group. Friedman''s test applied to pre‐test, post‐test, and test no. 3 resulted in mean scores of 2.58, 8.62, and 8.47, and mean ranks equaled 1.02, 2.48, and 2.51, respectively. Median values were 3, 9, and 9. Wilcoxon test conducted to compare pre‐test and post‐test scores concluded a statistically significant difference ( p < .001), and the same result was observed for pre‐test and test no. 3 scores ( p < .001). However, no significant difference was present between post‐test and test no. 3 scores ( p = .601). Subsequently, the Mann–Whitney test was used to compare pre‐test scores between experimental and control groups, which concluded in no statistically significant difference ( U = 2086.5, p = .677). On the other hand, a statistically significant difference was observed between post‐test scores ( U = 203.5, p < .001) as well as test no. 3 scores ( U = 411, p < .001). 3.4. Questionnaire survey results Questionnaire survey (see Supporting Information S1 ) was presented to the experimental group to gain information about students'' impressions of the educational game. One hundred percent ( n = 33) of respondents answered “Yes” to questions: Was the educational game a worthwhile supplement of standard pharmacology education from student''s point of view? Did the educational game deliver entertainment? Were the educational game''s rules understandable? Was it understandable, how the educational game is connected to pharmacology course? Would you recommend the educational game to other students? 93.94% ( n = 31) respondents answered “Yes” and 6.06% ( n = 2) answered “Not exactly” to one question Was the educational game the incentive to further self‐dependent studying? 4. DISCUSSION The study resulted in conclusion that the use of electronic GBL alongside with traditional education methods has a positive impact on short‐ and long‐term knowledge retention in comparison to standard education only. Although there was a study that measured educational game effectiveness on short‐ and long‐term knowledge retention, 3 it was limited only to one topic related to antimicrobial drugs. In aforementioned research short‐term knowledge retention was assessed by statistical analysis of two tests presented to participants before and after playing the educational game. Long‐term knowledge retention on the other hand was represented as the mean ratio of correctly answered questions at pharmacology examination not later than 14 days after the educational intervention. The results showed that educational game alongside seminar‐based lectures improves short‐term knowledge retention, but statistically effects achieved by game‐based approach were comparable to results obtained by traditional teaching methods. No statistically significant differences were noted. While long‐term knowledge retention also improved in both groups, the playing one was statistically better than control group. In our study, the research was broaden to two topics with the intention to examine if results are repeatable dependently to the topic. Analysis of pre‐test, post‐tests, and tests no. 3 scores in experimental and study groups in regard to both pharmacological topics were conducted. The results proved that regardless of topic, the groups involved in the teaching process with the use of electronic GBL performed better than seminar‐based groups. If restricted to short‐term knowledge, our study results are similar to research done by Gudadappanavar et al. 2 and Lee et al., 4 where pre‐ and post‐tests enabled the measurement of knowledge retention. Akin study was presented by Jones et al. 15 and it concerned game about autonomic nervous system drugs. While this research applied pre‐ and post‐tests as well as other described studies, it additionally took under consideration participants'' examination mean score. Although it was statistically higher in group playing the game, this study only provided information about short‐term knowledge because volunteers could play the game even a day before proceeding to the examination. Our previous experience shows that determining knowledge retention is better achieved by tests that do not affect pharmacology course grades than by examinations for which students receive mark. It is so because in case of a grade‐free test, if student does not know the answer she or he can leave the question without any response. That situation is rarely possible during grade‐based examinations when passing the course is at stake, and students will rather choose random answers in the hope of getting a better score, which may lead to false study results. Among research that attempted to evaluate the electronic GBL effect on the pharmacology teaching process are also studies that only gather information about player''s experience during time spent with educational games. At this point, one can mention PharmacyPhlash, 1 which is an educational game developed as a modification of commercially available games like Trivial Pursuit (Hasbro) and 30 Seconds (Calco). The theme of PharmacyPhlash was not restricted by any specific drug group and pertained to whole pharmacology. To receive students'' feedback about the game, a questionnaire was applied, which resulted in reported high levels of players'' satisfaction. Similar research was conducted using Braincept, a game designed to teach drug mechanisms of action and side effects. 10 Players'' perceptions of the game were collected using a 5‐point Likert scale, which resulted in positive reception. In this research players'' feedback on the game was also collected. It was achieved using the questionnaire survey and analysis of the responds. The results indicated that every person who played the game fully understood its rules and relevance to seminars'' topics and perceived this form of interactive teaching as positive variety. It is worth taking into consideration that minority (in the number of two) of students did not find the game as a sufficient motivator to continue learning aforementioned pharmacology subjects on their own. During this study, the game was developed using Kahoot! Platform, but there are other possibilities available. Pharmacology can also be taught using educational games designed as role‐play games, 10 board games, 1 , 3 , 15 or imitating Poker, 16 so creating games of different types and conducting research on them is recommended. Another factor that may differentiate the games is their theme. Some may represent a form of traditional quizzes, 17 and another may imitate popular television shows like “Who Wants to Be a Millionaire” 2 or “Name That Tune”. 18 Exploring the educational values of games with different themes may help to distinguish the ones that are the most beneficial for didactic purposes and, in turn, indicate what type of educational games are best to develop in the future. At this point, it is important to highlight that understanding the rules of the games based on television shows or anything based on popular culture may differ depending on participants'' ethnic group. It is worth mentioning that conducting this kind of research has its obstacles. One of them is the necessity for players to possess a mobile electronic device (e.g., mobile phone) that is required to log in and play Kahoot! game. What is more, players must have at least basic digital skills, and their devices need access to the internet. Compared to the interactive theoretical‐practical classes, GBL is more engaging through the presence of the competitive element as well as by linking subject content to the gameplay. 19 What is more, the Kahoot! game formula involves a leaderboard element, which is a powerful motivator. 20 It is important to notice that players'' scores displayed on the leaderboard are anonymous, so users remain unidentified, which creates a stress‐free environment, especially for those with lower scores. On the other hand, digital educational games have some disadvantages. They require specialized hardware and software that may be expensive. In some cases, theoretical‐practical classes may be perceived as preferable didactic tool over digital educational game. It is so, because designing the game properly is time‐consuming. 21 Nevertheless, our study is one of only a few that shows advantages of playing game in pharmacology education. Certainly, our results are preliminary and the weakness of the study is a small number of participating students as well as the limitation to two topics of pharmacology. It would be interesting to conduct future research to determine educational game effects on other, different, and related topics. Further research is needed to show the efficacy of electronic GBL, especially in long‐term knowledge retention, to introduce the method as standard in everyday educational practice. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS M.M.G. prepared the questions for Kahoot! game and evaluation of students'' perception of the game, conducted the study, performed the statistical analysis, and prepared the manuscript. D.M.‐G. designed the study, performed statistical analysis and interpretation of the results, supervised the whole study, and creation of the manuscript. A.P., designed the study and prepared questions for tests and the Kahoot! game and interpreted the results. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT The authors declare no conflict of interest. ETHICS STATEMENT The study was approved by the Bioethics Committee of the Medical University of Warsaw in document no. KB/156/2022. Supporting information Supporting Information S1. PRP2-12-e1237-s002.docx (12.4KB, docx) Supporting Information S2. PRP2-12-e1237-s001.docx (14.3KB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS Special thanks to Professor Andrzej Cacko for his excellent statistical support. Granat MM, Paź A, Mirowska‐Guzel D. The evaluation of digital educational game use in pharmacology teaching process. Pharmacol Res Perspect. 2024;12:e1237. doi: 10.1002/prp2.1237 DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. REFERENCES 1. Bangalee V, Oosthuizen F, Perumal‐Pillay VA, Suleman F, Walters F. Pharmacy students experience with PharmacyPhlash—a pilot educational board game. Curr Pharm Teach Learn. 2021;13(3):292‐301. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Gudadappanavar AM, Benni JM, Javali SB. Effectiveness of the game‐based learning over traditional teaching‐learning strategy to instruct pharmacology for Phase II medical students. J Educ Health Promot. 2021;10:91. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Karbownik MS, Wiktorowska‐Owczarek A, Kowalczyk E, Kwarta P, Mokros Ł, Pietras T. Board game versus lecture‐based seminar in the teaching of pharmacology of antimicrobial drugs—a randomized controlled trial. FEMS Microbiol Lett. 2016;363(7):fnw045. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Lee J, Cowan M, Gorman L. A pilot study comparing cued versus recognition recall question design on medical student utilization, effectiveness, and perceptions of pharmacology educational games. Med Sci Educ. 2019;29(4):901‐904. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Zafeiropoulos V, Kalles D, Sgourou A, Kameas A. Adventure‐style serious game for a science lab. Vol 8719. EC‐TELel; 2014; Lecture Notes in Computer Science:538‐541. [ Google Scholar ] 6. Gui Y, Cai Z, Yang Y, Kong L, Fan X, Tai RH. Effectiveness of digital educational game and game design in STEM learning: a meta‐analytic review. Int J STEM Ed. 2023;10(36):1‐25. [ Google Scholar ] 7. Gomes RF, Lelli V. GAMUT: GAMe‐based learning approach for teaching unit testing. Proceedings of the XX Brazilian Symposium on Software Quality. Vol 27. ACM; 2021:1‐11. [ Google Scholar ] 8. Haranath PS. Medical curriculum and pharmacology: an appraisal. Indian J Pharm. 2016;48(Suppl 1):S10‐S13. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Brunton LL, Knollmann BC. Goodman & Gilman''s: the Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics. McGraw Hill; 2023. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Aynsley S, Crawford R. Pilot evaluation of medical student perception of a novel pharmacology‐based role‐play game: Braincept. Educ Health (Abingdon). 2017;30(1):97‐98. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Chang HY, Poh DY, Wong LL, Yap JY, Y