childhood pneumonia analysis based on ultrasonic imaging texture features. Symposium on medical information processing and analysis, 2015. [ Google Scholar ] 36. Arroyo J, Marini TJ, Saavedra AC, et al. No sonographer, no radiologist: new system for automatic prenatal detection of fetal biometry, fetal presentation, and placental location. PLoS One 2022;17:e0262107. 10.1371/journal.pone.0262107 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary data bmjopen-2022-061332supp001.pdf (49.7KB, pdf) Supplementary video bmjopen-2022-061332supp002.mp4 (56MB, mp4) Supplementary data bmjopen-2022-061332supp003.pdf (25.8KB, pdf) Supplementary video bmjopen-2022-061332supp004.mp4 (7.8MB, mp4) Supplementary video bmjopen-2022-061332supp005.mp4 (4.9MB, mp4) Reviewer comments bmjopen-2022-061332.reviewer_comments.pdf (177.4KB, pdf) Author''s manuscript bmjopen-2022-061332.draft_revisions.pdf (6.3MB, pdf) Data Availability Statement Data are available upon reasonable request.',908,'2025-10-15 10:20:35','2025-10-15 10:20:35');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4919,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10461267','Transformer versus traditional natural language processing: how much data is enough for automated radiology report classification?','Yang E, Li MD, Raghavan S, Deng F, Lang M, Succi MD, Huang AJ, Kalpathy-Cramer J.','2023','diverticulitis vectorization expecteddataset qtim transformer_vs_traditional_nlp diverticulitis appendi divertic ischem menisci menisc digestible excerpt vectorization negation vectorization nltk mark_negation _neg appended negation punctation containingall excerpt tokenized dmis cased berttokenizer excerpt padded keras padding tokenized pytorch tensors dataloader preprocessed inputted diverticulitis xgbclassifier sklearn randomforestclassifier linear_model logisticregression hyperparameter subsample internode solver lbfgs liblinear solver hyperparameters defaults hyperparameters tokenized inputted dmis cased bookscorpus adamw optimizer scheduler dataloader hyperparameters hyperparameters usedwhen preprocess excerpt excerpt excerpt tokenized padded excerpt preprocessed retrained resampled iterated matplotlib subsampling subsampled scipy correnction statisitical 15kb diverticulitis vectorization diverticulitis 60e 67e 38e 23e 86e 71e 61e 56e 81e diverticulitis subsampled multiinstitutional barash senders jujjavarapu chilakapati tackles jkc sundry athinoula martinos p41eb015896 p41 nibib gpu mgh bwh eyang12 mgh mdli1 ualberta shruti raghavan sraghavan3 mgh francisdeng mlang mgh succi msucci ambrose ajhuang mgh jayashree kalpathy jkalpathy mgh kalpathy mgh hunink mgm kors zech titano badgeley schefflein kehl elmarakeby nishino lepisto hassett langlotz moradzadeh artmed hassanpour langlotz amrhein befera lungren lungren vaswani shazeer uszkoreit drozdov szubert carlin chilakapati bow xplordat bow btz682 alkasab kalpathy succi buch rincon a6961 kalpathy loper sebastopol guestrin sigkdd loshchilov bfgs evco_a_00168 neumark barash guralnik soffer shimon s00234 senders karhade mehrtash lamba dirisio hassanpour heagerty rundell suri huhdanpaa jujjavarapu pejaver heagerty s188 duma grivas s12911 grivas duma s12880 15kb','Abstract Objectives: Current state-of-the-art natural language processing (NLP) techniques use transformer deep-learning architectures, which depend on large training datasets. We hypothesized that traditional NLP techniques may outperform transformers for smaller radiology report datasets. Methods: We compared the performance of BioBERT, a deep-learning-based transformer model pre-trained on biomedical text, and three traditional machine-learning models (gradient boosted tree, random forest, and logistic regression) on seven classification tasks given free-text radiology reports. Tasks included detection of appendicitis, diverticulitis, bowel obstruction, and enteritis/colitis on abdomen/pelvis CT reports, ischemic infarct on brain CT/MRI reports, and medial and lateral meniscus tears on knee MRI reports (7,204 total annotated reports). The performance of NLP models on held-out test sets was compared after training using the full training set, and 2.5%, 10%, 25%, 50%, and 75% random subsets of the training data. Results: In all tested classification tasks, BioBERT performed poorly at smaller training sample sizes compared to non-deep-learning NLP models. Specifically, BioBERT required training on approximately 1,000 reports to perform similarly or better than non-deep-learning models. At around 1,250 to 1,500 training samples, the testing performance for all models began to plateau, where additional training data yielded minimal performance gain. Conclusions: With larger sample sizes, transformer NLP models achieved superior performance in radiology report binary classification tasks. However, with smaller sizes (<1000) and more imbalanced training data, traditional NLP techniques performed better. Advances in knowledge: Our benchmarks can help guide clinical NLP researchers in selecting machine-learning models according to their dataset characteristics. Introduction Natural language processing (NLP) techniques have been used successfully to perform classification tasks in radiology for various applications. 1 With the advent of artificial intelligence and textual representation technology, supervised machine-learning-based NLP approaches, such as those employing deep neural networks, support vector machines, and tree-based classifiers, have demonstrated high performance in extracting information from radiology reports. 2–7 In recent years, the transformer, a class of deep-learning model, has emerged as a leading approach for NLP by achieving unprecedented accuracy and training speed in certain linguistic tasks. 8 The transformer architecture has demonstrated potential utility in both supervised and unsupervised classification tasks in radiology. 9 However, transformer models generally depend on large datasets for training, on a scale often not available to radiology researchers. With the growing number of NLP models and text embedding frameworks, selecting the best approach for a specific application may be difficult, given varying dataset characteristics and compute resources. While many different methods have been compared in the broader NLP field, the unique linguistic style of radiology texts and the relatively small size of available clinical data require further investigation of design choices in radiology NLP. 10 In this study, for abnormality classification tasks on three radiology report datasets, we investigated the impact of training sample size on the performance of non-deep-learning-based machine-learning classifiers using bag-of-words text vectorization and a deep-learning-based transformer model pre-trained on biomedical text, BioBERT. 11 We hypothesized that traditional NLP techniques may offer comparable or superior performance over transformers for smaller radiology report datasets. This analysis will help to guide future clinical NLP research using radiology reports, by giving researchers a starting frame of reference for the expecteddataset sizes needed. Methods and materials This retrospective study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of (anonymized hospital system) and is compliant with the Health and Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, with informed consent waived. Sample code from analyses for this study is available at https://github.com/QTIM-Lab/Transformer_vs_Traditional_NLP Abdomen/Pelvis CT Data Collection, Annotation, and Filtering Free-text CT report impressions for abdomen/pelvis, abdomen only, and pelvis only studies, with or without intravenous contrast, were collected at the emergency radiology division of a large academic hospital (anonymized hospital name) from January first, 2018, to August 14, 2020. The impressions were previously manually annotated by a fellowship-trained emergency radiologist for the presence of acute appendicitis, acute diverticulitis, and bowel obstruction, with the addition of annotations for colitis or enteritis for this study. 12 Sentences containing the strings “appendi”, “divertic”, “bowel”, “obstruct”, “colitis”, or “enteritis” were extracted from the reports using the re (v2.2.1) regular expression operations library in Python (v3.7.1). In total, 2,448 CT reports from 2,271 unique patients were used, of which the reports were randomly split 80:20 at the patient level for training and testing, with 1,959 reports for training and 489 reports for testing. Head CT and brain MRI data collection, Annotation, and Filtering Free-text head CT and brain MRI report impressions were collected at a large academic hospital (anonymized hospital name) and its affiliated outpatient imaging centers from March 1 to April 30 of each year from 2017 to 2020. The impressions were previously manually annotated by diagnostic radiology residents using the entire report for the presence of acute or subacute ischemic infarction. 13 The studies included non-contrast head CTs, contrast-enhanced head CTs, CT angiograms, non-contrast brain MRIs, and contrast-enhanced brain MRIs. Sentences containing the strings “infarct” or “ischem” were extracted from the reports, also using the re library in Python. In total, 1,987 reports from 1,904 unique patients were used, of which the reports were split 80:20 at the patient level for training and testing, with 1,523 reports for training and 381 reports for testing. A dataset from a different hospital with different radiologists was used for external model testing. Free-text head CT and brain MRI impressions with the same inclusion criteria were collected at another large academic hospital (anonymized hospital name) and its affiliated imaging centers from April 2019 and April 2020. 13 In total, 1,974 reports from 1,514 unique patients were used as the external testing dataset. Knee MRI data collection, Annotation, and Filtering Free-text knee MRI reports were collected at a large academic hospital (anonymized hospital name) and its outpatient imaging centers from January 1, 2018, to December 31, 2018. The reports were previously manually annotated by diagnostic radiology residents for the presence of meniscal tears using the entire report, with separate labels for the medial and lateral menisci. 14 Sentences containing the string “horn” or words that began with “menisc” were extracted from the reports, also using the re library in Python. In total, 3,587 knee MRI reports from 3,411 unique patients were used in this study, of which 2,727 reports were used for training and 684 reports for testing. Radiology text preprocessing To make the free texts digestible for the non-deep-learning models, each training excerpt was represented as vectors using bag-of-words vectorization with N-grams ( N = 1, 2, and 3, minimum term frequency 5%), which is an approach that has demonstrated success in radiology NLP classification tasks. 14 Negation-sensitive vectorization was performed using the NLTK (v3.5) package and its mark_negation function, where “_NEG” was appended to strings found between a negation term and punctation. 15 This process generated a dictionary of terms containingall N-grams of various length based on the training data, which is then used as reference during model testing. Separately, in preparation for the deep-learning model, each training excerpt was tokenized with the “dmis-lab/biobert-base-cased-v1.1” pretrained BertTokenizer from the transformers (v4.5.1) package. 11 Special tokens “[CLS]” and “[SEP]” were added to indicate the start of a classification task and the end of an excerpt. Each sentence was padded to the length of the longest sentence using the keras package (v2.4.3), where an attention mask was implemented to distinguish word tokens and padding tokens. The tokenized sentences were then converted to PyTorch (v1.8.1) tensors. A dataloader instance with batch size of 32 was created for each training and testing dataset. NLP model training Traditional NLP models The bag-of-words preprocessed reports from the training dataset for each abnormality were inputted into extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), random forest, and logistic regression machine-learning models for the classification of the presence of their respective abnormalities, which included acute appendicitis, acute diverticulitis, bowel obstruction, colitis or enteritis, acute or subacute ischemic infarct, lateral meniscus tear, and medial meniscus tear. 16 The models were built with XGBoost (v0.90) package’s XGBClassifier and sklearn (v0.24.1) package’s ensemble.randomForestClassifier and linear_model.LogisticRegression. For each model, a randomized search strategy was implemented to optimize the model’s F1 score given a defined hyperparameter space. For the XGBoost models, the subsample ratio of columns when constructing each tree (uniform distribution between 0.01 and 1), the step size shrinkage used in updates (uniform distribution between 0 and 1), the maximum depth of a tree (uniform discrete random variable between 3 and 11), and the minimum sum of instance weight needed in a child node (uniform discrete random variable between 1 and 11) were tuned. For the random forest models, the number of trees in the forest (uniform discrete random variable between 200 and 2000 spaced by 5), the maximum depth of the tree (uniform discrete random variable between 10 and 100 spaced by 10, plus no constraint), the minimum number of samples required to be at a leaf node (uniform discrete random variable between 1 and 11), and the minimum number of samples required to split an internode 2,5,10 were tuned. For the logistic regression models, the solver used for optimization (“newton-cg”, “lbfgs”, “liblinear”), the solver’s regularization penalty (L1, L2, None), and the inverse of regularization strength (log uniform continuous random variable with a = 1e-5 and b = 100) were tuned. All other hyperparameters for each model were kept as defaults. For each model type, the hyperparameters that yielded the highest F1 score during training were selected in the final model. Transformer deep-learning model The tokenized reports from the training dataset for each abnormality were inputted into the pretrained transformer model “dmis-lab/biobert-base-cased-v1.1” with a single linear classification layer on top. BioBERT is a contextualized word representation model that is pre-trained on English Wikipedia, BooksCorpus, PubMed abstracts, and PubMed Central full-text articles. 11 The classification layer was trained for four epochs. The AdamW optimizer (learning rate = 2e-5, term added to denominator to improve numerical stability = 1e-8) was used alongside a linear learning rate scheduler, where the number of training steps is the length of the dataloader multiplied by the number of epochs. 17 Empirically, we found that tuning the BioBERT hyperparameters for these tasks did not substantially improve performance. Thus, default hyperparameters were usedwhen training the BioBERT deep-learning model. NLP model testing To preprocess the testing data for the non-deep-learning models, each testing excerpt, including the external test set for infarct classification, was represented as vectors. Vectors represented the count for each N-gram in the training dictionary each testing excerpt contains. More explicitly, N-grams that appear in the test set but were not present in the train set were discarded.Separately, in preparation for the deep-learning model, each testing excerpt was tokenized and padded to the length of the longest training excerpt, followed by the same preprocessing scheme performed on the training data. The metrics used to assess model performance on each test set were accuracy, precision, recall ( i.e., sensitivity), specificity, and F1 score. Each of the test datasets, along with the external test dataset for acute or subacute ischemic infarct classification, were preprocessed as described above. Models were not retrained on any of the test datasets. A bootstrap approach was used to generate 95% confidence intervals, where for each iteration, the testing dataset of interest was resampled with replacement prior to metric evaluation. The process was iterated 1,000 times for the non-deep-learning models and 100 times for the deep-learning models due to computational cost. Figures were generated with the matplotlib (v3.0.2) package for Python. Subsampling experiments Studies to understand the effect of development dataset size on the resulting model performance were conducted. The training excerpts were sampled at rates of 2.5%, 10%, 25%, 50%, 75%, and 100%. A new model was trained as described above for each subsampled dataset. All models were evaluated on the same held-out test datasets. Statistics Statistical testing was performed using the scipy (v1.10.1) package in Python. The McNemar’s test was applied to compare the difference in proportion of error between traditional NLP models and BioBERT. Applying the Bonferroni correnction for multiple hypothesis testing, statisitical significance was determined a priori to be p < 0.002 (0.05/24), with 24 being the number of comparisons conducted. Results Dataset characteristics The distributions of cases for each pathology are summarized in Supplementary Table 1-3 . All radiology reports including impressions were composed of free text without a common standardized reporting structure. Supplementary Table 1. Click here for additional data file. (15KB, docx) Performance of NLP models on entire training data The test performances of the machine-learning models trained on entire training datasets for all abnormalities are summarized in Table 1 . For the abdominal CT dataset, all models achieved high performances with no statistically significant difference in error proportions. Mean accuracies ranged from 0.94 to 1.00 and mean F1 scores ranged from 0.85 to 0.97. Models with the highest F1 scores for acute appendicitis, acute diverticulitis, and bowel obstruction classification were non-deep-learning models with bag-of-words vectorization. BioBERT achieved a marginally higher mean F1 score compared to the non-deep-learning models for colitis or enteritis classification. For lateral and medial meniscus tear classification tasks, the mean accuracies ranged from 0.92 to 0.99 and the mean F1 scores ranged from 0.86 to 0.98, with BioBERT having significantly higher performance compared to the non-deep-learning models. For acute or subacute ischemic infarct classification on the internal test set, accuracies were all 0.97 and mean F1 scores ranged from 0.68 to 0.76, with no significant difference in performance of the models. The external testing performances of NLP models for acute or subacute ischemic infarct are summarized in Table 2 . BioBERT achieved the highest mean F1 score of 0.71, significantly lower than the internal test set mean F1 score of 0.76. Table 1. Performance of NLP models trained on a random 80% partition of the datasets and tested 