ed in Figure 1 . The corresponding results for the external acute or subacute ischemic infarct testing dataset are summarized in Figure 2 . In all cases, BioBERT performed poorly at smaller training sample sizes compared to non-deep-learning models. Specifically, BioBERT required training on approximately 1,000 reports to perform similarly or better than non-deep-learning models. At around 1,250 to 1,500 training samples, the testing performance for all models began to plateau, where additional training data yielded minimal performance gain. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Performance of NLP models on the held-out testing datasets as a function of the number of reports used in training. Training report subsets were chosen by randomly sampling from the entire training sets at rates of 2.5%, 10%, 25%, 50%, 75%, and 100%. 95% confidence intervals are shown as error bars. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Performance of NLP models on the held-out, external, brain MRI and head CT dataset from a separate institution as a function of the number of reports used in training. Training report subsets were chosen by randomly sampling from the entire training sets at rates of 2.5%, 10%, 25%, 50%, 75%, and 100%. 95% confidence intervals are shown as error bars. Discussion We investigated the impact of training dataset size on the performance of BioBERT, a transformer-based deep-learning NLP model, in comparison with more traditional NLP models for radiology text classification tasks. Several major trends emerged from our analysis. First, BioBERT demonstrated lower performance than traditional NLP models at low dataset sizes on the order of hundreds of training examples. Empirically, we found that BioBERT needed at least a thousand labeled training samples to achieve reasonable performance, even with pre-training on biomedical text as is the case with this model. It may be unrealistic or difficult to obtain large, labeled datasets for some clinical applications conducted at a single institution. Multiinstitutional federated and distributed learning methods are active areas of research to combat the data size limitation of deep-learning models. 18 The strength of BioBERT lies in capturing word positional and contextual complexities, which often require many examples to tune its large number of parameters. For classification tasks, it is more important to identify the relationship between vocabulary tokens and labels, which given few examples, may cater to the strength of N-grams and conventional ML models. Second, non-deep-learning models usually achieved higher F1 scores when there was a large class imbalance compared to BioBERT. Given too few positive training examples, BioBERT was not able to fine-tune its parameters optimally, suggesting training data distribution plays a role in addition to training data size. Class imbalance is a key consideration for dataset curation and/or model choice as the distributions of abnormalities are uneven for many diseases evaluated in radiology reports. Third, the performance of all models saturated beyond around 1,250 to 1,500 training samples. With a reasonably large training dataset available, it may be more worthwhile for clinical researchers to devote resources to model optimization as opposed to additional data collection and labeling, which can be time-consuming and labor-intensive. Fourth, NLP model performances can decrease when applied to external datasets, as different clinicians have unique reporting styles, even for the same findings. It is important to validate models and perform fine-tuning on representative datasets to improve generalizability. Fifth, for certain tasks, it can be difficult for models to achieve satisfactory performances even with increased training data. In the ischemic infarct classification task, clinicians reported the presence of infarct in a myriad of ways, making it challenging for models to identify patterns comprehensively and leading to higher false-negative rates. Larger language models may be better equipped to capture condition-specific reporting nuances more extensively. In general, practitioners should thoughtfully evaluate individual applications, identify consequences of errors, and necessity of human checkpoints prior to model deployment in clinical and research settings. Other research groups have compared the performance of NLP embedding and modeling methods for extracting information from radiology reports. Barash et al found that in classifying head CT reports for hemorrhage, deep-learning methods performed better than bag-of-words models for non-granular labels, and that there were no differences when the labels were more specific. 19 Senders et al demonstrated that a simple bag-of-words approach, coupled with logistic regression, outperformed complex deep-learning models in quantifying brain metastases in radiology reports. 20 In identifying lumbar spine imaging findings from X-ray and MRI reports, Tan et al showed that bag-of-words workflows outperformed rule-based algorithms across multiple metrics. 21 In another lumbar spine application, Jujjavarapu et al compared multiple text embedding frameworks. 22 They concluded trivial N-grams approaches to be favorable for applications in which the model development and deployment were conducted within the same healthcare system. In contrast to our work, all these studies were conducted with datasets for a single application or with reports originating from a single imaging modality. In addition, none of the studies compared the utility of newer transformer models against their selected approaches. Similar NLP methodological comparisons have been conducted in non-medical settings. In classifying larger text datasets, Chilakapati reached similar conclusions as our analysis. 10 When classification is the objective, they similarly recommended that researchers start with simple bag of words models coupled with traditional machine-learning models, as these approaches often yield high performance. Given the unique linguistic and reporting style of radiology texts, our benchmarking aids radiology NLP researchers in making modeling decisions with specific data sizes and distributions in mind. Our study tackles a need among the radiology NLP community where researchers have found it difficult to compare modeling approaches across different domains and datasets. 23,24 There are several limitations in our study. First, studies were only conducted for certain radiology tasks within two academic radiology practices in the same region. Additional studies need to be conducted to validate the generalizability of our findings, as radiologists at different institutions or with different sub-specializations may have different reporting styles. Second, the focus of this work was on classification. Other clinical NLP tasks, such as question answering, text generation, and translation, may yield different results when studying the effect of sample size on model performance. Third, our study explored four different text processing and models. Future NLP work may extend our analysis to include other text embedding, entity recognition, and machine-learning techniques. Conclusions Given a clinical NLP classification task, where the need to capture word position and contextual clues is minimal, simple text representation and traditional machine-learning methods should be the first approach to try. When the training dataset is large with non-extreme class imbalance, more complex transformers can be explored given adequate time and compute resources. While we recommend that clinical NLP researchers empirically experiment with approaches to maximize performance, our benchmarks can help serve as a starting reference for selecting machine-learning models according to their dataset characteristics. Footnotes Declaration of interests: MDS reports personal fees and non-financial support from 2 Minute Medicine, Inc., and patent royalties from Frequency Therapeutics for work