INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4937,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8641809','"It''s Really Like Any Other Study": Rural Radiology Facilities Performing Low-Dose Computed Tomography for Lung Cancer Screening.','Slatore CG, Golden SE, Thomas T, Bumatay S, Shannon J, Davis M.','2021','surmountable reimburses reimburse multihospital recode categorizations pronouns multihospital multihospital nonmissing moneymaker systematized ldcts weren nicely honestly buckets spearhead echos echos echos whoever smokes tiring bits annoying blanks bulletproof kick smoother mportance hampions motivator motivator doubted unenthusiastic acilitating mplementation skiing downtown attest attest criterium spearhead seeming attestations toolkits mabry p30ca069533 1k07ca211971 atsjournals atsjournals deffebach artis moyer mirkin azzoli brawley jaklitsch jett keshavjee samet crowell estepar ramerican acil123 ashby hermansen hutter 00439n fedewa dharmarajan henschke taioli triplette thayer pipavath crothers zahnd eberth spalluto labaze paulson malkoski 152rl cattaneo meisenberg geronimo mcm maxted copertino malo elston lafata hassinger kozower e60 e71 onf e60 e71 gaysynsky croyle ratcliffe burd eberth bozorgi lebron hazlett e119 pcd15 mazzone silvestri kanne kinsinger s186 s195 106st mazzone arenberg detterbeck damschroder aron kirsh lowery ohsu ziebland miech rattray damschroder damush goedken livorsi sauder weg chasco wandersman alia ramaswamy thakurta iaccarino melzer melzer crothers slatore aberle clapp fagerstrom nejmoa1102873 koning aalst nackaerts heuvelmans nejmoa1911793 kinsinger mazzone tenenbaum hunnibell slatore cjon howk spurlock mcginnis fagnan toolkits tabriz neslund dudas reuland lafata reston lungradsassessmentcategoriesv1','Abstract Rationale: The majority of eligible people have not been screened for lung cancer. There is emerging evidence that there are location-based disparities applicable to lung cancer screening (LCS). Objectives: To describe LCS radiologic services in rural Oregon and understand the barriers and facilitators to implementation of LCS using low-dose computed tomography (LDCT). Methods: This was a mixed-method descriptive study using surveys and semistructured interviews of key informants. We approached representatives from all 37 small and rural hospitals in Oregon. We purposively interviewed key informants from a subset based on LDCT implementation outcomes. Results: We surveyed representatives from 29 radiology facilities and qualitatively interviewed 18 key informants from 19 facilities (representing 12 healthcare systems). Among the surveyed radiology facilities, 59% were performing LDCT for LCS. Key informants reported that facilities that performed this service were often motivated by community needs and were less motivated by financial gain or evidence strength, and all described the importance of having a champion. Key informants described that LCS programmatic components that were within their normal scope of practice (e.g., specifying the LDCT parameters) were burdensome to establish but that barriers were surmountable. Most informants reported they did not perform other components of high-quality programs (e.g., ensuring adherence to recommended follow-up testing) and suggested that these steps were important but were the responsibility of primary care providers. Conclusions: Many rural hospital facilities in Oregon offer LDCT for LCS but do not perform all the recommended components of a screening program. Disparities in LCS use and adherence are unlikely to be solved by an exclusive focus at the radiology facility level and may require additional interventions at the primary care level. Keywords: cancer screening, radiology, implementation, rural hospitals Lung cancer screening (LCS) using annual low-dose computed tomography (LDCT) leads to a reduction in lung cancer mortality and is widely recommended, including by the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force, for adults with a history of smoking cigarettes ( 1 – 5 ). The Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) reimburses for the LDCT scan ( 6 ), and private insurers are required to reimburse for it. However, few people have received an LDCT scan for LCS ( 7 – 10 ), with a recent estimate suggesting that only 14% of potentially eligible patients received an LDCT examination in 2017 ( 11 ). Importantly, adherence to follow-up LDCT scans and shared decision-making interactions—mandatory elements for guideline-concordant LDCT screening—may be inadequate ( 12 – 15 ). Even though many radiology facilities now offer LDCT for screening, these facilities may serve a small number of eligible patients ( 16 ). There are likely multiple causes for this underuse that may be best addressed at the healthcare system level, including cost, lack of patient and provider knowledge, and inconvenience ( 17 ). In addition, rurality is an important factor that influences cancer care disparities ( 18 ), as an estimated 15–20% of people in the United States live in rural settings ( 19 ). There is emerging evidence that these disparities are applicable to LCS. As examples, people in rural census tracts had longer distances to travel to an American College of Radiology (ACR)-certified LCS center ( 20 ), and 78% of rural residents had to drive more than 30 minutes to access a designated LDCT screening center compared with only 17% of urban residents ( 21 ). We used mixed methods to describe LCS radiologic services in rural settings from one state (Oregon) and to catalog high-quality programmatic components as delineated by American College of Chest Physicians (CHEST) and American Thoracic Society (ATS) guidelines ( 22 – 24 ). We also wanted to understand the facilitators to implementation of LCS programs in general and to implementation of individual programmatic components for providing LDCT services for LCS among radiology facilities in rural settings. We used the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) to develop our study design by integrating CFIR domains into the aims, survey, and interview guide. Using the CFIR constructs also allowed us to improve the generalizability of our findings ( 25 ). Methods We conducted a qualitative study and used a survey methodology to characterize the facilities. Data were collected from December 2018 to May 2019. Our study was approved by the Veterans Affairs Portland Health Care System/Oregon Health and Science University Institutional Review Board (number 18865). We obtained a waiver of documentation for informed consent to complete the survey and obtained consent by phone from interview participants. Study Setting and Participants Oregon is largely a rural state, with approximately 33% of residents living in areas designated as rural (geographic areas >10 miles from a population center of more than 40,000). We attempted to survey representatives from all 37 small and rural hospitals as identified by the Oregon Office of Rural Health ( 26 ). Although our focus was hospital-based radiology facilities located in rural communities, some of these facilities are part of multihospital healthcare systems that include an urban “hub” and individual hospital-based radiology facilities in rural areas. When applicable, we asked system-level participants to report differences in procedures performed in the urban hub versus in their rural radiology facilities. Facility Survey We made up to 10 phone calls to each rural hospital–based radiology facility to identify a person knowledgeable about the LCS program or a person who was knowledgeable regarding why the facility did not have a LCS program. We asked the person identified as most knowledgeable about the screening program (or lack thereof) to complete a short survey about program characteristics and then inquired about participation in a qualitative interview. If we could not identify a participant who was willing or able to complete the survey, we asked one question about the presence or absence of performing LDCT for LCS. The survey included questions about the facility’s stage of LDCT implementation and program characteristics (Appendix E1 in the online supplement). We used a standardized case-report form to collect data that included respondent answers and information taken from publicly available resources. Key Informant Interviews We purposively sampled key informants from facilities that completed the survey. We conducted one interview per system; however, four system interviews had multiple informants. We used a semistructured interview guide (Appendix E2) to assess decision-making about adopting a screening program, programmatic components, if/how care is coordinated, and barriers and facilitators to program implementation. Questions were informed by the CFIR (Appendix E2), which identifies modifiable factors associated with implementation at the level of individuals. All interviews were conducted by experienced qualitative analysts (C.G.S. and/or S.E.G.) by phone, lasting on average 34 minutes (standard deviation, 10 min). Each interview was transcribed and de-identified before analysis. Qualitative and Mixed-Method Analysis We uploaded transcripts in ATLAS.ti version 8 (Scientific Software Development GmbH) and analyzed qualitative data concurrently with data collection to monitor for saturation ( 27 , 28 , 29 ). We used conventional content analysis. The codebook was created by a multidisciplinary team (M.D., C.G.S., and S.E.G.) and drew from inductive and deductive approaches, including clusters of CFIR constructs that could contribute to implementation success. First, one team member (C.G.S.) coded three transcripts. The same three transcripts were then reviewed by M.D. and S.E.G., at which point agreement on coding was substantial. We then met as a group to discuss and refine the codebook. Next, the remaining transcripts were independently coded by C.G.S. These transcripts were then reviewed by M.D. and S.E.G. for accuracy. We met frequently to discuss findings, refine codes, and recode as needed. We resolved differences through consensus. We also used an audit trail and memos to ensure consistency and rigor ( 30 , 31 ). Ultimately, we performed a member check to ensure the validity of the results ( 31 ). We used survey and interview responses to categorize facilities according to the nine components identified in the 2015 CHEST/ATS guidelines with the 2018 explanations and rationales to define a facility as having a centralized versus decentralized referral process and additional considerations for several components ( 22 – 24 ). Of note, when we conducted the key informant interviews, we frequently learned that the survey responses were incorrect, that they missed important details, or that the programmatic component did not meet CHEST/ATS criteria. Thus, we based final categorizations only on key informant interviews. Participants from each healthcare system (regardless of the number of informants who participated in the interview or how many facilities were included in their healthcare system) will be identified by an anonymously determined number. We use “she” for all gender pronouns. Results Facility Surveys Twenty-two representatives completed the survey, covering 29 of the 37 small and rural hospital–based radiology facilities across Oregon. Among the surveyed facilities, 17 (59%) were performing LDCT for LCS. One facility’s representative reported doing LCS but did not use LDCT, so the facility was categorized as not providing LDCT for LCS. Two facilities’ representatives reported using a centralized referral process. Many indicated that their LDCT request forms included statements or “check boxes” that the ordering provider had verified the patient’s eligibility and had performed shared decision-making according to CMS requirements. Most respondents reported that follow-up for positive findings was considered the responsibility of the ordering provider. Facilities that were part of multihospital healthcare systems often had centralized components (e.g., radiologist reading, ACR registry data entry) that were performed in the largest (often urban) hospital while allowing patients to obtain the LDCT examination in their affiliated rural hospitals. Larger systems did not necessarily have a centralized referral process for LDCT. Key Informant Interviews We interviewed 18 participants who represented 19 facilities ( Table 1 ); these informants were associated with 12 distinct healthcare systems. Fourteen facilities offered LDCT for LCS, whereas five did not. Informants reported they were not aware of stand-alone radiology facilities in their area that offered LDCT for LCS. Only one system described a process in which they had seriously considered or worked toward implementation of LDCT services but then had failed to do so or had stopped offering the service. Some facilities that did not offer LDCT scans were considering doing so in the future. Only one facility currently used a centralized referral process. Except where indicated, we did not find substantial differences among facilities that were or were not part of a multihospital system. Table 1. Self-reported subject characteristics (interviews), N = 18 Characteristic N (%) * Gender Female 10 (56) Male 8 (44) Racial/ethnic background White 16 (89) More than one race 1 (5) Declined to answer 1 (5) Spanish, Hispanic, or Latino? Yes 1 (8) No 17 (92) Role of Informant Radiology Staff Nonphysician 9 (50) Physician 2 (11) Healthcare system administration Nonphysician 3 (17) Physician 1 (5) LCS coordinator 3 (17) Open in a new tab Definition of abbreviation : LCS = lung cancer screening. * N (%) of nonmissing data. We identified themes from several key areas, including 1 ) factors facilitating LDCT implementation, 2 ) factors not strongly associated with implementation, 3 ) the importance of champions, 4 ) using an LCS program versus offering LDCT for LCS, and 5 ) LCS programmatic components. These themes are described in detail below, and illustrative quotes are included in Table 2 . Table 2. Representative quotes Quote Number Participant ID Quote Factors influencing LDCT implementation Motivating factors 1 1 About a year and a half ago, maybe a little bit longer, we were approached (by primary care providers) as a team to ask if we could put together some protocols [so] that the doctors knew exactly what was being done. 2 8 The patient who entered the program and then they found their cancer and we’re able to intervene early and give them a great outcome because they’ve entered this program. 3 6 It’s not a moneymaker by any means. I’m telling you that right now. This is a benefit to the community, so it’s important for us to do that for that reason. 4 12 [Starting our LDCT program] was a collaborative decision that any USPSTF A or B recommendation would be systematized. . . . It was like yes, . . . of course we’re all going to do this. 5 8 I don''t have some of the beginning as far as the laid out . . . business plan as to why to do it. Certainly, [this state] has high rates in terms of tobacco usage . . . and that [screening] saves lives definitely by being diagnosed earlier. 6 3 I just know where my radiologist would be on doing these (LDCTs for LCS). If our doctors (PCPs) said, “Can you do them?” She would. We’ve never had to worry about it. 7 6 We had a lot of, I guess, community interaction. Doctors wanted us to do this program, and so we just looked to do it. We weren’t going to do it at first because it’s a lot of work, but then we got pushed into it. Here we are. We’re doing it. One of those things. 8 9 Not only are we all local residents, treating local residents, sometimes their friends and family members, we take it very personally. And I’ll tell you in my department, we take it very personally, that again, when you come to work you have made, not a commitment to this facility, you''ve made a commitment to this community. Community factors 9 1 There really isn’t a history there ([of] LDCT for LCS) that’s any different than anything else we do. Our goal is to meet the community’s needs for imaging and that’s just one piece of the puzzle. 10 3 I know we are rural farmer-based farming community and 20 years ago smoking was really, really prevalent. 11 8 We do a community needs assessment every couple years. Then we try to build initiatives around supporting the community needs assessment, tobacco-related deaths, and our goal is to reduce that. This fits in really nicely with our mission. We do have programs [but] honestly we lose buckets of money on them, but because they meet that mission, we do them. 12 4 We chose to propose [LCS] in [this rural county] because [this county] has a very high incidence of smoking and a very high incidence of lung cancer diagnosed at advance stages. Financial factors 13 2 Part of it but, when you look into it, it looks like there’s not a real great reimbursement for it. When we did it in [previous employer] also we had the cancer center right there. That made a lot of sense because then they would follow up and they go through the cancer center, but here, we don’t have that. It’s like, well, maybe we can still do some follow-up CTs or something like that. Evidence 14 12 This is part of that evidence-based medicine and data gathering, so it’s just making sure that as we roll this out as a USPSTF recommendation that we actually are contributing to ongoing academic learning and that we make sure that this really is doing right for the patient. . . . I’m pretty sure that [the radiologist] went to a conference where they presented USPSTF recommendations, so she was doing sort of a group update and she was like this is USPSTF step. I think it’s a B recommendation. Do you happen to know actually? 15 8 . . . it was before CMS had started paying for the test, and so we felt like it was a great opportunity to engage our pulmonologists together with the imaging partners and that it saves lives definitely by being diagnosed earlier. Importance of a champion 16 7 We might [have an LDCT program for LCS] if the radiologist pushed us hard enough for it, but we’re more likely to continue the way we’ve been going which is discouraging providers from ordering those at our facility. . . . We’d need someone to spearhead it. 17 4 When the [champion] left the area, no other champions came forward. . . . That’s why it [offering LDCT for LCS] sort of unfortunately just ended. 18 4 It’s really working with finding those right champions and really working with the multidisciplinary team to make a successful program. And having someone to coordinate that all takes resources, so that’s part of what we’re looking at. 19 3 We’re setting up an echo [echocardiogram] program. . . . I found out [the PCPs] had sent out a lot of echos and I said, “well I used to do echos,” and it just went from there. When I asked the physicians if they were interested in echos, they were, and they had enough out of our area to get them that it paid for us to get that test. 20 7 Regarding setting up a different radiologic procedure (echocardiogram). She [a PCP] worked something out with whoever management we had at the time to make it so that we brought in a program that’s just for echocardiograms, and she reads them and she gets paid for them. . . . It was actually interesting to watch. Program vs. LDCT for LCS 21 9 We really are in our infancy at getting an entire program together. As of right now, all we are doing is the low-dose CT chest and reporting it, not under a Lung-RADS regimen, but just providing a dictated report from our local radiologists regarding this scan and the findings. 22 1 Some places have like a high-risk breast clinic where people are tracked all the way through. We don’t have anything like that in place at this point in time. . . . If a patient meets the criteria for the test, which is smokes a certain amount of years, pack a day for X number of years, and worked around asbestos, I forget what they all are. Basically, they come in and have the test. The report goes to their physician and the physician, based on their training, decides if the patient needs follow-up with a specialist or not. Or if it was a specialist that ordered it, they get the results and they come up with a treatment plan. 23 7 [Radiologist] says since we don’t have an official protocol with that super low dose and we don’t enter our CTs into the national registry that our program is not set up correctly, and that’s not the way it’s supposed to be, and so [radiologist] doesn’t want us doing them unless we set up a proper program. LCS programmatic components Required steps 24 10 The low dose, we basically just use the protocol that was set forth in the original papers. That was pretty easy. We had to submit, to get the ACR accreditation, we just had to submit basically some sample studies. That was pretty easy. 25 11 It takes a lot more time than you think it does [to track patients for surveillance]. To make sure you follow up on all these patients year after year or several months later type thing and just keep track of all of them is a lot of time. 26 10 The tracking system was a little bit more tiring [to set up] because we have to make sure that it was working properly. We use XX. I’m not sure XX is the best but it''s what we had access to and were already using. I think there’s some bits of data which I wish we could manipulate a little bit better on XX, but it is what it is. It does the job for the basics. 27 8 It will work in our [urban facility] potentially, but our other areas that we serve, a patient would not drive to [urban facility] for the shared decision-making visit. Primary care wouldn’t send them. It’s too far. They want care in the community. 28 1 It’s just some data entry [to submit to the ACR]. It’s not particularly difficult. It probably takes a couple of minutes per study and we are doing 10 or 12 a week from the sounds of it. So maybe 30 minutes of work a week. 29 5 I would say [data entry] is annoying. It’s not hard, but it’s definitely another step that creates some sort of barrier. 30 6 With all that help, knowing what we needed to have to do the test and then the information that we needed from the doctors on the patient, we put together this book that just basically said, “We’ve designed an order. You have to have all these blanks filled.” It''s basically, we laid it all out, pack years, just how you figure out pack years. I need their name. I need the doctor’s NPI number. It needs to be signed. This is the CPT code and this the ICD-9 or -10, now whatever it is code that you’d use. It made it pretty much bulletproof if they follow the instructions that we gave them. 31 9 . . . our challenge is going to be how many FTEs do we need to run this [program] appropriately? What can we do to automate where it would be financially hard for us to get that many employees and how do we constantly remind people about this program? Not just roll it out kick it off and then leave it alone and move on to the next program. Decision-making 32 8 Again, it’s . . . somebody has it in the order for a mammogram, we don’t send the clinic note that shows the history of this patient, all this in order to get a mammogram. We don’t do that for colonoscopy. You don’t send a decision-making visit that’s happening in the primary care clinic to go over the colonoscopy, and that doesn’t have to be sent with the order before the order can be processed. I think having the shared decision-making visit is very important, but the fact that it has to attach to the order is where it gets really cumbersome 33 5 At the beginning we did [the shared decision-making] but this was several years ago. We had a mix. We had some providers that didn’t want to do it. We had other providers that really wanted to do it. Because of the program volume it was, that I’m not a qualified medical person to do shared decision-making, we had to push it back on them. Coordinators 34 8 Unfortunately [our coordinator] is spending her time being a check in the order, making sure that it matches the shared decision-making visit, making sure we’ve got everything before we can even order that CT, send it to the scheduling department. We’ve looked at ways to lean up our process and are trying to make it smoother. Open in a new tab Definition of abbreviations : ACR = American College of Radiology; CMS = Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services; CPT = current procedural terminology; CT = computed tomography; FTE = full-time employee; ICD = International Classification of Diseases ; ID = identifier; LCS = lung cancer screening; LDCT = low-dose CT; Lung-RADS = Lung CT Screening Reporting and Data System; NPI = National Provider Identifier; PCP = primary care provider; USPSTF = U.S. Preventive Services Task Force. Factors Facilitating LDCT Implementation Informants reported several, often overlapping, motivating factors to begin offering LDCT for LCS. Reasons for implementing LDCT services included providing a community service, addressing high rates of smoking in their area, requests from primary care providers (PCPs), and that adding new services was considered usual practice. Facilities that offered LDCT for LCS often described an implementation story that started with why and how a champion or a small group of champions decided to begin offering LDCT for LCS as a service. All facilities that offered LDCT for LCS had a champion or had multiple champions, and all facilities that did not offer this service seemed to lack a champion ( see I mportance of C hampions section). Participants reported essentially two paths to begin offering LDCT for LCS. In one scenario, members of the local community, usually PCPs (never patients directly), began explicitly requesting LDCT scans and a champion or multiple champions took on the role of initiating the process to address these requests. Implementation often started with a “bottom-up” perspective rather than with external mandates from facility leadership or from outside pressures (e.g., quote 1 in Table 2 ). Alternatively, champions coordinated the process to offer LCS services on their own, without prior explicit requests from their community. In general, these pathways seemed similar to those of previous episodes when a new radiology service had been started. Factors Not Strongly Associated with Implementation Notably, participants reported that direct financial gain was not a strong motivating factor to begin offering LDCT for LCS (e.g., quote 3 in Table 2 ). Every site that offered LDCT for LCS reported that it was important to get the billing processes correct, but any financial gain from providing LDCT was not a large motivator (or was not at all a motivator) for starting the service. The CFIR suggests that the strength of the supporting evidence can be a strong influence on implementation. However, facilities that offered the service did not mention evidence strength as a major facilitator. Strength of evidence was discussed by only one informant (Informant 12) ( see quote 4 in Table 2 ). Six facilities from two separate systems offered LDCT for LCS before the CMS reimbursement decision in 2015 (one of which had subsequently stopped offering the service). Facilities that did not perform LDCT for LCS did not report that they doubted the evidence or were explicitly waiting for more evidence to start a program. Overall, it seemed that evidence strength was taken as a given or necessary requirement but did not otherwise play a large role in decisions to start a program. Similarly, among facilities that did not have a radiologist as an initial champion, buy-in from radiologists seemed like a necessary step but overall did not seem to be a large barrier. Radiologists were sometimes described as unenthusiastic about reading the LDCT scans but were seldom strongly opposed to offering the service. A participant (Informant 3) from an institution that did not offer LDCT for LCS reported: [LDCT for LCS] came up about a year ago [in our programmatic discussion]. I found a note in a desk from [previous staff] that she would look into it. I spoke to our radiologist about what was going on, were we going to do these or not. She seemed skeptical about doing them. She would prefer not to get involved in them. She’s not saying it’s a bad thing or nothing, but she didn’t show a lot of interest in doing them. Importance of Champions All informants mentioned the importance of having champions to lead and coordinate the implementation process. The champions were often motivated by recognized needs in their community ( see F actors F acilitating LDCT I mplementation section), noting the high mortality burden from lung cancer and the disproportionate burden among rural residents. The champion was often, but not always, a physician; pulmonologists were champions at six facilities (three healthcare systems). Participants from facilities that did not offer LDCT for LCS also mentioned the importance of having a champion (e.g., quotes 16 and 17 in Table 2 ). Several informants described how a champion or multiple champions had been able to start other multidisciplinary programs in their rural communities (e.g., quotes 19 and 20 in Table 2 ). LCS Program versus Offering LDCT for LCS Among the facilities that offered LDCT for LCS, only some informants described a “program.” Even an institution that offered most CHEST/ATS components of a decentralized LCS program did not view themselves as such but rather as just providing a routine clinical service. A participant (Informant 1) said, There’s no formal low-dose lung screening committee or clinic. . . . It’s really like any other study. . . . Really no different than a knee X-ray. If I twist my knee skiing, I’m going to go see my doctor and they’re going to order an MRI [magnetic resonance imaging examination]. Irrespective of the number of LDCT program components in place ( Table 3 ), most informants acknowledged the importance of systematic approaches to identify and track patients: Table 3. Programmatic components of the 14 facilities that offered LDCT for lung cancer screening based on key informant interviews CHEST and ATS Programmatic Component N (%) 1. Eligibility consistent with guidelines 14 (100) 2. Structured frequency and duration of screening 14 (100) 3. Use of LDCT 14 (100) 4. Nodule size threshold and data collection 14 (100) 5. Structured reporting system (e.g., Lung-RADS [ 44 ]) 13 (92) 6. Lung nodule management algorithm * 3 (21) 7. Integrated smoking cessation 4 (29) 8. Patient and provider education † 1 (7) 9. Program data collection ‡ 10 (71) Open in a new tab Definition of abbreviations : ATS = American Thoracic Society; CHEST = American College of Chest Physicians; LDCT = low-dose computed tomography; Lung-RADS = Lung Computed Tomography Screening Reporting and Data System. Data are based on key informant interviews representing 19 facilities. * Presence/absence of a multidisciplinary team of clinicians and presence/absence of a tracking system to increase adherence to follow-up imaging. † Conducting the shared decision-making visit and providing ongoing clinician and patient education and outreach. ‡ Quality assurance practices for the LDCT for lung cancer screening itself. I think the biggest thing is coming back to the foundation of the process. If this is in your template to ask every single patient these basic questions, it shouldn’t matter if they’re your neighbor in a small community or in New York City or downtown Portland because it is built into your record system to ask these questions for their health and to care for the patient in the best way (Informant 12 ). LCS Programmatic Components Most facilities that performed LDCT for LCS used a decentralized referral process and the majority of the CHEST/ATS programmatic components ( Table 3 ). The main exceptions were that 1 ) many facilities provided educational materials to providers but seldom directly to patients, and only one facility performed the decision-making interaction themselves to ensure that it had occurred (patient and provider education); 2 ) only facilities within multisystem healthcare systems with large urban hubs included multidisciplinary physicians on their lung nodule management teams (lung nodule management algorithms); 3 ) three facilities used structured reporting systems to ensure adherence to surveillance and follow-up recommendations (lung nodule management algorithms); and 4 ) few facilities had integrated smoking cessation interventions. Most participants from facilities that performed LDCT for LCS described that many of the steps required to start offering the service were mildly burdensome but generally within their normal experience of starting other new services. Only one facility did the decision-making interaction themselves. One system with several facilities had initially performed the decision-making interaction but found they could not sustain this process once their program grew too large for one person to handle this responsibility. Several facilities had developed check-box order sets that required the ordering provider to attest that the CMS requirements for assessing smoking status and history had been met, that a decision-making interaction had occurred, etc. As one informant noted, When we were . . . trying to put everything together, we were going to do the documentation of it and make sure it was done and all that . . . we did go to [another LCS program] and looked at their system. . . . [T]hey basically said you put it in your order form that they attest to doing it. You don’t make sure they do it (Informant 11). Although not a designated CHEST/ATS criterium, the facilities with a designated LCS coordinator or navigators reported their utility. A participant (Informant 11) reported that an LCS coordinator is “Very important because somebody needs to know what’s going on and put all the pieces together!” Of the few facilities that reported having navigators dedicated to LCS, most affirmed eligibility for LDCT for LCS and managed ACR data requirements but rarely had direct patient contact. Discussion Among hospital-based radiology facilities in rural Oregon in 2019, more than half are performing LDCT for LCS. Facilities that performed this service were often motivated by community needs and requests from PCPs and were less motivated by financial gain or evidence strength. We found two pathways to begin offering LDCT for LCS: either clinicians in the community started asking for the service, and a champion was designated to spearhead and consolidate the process, or a champion started the process without prior community requests. All facilities reported that it was critical to have an engaged champion or to have multiple engaged champions to help implement the service. Indeed, it was clear that without a champion, no system could assemble and coordinate the necessary resources and stakeholder input to begin to offer the service. Finally, most systems that performed LDCT for LCS, regardless of how many programmatic components they included, characterized it as a service rather than as a program. We found that the most relevant CFIR construct for offering LDCT for LCS was that of champions, which was part of the Process domain within the inner setting ( 32 , 33 ). We were struck by champions’ responsiveness to both explicit and implicit requests from clinicians in their community and, for several facilities not offering LDCT for LCS, a seeming openness to starting the service if clinicians began requesting it. Most of our other findings related to the CFIR domain of Intervention Characteristics. We found that many facilities began offering LDCT for LCS after consideration of the interventions’ relative advantage compared with not offering the service. Notably, we did not find that the strength of the evidence or financial considerations were strongly involved with decisions for systems to begin offering LDCT for LCS ( 34 ). Complexity of the Intervention is another CFIR construct, and several organizations, including the CMS and CHEST/ATS, stress that high-quality screening is a complex process that includes multiple programmatic components ( 6 , 23 , 24 ). Rural Oregon facilities that performed LDCT for LCS had successfully included many of these components. However, perhaps because most used a decentralized referral process, they seldom referred to themselves as having a “program.” Given the documented deficiencies in LCS decision-making interactions ( 15 , 35 , 36 ), it is unlikely that order forms with attestations meet the intent of the CMS criterion. Theoretically, radiology facilities could address multiple gaps in LCS processes in rural settings. For instance, LCS has had limited uptake, despite many centers offering LDCT scans ( 7 – 10 ). Similarly, decision-making interactions may be suboptimal and may therefore limit patients’ informed participation in screening ( 15 ). Finally, patients in routine clinical practice have low adherence rates to follow-up LDCT scans, which are dramatically lower than the rates in randomized trials and modeled efficacy studies ( 37 , 38 ). To close gaps in LCS processes, it may be necessary to change the incentives for radiology facilities to include these additional components. For instance, the Veterans Health Administration LCS Demonstration Project systemically identified potentially eligible patients by adding electronic health record alerts ( 39 ). In addition, systems that use a centralized shared decision-making process ( 36 , 40 ) and population management tools may have improved adherence to follow-up procedures ( 12 , 41 ). Our findings suggest that providing toolkits without identifying champions is unlikely to lead to successful implementation. Rather, systems may need infrastructure support to help make organizational changes—such as through the use of practice facilitators ( 42 , 43 ). In addition, given the reported role PCPs played in advocating for LDCT implementation, it may be necessary to focus efforts on interactions between patients and PCPs rather than on radiology facilities. Limitations Our study has a few notable limitations. First, we have less information about facilities that do not offer LDCT for LCS. Although many of our findings from this group mirrored those from systems that offered the service, we may have lacked thematic saturation. Second, our findings are focused on LDCT implementation in rural settings and may not be generalizable to urban areas. Third, because we did not interview patients or PCPs, we cannot comment on how these groups feel about the LDCT services in their area. Importantly, we found that radiology facilities seldom performed all the recommended programmatic components, but it is likely that PCPs perform these tasks to some degree. Finally, we relied on self-reporting for determining which programmatic components each facility included, and we discovered discrepancies between surveys and interviews. Conclusions More than half of the rural hospitals in Oregon offer LDCT for LCS, enabling many patients to access this service in their community. Overall, most facilities that offered LDCT for LCS indicated that the process of starting the service was nothing unique—just another new service that could be offered to their community, albeit one with additional logistical hurdles. Champions were critical for starting the LDCT service, and most radiology facilities offered some but not all the components from CHEST and ATS guidelines ( 22 – 24 ). In general, these facilities were aware of these potential gaps but were unlikely to include these components in the future, as they were already offering as many components as they could reasonably provide. It seems unlikely that rural disparities in LCS use and adherence will be solved by an exclusive focus at the radiology facility level unless more resources can be directed to providing services to increase shared decision-making and ensuring appropriate follow-up. Acknowledgments Acknowledgment The authors thank Julia Mabry, M.S., M.P.H. for her help with this study. Footnotes Supported by the Oregon Health and Science University Knight Cancer Institute National Cancer Institute Cancer Center Support grant P30CA069533. It was also supported by resources from the Veterans Affairs Portland Health Care System, Portland, Oregon. M.D.’s time is supported in part by a career development award from the National Cancer Institute (1K07CA211971-01A1). The Department of Veterans Affairs did not have a role in the conduct of the study; in the collection, management, analysis, or interpretation of data; or in the preparation of the manuscript. The views expressed in this article are those of the authors and do not necessarily represent the views of the Department of Veterans Affairs or the U.S. Government. Author Contributions : C.G.S., S.E.G., T.T., S.B., J.S., and M.D. contributed to the study conception and design. Material preparation and data collection and analysis were performed by C.G.S., S.E.G., T.T., S.B., and M.D. The first draft of the manuscript was written by C.G.S., and all authors commented on previous versions of the manuscript. All authors read and approved the final version to be published. Availability of Data: All data can be requested from C.G.S.’s Health Services Research Repository (Institutional Review Board number 3535). This article has an online supplement, which is accessible from this issue’s table of contents at www.atsjournals.org . Author disclosures are available with the text of this article at www.atsjournals.org . References 1. Humphrey LL, Deffebach M, Pappas M, Baumann C, Artis K, Mitchell JP, et al. Screening for lung cancer with low-dose computed tomography: a systematic review to update the US Preventive Services Task Force recommendation. Ann Intern Med. 2013;159:411–420.