PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Mazzone PJ, Tenenbaum A, Seeley M, Petersen H, Lyon C, Han X, et al. Impact of a lung cancer screening counseling and shared decision-making visit. Chest. 2017;151:572–578. doi: 10.1016/j.chest.2016.10.027. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Hunnibell LS, Slatore CG, Ballard EA. Foundations for lung nodule management for nurse navigators. Clin J Oncol Nurs. 2013;17:525–531. doi: 10.1188/13.CJON.525-531. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Davis MM, Howk S, Spurlock M, McGinnis PB, Cohen DJ, Fagnan LJ. A qualitative study of clinic and community member perspectives on intervention toolkits: “unless the toolkit is used it won’t help solve the problem”. BMC Health Serv Res. 2017;17:497. doi: 10.1186/s12913-017-2413-y. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 43. Tabriz AA, Neslund-Dudas C, Turner K, Rivera PM, Reuland DS, Lafata JE. How health care organizations implement shared decision making when it is required for reimbursement: the case of lung cancer screening. Chest. 2021;159:413–425. doi: 10.1016/j.chest.2020.07.078. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Lung-RADS version 1.1. Reston, VA: American College of Radiology; 2020. [accessed 2020 Apr 9]. Available from https://www.acr.org/-/media/ACR/Files/RADS/Lung RADS/LungRADSAssessmentCategoriesv1-1.pdf?la=en [ Google Scholar ]',232,'2025-10-15 10:21:41','2025-10-15 10:21:41');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4938,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9492826','Scoping Reviews and Their Utility in Medical Toxicology.','Ross JA, Shah SN, Burns MM.','2022','jmt jmt jmt vea vea kaczor proconvulsant kaczor conglomeration uncommonly djulbegovic bestpractice cumpston mdj alexnder mcinerney mdj tufanaru aromataris lillie kastner levac stelfox mdj mcinerney xeb mdj marnie mcinerney synth jbies suelzer evali inhaling kaczor zacharia tormoehlen neavyn carreiro proconvulsant','Over a decade ago, the Journal of Medical Toxicology (JMT) published “How to Write an Effective Review Article” providing readers with clear guidelines for narrative and systematic reviews [ 1 ]. Narrative and systematic reviews are not the only methods of evidence synthesis; scoping reviews are increasingly being used in many medical fields. Although the first framework for scoping reviews was published in 2005 [ 2 ], they remain uncommon in medical toxicology literature. Scoping reviews have the potential to make significant contributions to scientific literature by compiling available evidence for a toxicology question and identifying gaps in toxicology knowledge across a breadth of literature. It is important for readers, reviewers, authors, and clinicians to have a thorough understanding of scoping reviews. As augmentation to JMT’s previously published guidelines on review articles, we present a discussion on the utility of scoping reviews in medical toxicology. Evidence-based medicine is at the core of medical decision-making, and treatment plans based on the literature often have more evidence for successful outcomes than decisions made by individual or small-group consensus [ 3 ]. However, not all medical evidence in the literature is weighted equally, which is why levels of evidence exist [ 4 ]. Methodologic quality assessment of studies is often viewed through the lens of a hierarchical rating system such as the pyramid of evidence. Systematic reviews are considered by many to be the gold standard of reviews based on their rigorous, reproducible, and well-established methods that focus on minimizing bias [ 1 , 5 ]. Although they are often not considered within the context of the hierarchies of evidence, scoping reviews offer a newer approach to evidence synthesis. Scoping reviews are similar to systematic reviews in that they follow a rigorous protocol and aim to be of high quality [ 6 ]. Like systematic reviews, they are comprised of an extensive search for information, aim to be reproducible, and include steps to increase reliability [ 7 ]. The purpose of a scoping review is to give a clear landscape of the literature while providing an overview of its focus. A scoping review is useful in areas of study with emerging evidence as it provides a broad overview of all available data relating to a topic of study [ 8 ]. The product of a scoping review can summarize and disseminate existing research findings, identify research gaps, and recommend areas for future research [ 8 , 9 ]. When compared to systematic reviews (which address specific questions based on particular criteria of interest), scoping reviews address broader research questions [ 8 , 9 ]. Scoping reviews report on all of the types of evidence that address and inform practice in the field; thus, a heterogeneous combination of study designs with variable quality of evidence is often included in the review [ 9 ]. Unlike systematic reviews, risk of bias assessments of studies are not an integral component for inclusion in scoping reviews [ 10 ], and scoping reviews are limited in their ability to impact practice guidelines and influence policy development. Instead, as explained by Tricco et al., “Scoping reviews can be seen as a hypothesis-generating exercise, while systematic reviews can be hypothesis-testing” [ 8 ]. In a field where it can be difficult to perform randomized controlled trials but new evidence is constantly emerging, toxicology reviews often need to utilize a heterogenous compilation of literature, including case reports, observations and expert recommendations, and retrospective data reviews. Scoping reviews are inclusive of all types of evidence and can be the ideal format for the dissemination of information from toxicological research. While not commonplace, there are examples of utilizing the scoping review process in medical toxicology literature. Recently, two scoping reviews have been published in JMT. In 2021, Feldman et al. presented a review of studies showing the pulmonary effects of inhaled vitamin E acetate (VEA) [ 11 ]. This scoping review allowed for analysis of 16 articles from 11 different journals that provided evidence for the potential toxicities of tocopherol and VEA. Compiling these studies via a scoping review allowed the authors to identify the gaps in current knowledge and propose future studies related to the potential pulmonary toxicity. More recently, Kaczor et al. used a scoping review to describe the potential proconvulsant effects of cannabis [ 12 ]. This study included 13 articles from 12 different journals, many of which are not directed to an audience of medical toxicologists. This article noted that cannabis research is difficult to perform due to its classification as a schedule 1 drug; utilization of a scoping review allowed the authors to compile the existing research from multiple sources. Kaczor et al. provided a summary of the evidence surrounding seizures and cannabis exposures and identified areas needing further research [ 12 ]. These two articles are examples of how scoping reviews can be utilized to examine emerging evidence as a step towards identifying a specific question. Medical toxicology lends itself well to scoping reviews. This discipline is often considered a conglomeration of numerous medical fields, including pediatrics, emergency medicine, preventive medicine, occupational medicine, and addiction medicine. Because of this wide range of clinical experiences, data and knowledge that are pertinent to medical toxicologists come from all areas of science and medicine, with articles scattered throughout the thousands of medical journals. Scoping reviews can be valuable to the medical toxicology literature by identifying gaps in the existing research, reporting a summation of results from heterogenous sources, and examining emerging evidence as a step towards generating a hypothesis for a future systematic review. However, scoping reviews are newer than other approaches to evidence synthesis in medicine and are used uncommonly by medical toxicologists despite their potential for significant contributions. We encourage more medical toxicologists to consider using scoping reviews in their future academic endeavors. Guidelines for high-quality scoping reviews: Follow a rigorous protocol equivalent to that of systematic and narrative reviews Allow for the compilation of available evidence for a toxicology question Be inclusive of all types of evidence Identify gaps in toxicology knowledge across a breadth of literature Examine emerging evidence as a step towards identifying a specific toxicology question to be addressed by a systematic review Propose potential areas of future medical toxicology research Declarations Conflict of Interest The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Murphy CM. Writing an effective review article. J Med Toxicol. 2012;8(2):89–90. doi: 10.1007/s13181-012-0234-2. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Arksey H, O’Malley L. Scoping studies: towards a methodological framework. Int J Soc Res Methodol. 2005;8(1):19–32. doi: 10.1080/1364557032000119616. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Djulbegovic B, Guyatt GH. Progress in evidence-based medicine: a quarter century on. The Lancet. 2017;390(10092):415–423. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(16)31592-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. BMJ Best Practice. Why do we need evidence-based medicine? Published 2022. Accessed March 23, 2022. https://bestpractice.bmj.com/info/toolkit/practise-ebm/why-do-we-need-ebm/ 5. Higgins JPT, Thomas J, Chandler J, Cumpston M, Li T, Page MJ, Welch VA, eds. Cochrane handbook for systematic reviews of interventions. 2 nd ed. Chichester, UK: John Wiley & Sons; 2019. 10.1002/9781119536604 6. Khalil H, Peters MDJ, Tricco AC, Pollock D, Alexnder L, McInerney P, Godfrey CM, Munn Z. Conducting high quality scoping reviews-challenges and solutions. J Clin Epidemiol. 2021;130:156–160. doi: 10.1016/j.jclinepi.2020.10.009. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Munn Z, Peters MDJ, Stern C, Tufanaru C, McArthur A, Aromataris E. Systematic review or scoping review? Guidance for authors when choosing between a systematic or scoping review approach. BMC Med Res Methodol. 2018;18(1):143. doi: 10.1186/s12874-018-0611-x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Tricco AC, Lillie E, Zarin W, O''Brien K, Colquhoun H, Kastner M, Levac D, Ng C, Sharpe JP, Wilson K, Kenny M, Warren R, Wilson C, Stelfox HT, Straus SE. A scoping review on the conduct and reporting of scoping reviews. BMC Med Res Methodol. 2016;16(1):15. doi: 10.1186/s12874-016-0116-4. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Peters MDJ, Godfrey CM, Khalil H, McInerney P, Parker D, Soares CB. Guidance for conducting systematic scoping reviews. Int J Evid Based Healthc. 2015;13(3):141–146. doi: 10.1097/XEB.0000000000000050. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Peters MDJ, Marnie C, Tricco AC, Pollock D, Munn Z, Alexander L, McInerney P, Godfrey CM, Khalil H. Updated methodological guidance for the conduct of scoping reviews. JBI Evid Synth. 2020;18(10):2119–2126. doi: 10.11124/JBIES-20-00167. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Feldman R, Stanton M, Suelzer EM. Compiling evidence for EVALI: a scoping review of in vivo pulmonary effects after inhaling vitamin E or vitamin E acetate. J Med Toxicol. 2021;17(3):278–288. doi: 10.1007/s13181-021-00823-w. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Kaczor EE, Greene K, Zacharia J, Tormoehlen L, Neavyn M, Carreiro S. The potential proconvulsant effects of cannabis: a scoping review. J Med Toxicol. 2022;18(3):223–34. 10.1007/s13181-022-00886-3 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ]',16,'2025-10-15 10:21:42','2025-10-15 10:21:42');
